  "https://www.instagram.com/p/DY7bJr5DJfR/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "🎬 A viral Claude prompt that replaces assistant-mode hedging with operator-mode decisions — concrete, blunt, trade-off-aware, starting every answer with &#x27;Here&#x27;s what I&#x27;d actually do.&#x27;\n🏷 Recommendations · Mindset\n📄 <i>Someone just shared the most useful Claude prompt I&#x27;ve seen this year. It&#x27;s called Founder Thinking Mode. And once you activate it — you&#x27;ll never have a generic AI conversation again. Every time you open Claude for a business decision, you activate a mode that forces it to think like a seasoned operator — not a helpful assistant. No hedging. No &#x27;it depends.&#x27; No generic frameworks. Just: &#x27;Here&#x27;s what I&#x27;d actually do.&#x27; Save this. Use it today.</i>\n\n🛠 <b>TRY</b>\n• <a href=\"https://x.com/FutureStacked/status/2062470044424994926\">Founder Thinking Mode — Claude persona prompt</a>\n   ↳ Paste this at the start of every Gameplan decision session: &#x27;You are now in Founder Thinking Mode. Think like a first-principles operator who has built and exited companies. When I give you a problem — don&#x27;t give generic advice. Give me the exact decision a seasoned founder would make. Include trade-offs, risks, and what most people miss. Start every response with: Here&#x27;s what I&#x27;d actually do.&#x27; You already have &#x27;How to Actually Use Claude&#x27; in your vault — this is the missing activation layer for business decisions.\n• Stress-test variant: pitch Gameplan to a skeptical Series A investor\n   ↳ Prompt: &#x27;Enter Founder Thinking Mode. I&#x27;m building: [Gameplan — 2 sentences]. Stress test my model like a Series A investor who&#x27;s seen 500 pitches. What breaks first? What am I not seeing? What do the top 3% of founders in this space do differently? Be blunt.&#x27; Run this before your next investor update — it will surface holes faster than any advisor.\n\n💡 <b>ADVICE</b>\n• Never open Claude for a business decision without setting an operator persona first — generic prompt gets generic answer\n   ↳ Direct upgrade to your daily Claude Code workflow: one-line system prompt at session start replaces all the hedging you&#x27;ve been tolerating. Costs 10 seconds, changes the quality of every output for that session.\n\n📝 Action Inbox/2026-06-13 Founder Thinking Mode Claude prompt.md · 🗂 Notion: 1 reel · 3 items",
    "markdown": "# Founder Thinking Mode Claude prompt\n\n*🏷 Recommendations · Mindset*\n\nA viral Claude prompt that replaces assistant-mode hedging with operator-mode decisions — concrete, blunt, trade-off-aware, starting every answer with 'Here's what I'd actually do.'\n\n> Someone just shared the most useful Claude prompt I've seen this year. It's called Founder Thinking Mode. And once you activate it — you'll never have a generic AI conversation again. Every time you open Claude for a business decision, you activate a mode that forces it to think like a seasoned operator — not a helpful assistant. No hedging. No 'it depends.' No generic frameworks. Just: 'Here's what I'd actually do.' Save this. Use it today.\n\n---\n\n### 🛠 Try\n- [Founder Thinking Mode — Claude persona prompt](https://x.com/FutureStacked/status/2062470044424994926) — Paste this at the start of every Gameplan decision session: 'You are now in Founder Thinking Mode. Think like a first-principles operator who has built and exited companies. When I give you a problem — don't give generic advice. Give me the exact decision a seasoned founder would make. Include trade-offs, risks, and what most people miss. Start every response with: Here's what I'd actually do.' You already have 'How to Actually Use Claude' in your vault — this is the missing activation layer for business decisions.\n- Stress-test variant: pitch Gameplan to a skeptical Series A investor — Prompt: 'Enter Founder Thinking Mode. I'm building: [Gameplan — 2 sentences]. Stress test my model like a Series A investor who's seen 500 pitches. What breaks first? What am I not seeing? What do the top 3% of founders in this space do differently? Be blunt.' Run this before your next investor update — it will surface holes faster than any advisor.\n\n### 💡 Advice\n- Never open Claude for a business decision without setting an operator persona first — generic prompt gets generic answer — Direct upgrade to your daily Claude Code workflow: one-line system prompt at session start replaces all the hedging you've been tolerating. Costs 10 seconds, changes the quality of every output for that session.\n\n---\n\n*📝 Action Inbox/2026-06-13 Founder Thinking Mode Claude prompt.md · 🗂 Notion: 1 reel · 3 items*",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-13T16:20:08"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYhv57Sv_cw/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "🎬 An AI ethics professor lists 5 books she teaches in her university course covering surveillance capitalism, algorithmic bias, and the political economy of AI.\n🏷 Recommendations\n📄 <i>Save this post to come back to! As a professor in AI Ethics, these are the 5 books I am teaching during my course, and they give you an excellent overview of the field of AI and its impact on humanity.</i>\n\n📚 <b>READ</b>\n• <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/en/book/show/26195941-the-age-of-surveillance-capitalism\">The Age of Surveillance Capitalism</a>\n   ↳ Maps directly to his athlete platform and IG content work — foundational for understanding how algorithmic tracking monetizes user behavior on the platforms he builds for and publishes on.\n• <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/222725518-empire-of-ai\">Empire of AI: Dreams and Nightmares in Sam Altman&#x27;s OpenAI</a>\n   ↳ He uses Claude Code daily — understanding the power dynamics and culture behind the AI tools he depends on is strategically useful. National Book Critics Circle winner 2025. 📅 496 pages, block dedicated time.\n• <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/en/book/show/50131136-atlas-of-ai\">Atlas of AI: Power, Politics, and the Planetary Costs of Artificial Intelligence</a>\n   ↳ Foundational map of AI&#x27;s political and economic infrastructure — essential context for a founder building AI-powered content workflows.\n• <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/34762552-algorithms-of-oppression\">Algorithms of Oppression: How Search Engines Reinforce Racism</a>\n   ↳ Understanding search and recommendation algorithm bias directly informs content discoverability strategy on his athlete platform and social channels.\n• <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/16284965-24-7\">24/7: Late Capitalism and the Ends of Sleep</a>\n   ↳ Lower tactical relevance but high personal relevance — the always-on content creator grind is exactly the dynamic this book critiques. Short read at 144 pages.\n\n📝 Action Inbox/2026-06-13 5 AI ethics books.md · 🗂 Notion: 1 reel · 5 items",
    "markdown": "# 5 AI ethics books to read\n\n*🏷 Recommendations*\n\nAn AI ethics professor lists 5 books she teaches in her university course covering surveillance capitalism, algorithmic bias, and the political economy of AI.\n\n> Save this post to come back to! As a professor in AI Ethics, these are the 5 books I am teaching during my course, and they give you an excellent overview of the field of AI and its impact on humanity.\n\n---\n\n### 📚 Read\n- [The Age of Surveillance Capitalism](https://www.goodreads.com/en/book/show/26195941-the-age-of-surveillance-capitalism) — Maps directly to his athlete platform and IG content work — foundational for understanding how algorithmic tracking monetizes user behavior on the platforms he builds for and publishes on.\n- [Empire of AI: Dreams and Nightmares in Sam Altman's OpenAI](https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/222725518-empire-of-ai) — He uses Claude Code daily — understanding the power dynamics and culture behind the AI tools he depends on is strategically useful. National Book Critics Circle winner 2025. 📅 496 pages, block dedicated time.\n- [Atlas of AI: Power, Politics, and the Planetary Costs of Artificial Intelligence](https://www.goodreads.com/en/book/show/50131136-atlas-of-ai) — Foundational map of AI's political and economic infrastructure — essential context for a founder building AI-powered content workflows.\n- [Algorithms of Oppression: How Search Engines Reinforce Racism](https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/34762552-algorithms-of-oppression) — Understanding search and recommendation algorithm bias directly informs content discoverability strategy on his athlete platform and social channels.\n- [24/7: Late Capitalism and the Ends of Sleep](https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/16284965-24-7) — Lower tactical relevance but high personal relevance — the always-on content creator grind is exactly the dynamic this book critiques. Short read at 144 pages.\n\n---\n\n*📝 Action Inbox/2026-06-13 5 AI ethics books.md · 🗂 Notion: 1 reel · 5 items*",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-13T18:23:06"
