  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DW7DFWIjW34/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "🎬 25 rapid-fire tips for talking to camera: volume beats perfection, be calculated in message but casual in delivery, and use DMs/time-travel prompts as idea engines.\n🏷 Mindset · Recommendations\n📄 <i>Five more tips for yapping on camera: 1. don&#x27;t just study big creators, look for people with less than 10K and study their outlier videos 2. create a folder of your favorite yap videos and study their first three seconds and text hooks 3. ask whether your video passes the &quot;I believe&quot; test 4. change the language in videos you watch so you can focus on tonality, delivery, and cadence 5. Disregard advice that doesn&#x27;t make sense for you. The goal is volume so you can see the data and get patterns.</i>\n\n💡 <b>ADVICE</b>\n• Start mid-thought — cut any setup and drop the viewer straight into the point; no one needs the intro.\n   ↳ Your Gameplan reels tend to over-explain context before the hook; this one structural cut would lift retention on every video you shoot.\n• Run the &#x27;so what?&#x27; test on every line of your script — if the answer is &#x27;obviously,&#x27; you don&#x27;t have a point yet.\n   ↳ Quick quality filter before recording that catches weak takes early, saving reshoots and editing time.\n• Volume over perfection: more reps give you data, data reveals your personal patterns — optimize from data, not theory.\n   ↳ Directly validates the high-output content strategy in context-about-me.md; removes the mental permission-seeking that delays posting.\n• Raw dog write daily — even a paragraph. Sharper thinking leads directly to sharper on-camera points.\n   ↳ Low-cost daily habit that compounds into better takes for Gameplan athlete platform content and personal brand reels alike.\n• Mine texts and DMs for content ideas — language you already used in real conversation is pre-validated copy.\n   ↳ You&#x27;re already talking to athletes and founders daily; those convos are a standing content queue you&#x27;re not tapping.\n• Write takes with tension — a viewer should be able to say &#x27;nah, I disagree.&#x27; Inarguable takes are forgettable.\n   ↳ The Gameplan and athlete content tends toward safe framing; adding a polarizing edge is the fastest lever to increase shares.\n• After your best take, immediately record a safety take — lower pressure usually makes it the real best take.\n   ↳ One extra 60-second habit per session that routinely upgrades final output with zero extra planning cost.\n• Time-travel prompt: &#x27;What 3 things would I tell myself 5 years ago?&#x27; — use as an emergency idea generator when blank.\n   ↳ Founder story content is your highest-trust angle for the athlete platform; this prompt surfaces authentic personal takes on demand.\n• Study creators under 10K and their outlier videos — that&#x27;s where you see what breaks through before the algorithm amplifies it.\n   ↳ Studying only top creators means studying already-amplified content; outliers from small accounts reveal the real creative signal.\n• Watch study videos in a foreign language (or muted) to isolate tonality, delivery, and cadence from the words.\n   ↳ Your RU/EN bilingual content spans two audiences with different cadence expectations; this technique helps calibrate delivery for each.\n• After recording: cut the hook and see if the body still holds. Then cut the last line and see if it lands harder. Both edits usually improve the video.\n   ↳ Turns editing from guesswork into a two-step structural test; easy to build into your post-record checklist.\n\n📝 Action Inbox/2026-06-13 Yapping on Camera Tips.md · 🗂 Notion: 1 reel · 11 items",
    "markdown": "# 25 Tips for Yapping on Camera\n\n*🏷 Mindset · Recommendations*\n\n25 rapid-fire tips for talking to camera: volume beats perfection, be calculated in message but casual in delivery, and use DMs/time-travel prompts as idea engines.\n\n> Five more tips for yapping on camera: 1. don't just study big creators, look for people with less than 10K and study their outlier videos 2. create a folder of your favorite yap videos and study their first three seconds and text hooks 3. ask whether your video passes the \"I believe\" test 4. change the language in videos you watch so you can focus on tonality, delivery, and cadence 5. Disregard advice that doesn't make sense for you. The goal is volume so you can see the data and get patterns.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 Advice\n- Start mid-thought — cut any setup and drop the viewer straight into the point; no one needs the intro. — Your Gameplan reels tend to over-explain context before the hook; this one structural cut would lift retention on every video you shoot.\n- Run the 'so what?' test on every line of your script — if the answer is 'obviously,' you don't have a point yet. — Quick quality filter before recording that catches weak takes early, saving reshoots and editing time.\n- Volume over perfection: more reps give you data, data reveals your personal patterns — optimize from data, not theory. — Directly validates the high-output content strategy in context-about-me.md; removes the mental permission-seeking that delays posting.\n- Raw dog write daily — even a paragraph. Sharper thinking leads directly to sharper on-camera points. — Low-cost daily habit that compounds into better takes for Gameplan athlete platform content and personal brand reels alike.\n- Mine texts and DMs for content ideas — language you already used in real conversation is pre-validated copy. — You're already talking to athletes and founders daily; those convos are a standing content queue you're not tapping.\n- Write takes with tension — a viewer should be able to say 'nah, I disagree.' Inarguable takes are forgettable. — The Gameplan and athlete content tends toward safe framing; adding a polarizing edge is the fastest lever to increase shares.\n- After your best take, immediately record a safety take — lower pressure usually makes it the real best take. — One extra 60-second habit per session that routinely upgrades final output with zero extra planning cost.\n- Time-travel prompt: 'What 3 things would I tell myself 5 years ago?' — use as an emergency idea generator when blank. — Founder story content is your highest-trust angle for the athlete platform; this prompt surfaces authentic personal takes on demand.\n- Study creators under 10K and their outlier videos — that's where you see what breaks through before the algorithm amplifies it. — Studying only top creators means studying already-amplified content; outliers from small accounts reveal the real creative signal.\n- Watch study videos in a foreign language (or muted) to isolate tonality, delivery, and cadence from the words. — Your RU/EN bilingual content spans two audiences with different cadence expectations; this technique helps calibrate delivery for each.\n- After recording: cut the hook and see if the body still holds. Then cut the last line and see if it lands harder. Both edits usually improve the video. — Turns editing from guesswork into a two-step structural test; easy to build into your post-record checklist.\n\n---\n\n*📝 Action Inbox/2026-06-13 Yapping on Camera Tips.md · 🗂 Notion: 1 reel · 11 items*",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-13T20:46:38"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DTg0_tkkXur/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>🎬 Script Videos to Maximize Retention</b>\n🏷 <i>Recommendations · Mindset</i>\nShort-form tutorial by jun_yuh demonstrating a 6-step video scripting framework for retention, using the reel itself as a live example of the method.\n<blockquote>The creator argues that hooks alone are not enough — the algorithm rewards overall retention. They outline a 6-step scripting structure: (1) hook with a curiosity gap, (2) agitate the pain, (3) rehook with a question or scene switch, (4) establish context, (5) build toward the solution, (6) end at peak engagement to prompt a rewatch. The video itself is scripted using this exact framework as a live demonstration.</blockquote>\n<i>No actionable items in this one.</i>\n<i>📝 Action Inbox/2026-06-13 Script Videos to Maximize Retention.md · 🗂 Notion: 1 reel · 0 items</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>🎬 Script Videos to Maximize Retention</b>\n🏷 <i>Recommendations · Mindset</i>\nShort-form tutorial by jun_yuh demonstrating a 6-step video scripting framework for retention, using the reel itself as a live example of the method.\n<blockquote>The creator argues that hooks alone are not enough — the algorithm rewards overall retention. They outline a 6-step scripting structure: (1) hook with a curiosity gap, (2) agitate the pain, (3) rehook with a question or scene switch, (4) establish context, (5) build toward the solution, (6) end at peak engagement to prompt a rewatch. The video itself is scripted using this exact framework as a live demonstration.</blockquote>\n<i>No actionable items in this one.</i>\n<i>📝 Action Inbox/2026-06-13 Script Videos to Maximize Retention.md · 🗂 Notion: 1 reel · 0 items</i>",
