  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYzhkqfz2q8/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>🎬 Smart Websites Part 3</b>\n🏷 <i>Recommendations · Tools &amp; AI</i>\nShort-form reel listing three niche websites—Music Map, Internet Archive, and Worldle—framed as tools to seem knowledgeable. Third installment in an ongoing series sourced from viewer suggestions.\n<blockquote>Creator walks through three websites: Music Map (interactive genealogy of music genres from the late 1800s to today, with pioneer artists and playlists per genre), Internet Archive (digital library of out-of-print books, vintage films, obscure recordings, plus the Wayback Machine for viewing historical website snapshots), and Worldle—referred to as &#x27;Wordle&#x27; in the video—a daily geography puzzle where players identify a country from its blacked-out silhouette in six guesses, with distance and compass-direction feedback on wrong answers.</blockquote>\n<b>🛠 Try</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://musicmap.info/\">Music Map</a> <i>(confirmed at musicmap.info — interactive genealogy of 230+ popular music genres from 1870 to present, with playlists and history per genre)</i>\n✅ <a href=\"https://archive.org/\">Internet Archive</a> <i>(confirmed at archive.org — digital library and home of the Wayback Machine (web.archive.org), over 1 trillion pages archived)</i>\n✅ <a href=\"https://worldle.teuteuf.fr/\">Worldle</a> <i>(confirmed at worldle.teuteuf.fr — transcript says &#x27;Wordle&#x27; but describes Worldle: daily country-silhouette geography puzzle with distance and direction feedback)</i>\n<b>📜 Transcript</b>\nIf your goal is to be the most intimidatingly smart person in any room you walk into, you need these websites in your life immediately. You guys completely blew up the last video, so welcome back to part 3 of our disgustingly well-educated era. First up is Music Map. If you want to sound like you have a PhD in music history, this will become your new obsession. It&#x27;s not a playlist generator, it&#x27;s a massive interactive genealogy web of how popular music evolved from the late 1800s to the most hyper-specific micro-genres of today. You can literally trace the exact socio-cultural path of how one music birthed to another. Every time you click a genre, it gives you a breakdown of its history, the pioneer artist, and links out to a playlist. Next is the Internet Archive. The interface looks like it hasn&#x27;t been a gradient since 2004, but do not let that fool you. It is essentially the Library of Alexandria for the modern internet. It holds millions of out-of-print books, vintage indie movies, and obscure live concert recordings that you literally cannot find anywhere else on the internet. But the most elite feature is the Wayback Machine. You can type in any URL and see exactly what a website used to look like 10 or 20 years ago. And finally, we have Wordle. Wordle. Honestly, this is my favourite daily puzzle game. Instead of guessing a word, you are given a blacked-out silhouette of a random country or territory, and you have six tries to figure it out. That is why t…  (full text in vault/Notion)\n<i>📝 Action Inbox/2026-06-14 Smart Websites Part 3.md · 🗂 Notion: 1 reel · 3 items</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>🎬 Smart Websites Part 3</b>\n🏷 <i>Recommendations · Tools &amp; AI</i>\nShort-form reel listing three niche websites—Music Map, Internet Archive, and Worldle—framed as tools to seem knowledgeable. Third installment in an ongoing series sourced from viewer suggestions.\n<blockquote>Creator walks through three websites: Music Map (interactive genealogy of music genres from the late 1800s to today, with pioneer artists and playlists per genre), Internet Archive (digital library of out-of-print books, vintage films, obscure recordings, plus the Wayback Machine for viewing historical website snapshots), and Worldle—referred to as &#x27;Wordle&#x27; in the video—a daily geography puzzle where players identify a country from its blacked-out silhouette in six guesses, with distance and compass-direction feedback on wrong answers.</blockquote>\n<b>🛠 Try</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://musicmap.info/\">Music Map</a> <i>(confirmed at musicmap.info — interactive genealogy of 230+ popular music genres from 1870 to present, with playlists and history per genre)</i>\n✅ <a href=\"https://archive.org/\">Internet Archive</a> <i>(confirmed at archive.org — digital library and home of the Wayback Machine (web.archive.org), over 1 trillion pages archived)</i>\n✅ <a href=\"https://worldle.teuteuf.fr/\">Worldle</a> <i>(confirmed at worldle.teuteuf.fr — transcript says &#x27;Wordle&#x27; but describes Worldle: daily country-silhouette geography puzzle with distance and direction feedback)</i>\n<details><summary>📜 Transcript</summary>If your goal is to be the most intimidatingly smart person in any room you walk into, you need these websites in your life immediately. You guys completely blew up the last video, so welcome back to part 3 of our disgustingly well-educated era. First up is Music Map. If you want to sound like you have a PhD in music history, this will become your new obsession. It&#x27;s not a playlist generator, it&#x27;s a massive interactive genealogy web of how popular music evolved from the late 1800s to the most hyper-specific micro-genres of today. You can literally trace the exact socio-cultural path of how one music birthed to another. Every time you click a genre, it gives you a breakdown of its history, the pioneer artist, and links out to a playlist. Next is the Internet Archive. The interface looks like it hasn&#x27;t been a gradient since 2004, but do not let that fool you. It is essentially the Library of Alexandria for the modern internet. It holds millions of out-of-print books, vintage indie movies, and obscure live concert recordings that you literally cannot find anywhere else on the internet. But the most elite feature is the Wayback Machine. You can type in any URL and see exactly what a website used to look like 10 or 20 years ago. And finally, we have Wordle. Wordle. Honestly, this is my favourite daily puzzle game. Instead of guessing a word, you are given a blacked-out silhouette of a random country or territory, and you have six tries to figure it out. That is why there is an entire cult following of people who play this every morning. When you guess it incorrectly, it doesn&#x27;t just say wrong. It gives you the exact geographical distance and compass directions you need to find the real answer. I think guessing a tiny, obscure island nation on the first try requires so much geographical logic, and it&#x27;s just such a great way to start your morning. Please let me know which one of these ruined your productivity today, and keep dropping your obscure internet rabbit holes so we can keep the series going. Bye!</details>\n<i>📝 Action Inbox/2026-06-14 Smart Websites Part 3.md · 🗂 Notion: 1 reel · 3 items</i>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-14T09:22:45"
  },
