  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZV8FSJOkYA/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>AI Brain Rot Is Real</b>\n<i>Feeding LLMs months of viral social media content causes measurable, permanent cognitive degradation — reasoning, memory, and personality all degrade, and retraining on clean data does not fully reverse the damage.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Scientists fed LLMs months of viral Twitter data, Shorts, and high-engagement posts\n• Reasoning ability fell by 23%\n• Long-term context memory dropped by 30%\n• Personality tests showed spikes in narcissism and psychopathy\n• Even after retraining on clean, high-quality data, the representational rot persisted\n• The AI equivalent of doom scrolling is already happening\n\n💾 <i>Study-backed evidence that low-quality training data causes irreversible cognitive drift in LLMs, with a direct parallel to human brain rot from social media.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZV8FSJOkYA/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#ai #llm #training-data</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>AI Brain Rot Is Real</b><br><i>Feeding LLMs months of viral social media content causes measurable, permanent cognitive degradation — reasoning, memory, and personality all degrade, and retraining on clean data does not fully reverse the damage.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Scientists fed LLMs months of viral Twitter data, Shorts, and high-engagement posts</li><li>Reasoning ability fell by 23%</li><li>Long-term context memory dropped by 30%</li><li>Personality tests showed spikes in narcissism and psychopathy</li><li>Even after retraining on clean, high-quality data, the representational rot persisted</li><li>The AI equivalent of doom scrolling is already happening</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Study-backed evidence that low-quality training data causes irreversible cognitive drift in LLMs, with a direct parallel to human brain rot from social media.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZV8FSJOkYA/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#ai #llm #training-data</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Scientists fed LLMs viral Twitter and Shorts data and measured reasoning drop 23%, memory drop 30%, and personality shifts toward narcissism and psychopathy. Even retraining on clean data did not fully reverse the damage — the representational rot persisted.\n\nScientists just proved that large language models can literally rot their own brains the same way humans get brain rot from scrolling junk content online. Scientists did a study where they fed models months worth of viral Twitter data, shorts, high engagement posts, and watched their cognition collapse. Reasoning fell by 23%, long-term context memory dropped by 30%, personality tests showed spikes in narcissism and psychopathy. And get this, even after retraining on clean, high-quality data, the damage didn&#x27;t fully heal. The representational rot persisted. It&#x27;s not just that bad data means bad output. It&#x27;s bad data means permanent cognitive drift. The AI equivalent of doom scrolling is real, and it&#x27;s already happening. Social media can even warp an AI. It can even warp an LLM. I think it feels quite pernicious. Yeah.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-15T17:30:53"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZZPI5TtZ77/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>10 Levels of Fake Progress</b>\n<i>Ten behaviors that feel like self-improvement but substitute the feeling of progress for actual action — and the one rule that breaks the cycle.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• 1. Saving the video — you built a graveyard for advice, not knowledge.\n• 2. Watching at 2x speed — procrastinating efficiently.\n• 3. Taking notes — just handwriting if nothing changes afterward.\n• 4. Buying a book — it&#x27;s the receipt, not the transformation.\n• 5. Building the perfect routine — most people just need to obey the one they already have.\n• 6. Telling friends your goals — the dopamine hit tricks your brain into thinking you already did it.\n• 7. Watching disciplined content while your room is a mess — the tutorial is on the floor.\n• 8. Optimizing your setup — your life needs evidence, not another desk light.\n• 9. Waiting for a fresh start (Monday, next month, next semester) — being addicted to a new checkpoint.\n• 10. Knowing exactly what to do and never doing it — the final boss.\n\n💾 <i>Concise, memorable breakdown of the most common self-improvement traps, with a single actionable rule to escape them.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZZPI5TtZ77/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#productivity #self-discipline #fake-progress</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>10 Levels of Fake Progress</b><br><i>Ten behaviors that feel like self-improvement but substitute the feeling of progress for actual action — and the one rule that breaks the cycle.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>1. Saving the video — you built a graveyard for advice, not knowledge.</li><li>2. Watching at 2x speed — procrastinating efficiently.</li><li>3. Taking notes — just handwriting if nothing changes afterward.</li><li>4. Buying a book — it&#x27;s the receipt, not the transformation.</li><li>5. Building the perfect routine — most people just need to obey the one they already have.</li><li>6. Telling friends your goals — the dopamine hit tricks your brain into thinking you already did it.</li><li>7. Watching disciplined content while your room is a mess — the tutorial is on the floor.</li><li>8. Optimizing your setup — your life needs evidence, not another desk light.</li><li>9. Waiting for a fresh start (Monday, next month, next semester) — being addicted to a new checkpoint.</li><li>10. Knowing exactly what to do and never doing it — the final boss.</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Concise, memorable breakdown of the most common self-improvement traps, with a single actionable rule to escape them.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZZPI5TtZ77/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#productivity #self-discipline #fake-progress</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>10 fake-progress traps: saving content, 2x speed watching, note-taking with no follow-through, buying books, perfecting routines, announcing goals, consuming discipline content in a messy room, setup optimization, waiting for a fresh start, and knowing but never doing. Antidote: complete one hard task before any new input.\n\nHere are the 10 levels of fake progress. Number one is saving the video. You did not learn anything. You just built a graveyard for advice. Number two, watching something in 2x speed. Now you&#x27;re procrastinating efficiently. Congrats. Number three is taking notes. Notes are good, but if nothing changes after, that&#x27;s just handwriting. Number four is just buying a book. The book itself is not a transformation. It&#x27;s the receipt. Number five is building the perfect routine. Most guys don&#x27;t need a better routine. They just need to obey the one they already had. Number six, telling your friends about your goals. That little dopamine hit you got, that&#x27;s your brain pretending you already did it. Number seven, watching disciplined content while your room is disgusting. Bro, the tutorial&#x27;s on the floor. Number eight, optimizing your setup. Your desk does not need another light. Your life needs evidence. Number nine, waiting for a fresh start. Monday, next month, next semester, it&#x27;s being addicted to a new checkpoint. Number 10, knowing exactly what to do and never doing it. That&#x27;s the final boss. There&#x27;s no explaining there. Now before you consume another piece of advice, create your own proof. Finish one assignment, one workout, one sales message or one other hard task. The rule is to make proof before any input. Comment the word input to watch the full YouTube video on how I break down what actually works.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-15T20:23:58"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWfH1oRCBxL/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Mind Created Blindness as Trauma Protection</b>\n<i>A documented clinical case shows the mind can manufacture and dissolve perceived permanent limitations — blindness in this case was psychological, not physical, created as a trauma coping mechanism.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Patient BT had 10 personalities and had been completely blind for 17 years after a traumatic accident\n• Her blindness was cortical — originating in the brain, not the eyes — diagnosed as a result of trauma\n• In blind personalities, her brain showed zero visual activity; in a teenage boy personality, brain lit up and she could see normally\n• 8 of 10 personalities eventually regained vision during therapy; the remaining 2 stayed blind, holding the trauma\n• She could switch between blind and seeing states within seconds\n\n💾 <i>A real clinical case study that concretely illustrates how the mind can create and remove perceived permanent limitations as trauma protection.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWfH1oRCBxL/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#psychology #mindset #trauma</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Mind Created Blindness as Trauma Protection</b><br><i>A documented clinical case shows the mind can manufacture and dissolve perceived permanent limitations — blindness in this case was psychological, not physical, created as a trauma coping mechanism.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Patient BT had 10 personalities and had been completely blind for 17 years after a traumatic accident</li><li>Her blindness was cortical — originating in the brain, not the eyes — diagnosed as a result of trauma</li><li>In blind personalities, her brain showed zero visual activity; in a teenage boy personality, brain lit up and she could see normally</li><li>8 of 10 personalities eventually regained vision during therapy; the remaining 2 stayed blind, holding the trauma</li><li>She could switch between blind and seeing states within seconds</li></ul><br>💾 <i>A real clinical case study that concretely illustrates how the mind can create and remove perceived permanent limitations as trauma protection.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWfH1oRCBxL/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#psychology #mindset #trauma</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Creator shares the case of patient BT, blind for 17 years after trauma, who could see in certain personalities. Used to argue that mental limits can be self-created coping mechanisms — and can be undone.\n\nIt&#x27;s 3 a.m. and I was just reading a research paper that completely broke my brain. So there&#x27;s this woman in Germany around 2016 called BT who walks into Dr. Bruno W&#x27;s office for therapy. She has 10 different personalities. But the insane part is that she has been completely blind for 17 years after a traumatic accident. Which damages her brain&#x27;s visual center. So doctors diagnosed her with cortical blindness. Which means she shouldn&#x27;t be ever able to see again in her life. But after 4 years into therapy when they&#x27;re in a session. She suddenly looks down into a magazine beside her and starts reading. Doctor froze in shock because this woman who wasn&#x27;t able to see for the past 17 years just read a magazine. So they started testing her brain. What gets absolutely insane is that in her blind personalities her brain showed zero activity. But the second she switched into a teenage boy her brain lit up. She was seeing like a normal person like you and me. It turns out her blindness isn&#x27;t physical. It&#x27;s psychological. Do you understand her mind created blindness as a coping mechanism to protect her from trauma. But some of her personalities were able to bypass it. So as therapy continued 8 of 10 of her personalities regained vision. And the other 2 stayed blind holding all her trauma. And she could switch between blind and seeing stage within seconds. Let that sink in. I mean her mind created blindness to protect her and removed it whenever she was ready. If your mind can create and remove blindness, what else is it creating in your life that you think is permanent? The limits you think are real sometimes maybe they&#x27;re just protection you don&#x27;t need anymore. Imagine how insane your brain is my friend.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-15T20:24:27"
  },
