  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZBpVj3OZVq/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Auto-Organize Screenshots with iPhone Shortcuts</b>\n<i>“Your iPhone already knows what&#x27;s in your screenshots. It might as well do something about that.”</i>\n\n🪜 <b>How to</b>\n1. Create a Shortcut that finds every screenshot taken today\n2. Use Repeat with Each to loop through them one at a time\n3. For each screenshot, use Apple Intelligence to pick the best album from your list (e.g. travel, tasks, shopping, other)\n4. Save the screenshot to the chosen album and move to the next\n5. Go to Automation and schedule the Shortcut to run every night\n\n<i>Useful for: Anyone who accumulates screenshots and wants them automatically sorted into named albums each night without manual effort.</i>\n\n💾 <i>Practical iPhone automation that eliminates manual screenshot sorting using built-in tools already on the device.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZBpVj3OZVq/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#iphone #automation #shortcuts</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Auto-Organize Screenshots with iPhone Shortcuts</b><br><i>“Your iPhone already knows what&#x27;s in your screenshots. It might as well do something about that.”</i><br>🪜 <b>How to</b><br><ol><li>Create a Shortcut that finds every screenshot taken today</li><li>Use Repeat with Each to loop through them one at a time</li><li>For each screenshot, use Apple Intelligence to pick the best album from your list (e.g. travel, tasks, shopping, other)</li><li>Save the screenshot to the chosen album and move to the next</li><li>Go to Automation and schedule the Shortcut to run every night</li></ol><br><i>Useful for: Anyone who accumulates screenshots and wants them automatically sorted into named albums each night without manual effort.</i><br>💾 <i>Practical iPhone automation that eliminates manual screenshot sorting using built-in tools already on the device.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZBpVj3OZVq/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#iphone #automation #shortcuts</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>The creator builds a Shortcut that finds all screenshots taken that day, loops through them one at a time, and asks Apple Intelligence to assign each to the best album from a custom list. An automation runs it every night, keeping the camera roll clean without any manual effort.\n\nYou&#x27;re taking screenshots like your iPhone&#x27;s going to do something with them. And right now, it&#x27;s just dropping everything into the camera roll. That&#x27;s why I taught my phone how to organize my screenshots into albums for me. Every night, it looks through any new screenshots I took that day and then puts them where they belong. So my camera roll stays organized, and when I need something later, I can actually find it. Here&#x27;s how it works. First, the shortcut finds every screenshot that I took today. Then it uses repeat with each to loop through them one at a time. For each screenshot, Apple Intelligence looks at the image and decides where it belongs. Then it gets one job. Look at this screenshot. Pick the best album from the list and reply only with the name. Mine can choose between travel, tasks, shopping, or other. Once AI picks an album, the shortcut saves the screenshot there and grabs the next one. Then go to automation and schedule the shortcut to run every night so your phone stays organized whenever you add new screenshots. Now your ideas, tasks, and reminders get sorted into albums that actually make sense. Your iPhone already knows what&#x27;s in your screenshots. It might as well do something about that.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-29T00:05:48"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWeuAmHjXES/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Original Thoughts Are a Muscle</b>\n<i>Independent thinking is a learnable muscle, not an innate trait — practice it by sitting with your raw reactions before the internet answers for you.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• People with interesting original ideas simply sit with ideas long enough to develop something to add — they are not born that way.\n• The internet&#x27;s lack of friction (read → share instantly, no processing) is what makes everyone converge on the same opinions.\n• Capture your raw reaction to a movie, book, or idea before you check reviews, YouTube, or the comment section.\n• Half-formed thoughts are still thoughts in progress — don&#x27;t suppress or discard them.\n• When you disagree with something, first state that you disagree, then figure out why — the figuring-out is where independent thinking is built.\n\n💾 <i>Practical framework for reclaiming independent thought in an era of frictionless, instant opinion-sharing.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWeuAmHjXES/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#independent-thinking #mindset #content-consumption</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Original Thoughts Are a Muscle</b><br><i>Independent thinking is a learnable muscle, not an innate trait — practice it by sitting with your raw reactions before the internet answers for you.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>People with interesting original ideas simply sit with ideas long enough to develop something to add — they are not born that way.</li><li>The internet&#x27;s lack of friction (read → share instantly, no processing) is what makes everyone converge on the same opinions.</li><li>Capture your raw reaction to a movie, book, or idea before you check reviews, YouTube, or the comment section.</li><li>Half-formed thoughts are still thoughts in progress — don&#x27;t suppress or discard them.</li><li>When you disagree with something, first state that you disagree, then figure out why — the figuring-out is where independent thinking is built.</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Practical framework for reclaiming independent thought in an era of frictionless, instant opinion-sharing.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWeuAmHjXES/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#independent-thinking #mindset #content-consumption</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Internet culture removes the friction needed to form original opinions — we read, see, and share before we&#x27;ve processed anything. The Feifei Substack piece argues independent thinking is a muscle: sit with ideas before seeking others&#x27; views, capture your raw reaction before the comment section gets to it, don&#x27;t discard half-formed thoughts, and when you disagree, start there and work out the why.\n\nI don&#x27;t know how many of you can relate to this, but I realized lately that I no longer have any original thoughts, which means that all of my thoughts and feelings and opinions have actually been borrowed by what I see on the internet and it is no longer my own. Now I thought this was completely normal until I read this article on Substack called How to Start Having Original Thoughts and it made me think about how fucked we are as a generation that we do not have our own opinions anymore. Anyway, that article was so insightful that I wanted to share a few things that I learned from it. So the first thing that the writer says is that having your own opinion and being an independent thinker is not a trait. It is a muscle that you practice, which means that people who have interesting ideas and thoughts are not born that way. They just sit with ideas long enough to start having interesting things to add to it. She goes on to say that the reason we have no original thoughts is because our environment is designed in a way where we read something, see something and we can share it immediately without first understanding or doing something with that information. This lack of friction is what causes us all to have the same opinion. And she suggested that after you watch a movie or read a book, before you find out the reviews or run to the comment section, you need to sit with it, sit with your thoughts and understand what you think about it. She says that you need to capture your raw reaction before the internet gets to it. This is so true because nowadays when I watch a movie and I don&#x27;t know what to think about it, I literally go to YouTube and I read the comment section and I choose one which resonates the most with me instead of creating my own feedback. The second thing that she said was that you should not be embarrassed of your half-formed thoughts. Half-formed thoughts are still thoughts in progress. And to start with the why, which means that if you don&#x27;t agree with something, start by saying that you don&#x27;t agree with it and then figure out the why. It is in this figuring out that you become an independent thinker. And my favorite part of the article is the outro where she says that you&#x27;re not trying to be someone else. You&#x27;re just trying to find out who you are underneath all this content consumption and borrowed opinions. And that we need to sit quietly long enough to notice our thoughts come up before we let the internet answer them for us. So interesting.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-29T00:05:01"
  },
