  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYSRW1YCar0/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Why We Can&#x27;t Copy Top Education Systems</b>\n<i>High-performing education systems cannot be transplanted across cultures because the beliefs about effort and innate ability that underpin their success cannot be copied.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Western cultures tend to view academic ability as innate — you either are or are not a maths person\n• Many Asian cultures genuinely believe success comes purely from effort and persistence, not fixed talent\n• The UK&#x27;s Shanghai Maths initiative imported teaching methods but could not transplant the growth mindset culture that makes them effective\n• A study comparing Japanese and Canadian students gave half an unsolvable problem: Canadian students who failed internalized it as personal inadequacy and lost motivation; Japanese students pushed harder and became even more motivated to try again\n• How teachers are valued in society — pay and prestige — is another untransplantable contextual factor: Singapore teachers are respected and well-paid; UK teachers are underpaid and taken for granted\n\n💾 <i>Explains with a concrete policy case and a cross-cultural study why the invisible barrier to education reform is cultural belief about effort versus innate ability, not teaching methods.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYSRW1YCar0/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#education #growth-mindset #culture</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Why We Can&#x27;t Copy Top Education Systems</b><br><i>High-performing education systems cannot be transplanted across cultures because the beliefs about effort and innate ability that underpin their success cannot be copied.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Western cultures tend to view academic ability as innate — you either are or are not a maths person</li><li>Many Asian cultures genuinely believe success comes purely from effort and persistence, not fixed talent</li><li>The UK&#x27;s Shanghai Maths initiative imported teaching methods but could not transplant the growth mindset culture that makes them effective</li><li>A study comparing Japanese and Canadian students gave half an unsolvable problem: Canadian students who failed internalized it as personal inadequacy and lost motivation; Japanese students pushed harder and became even more motivated to try again</li><li>How teachers are valued in society — pay and prestige — is another untransplantable contextual factor: Singapore teachers are respected and well-paid; UK teachers are underpaid and taken for granted</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Explains with a concrete policy case and a cross-cultural study why the invisible barrier to education reform is cultural belief about effort versus innate ability, not teaching methods.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYSRW1YCar0/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#education #growth-mindset #culture</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Top education systems cannot simply be copied because the cultural mindset that makes them work — especially beliefs about effort versus fixed ability — is not transferable. The UK&#x27;s Shanghai Maths initiative imported methods but not the growth mindset behind them. A study shows Japanese students respond to failure with increased motivation, while Canadian students internalize it as a sign they lack ability. Teacher status and pay are cited as another untransplantable factor.\n\nWhy can&#x27;t we just copy the world&#x27;s best education systems? Because even if you look at the international rankings and you see that, wow, Japan and Singapore are doing wonderfully in science and maths, maybe we can just borrow their textbooks, borrow their teachers, bring them here to the UK, you are very quickly gonna see that cultural factors will prevent it from working. For example, England really wanted to improve their mathematical results and they saw, great, Shanghai&#x27;s doing an amazing job. Let&#x27;s introduce a scheme called Shanghai Maths. While they can train the teachers in these Shanghai Maths methods, what they cannot transplant is a culture of growth mindset. In the West, we have such different values around effort, persistence, and intelligence. Westerners often view academic ability as an innate gift. I either have it or I don&#x27;t. I am a maths person or I&#x27;m not. Whereas many Asian cultures genuinely believe that everyone can be successful and it is merely a result of effort and persistence. This was exemplified in an incredible study that was done between Japanese students and Canadian students. All the students were given one of two problems. Half of the students were given a problem that was, was relatively easy, quite doable. And the other half were given an unsolvable problem without knowing that it was an impossible task. After doing this first round of problems, the kids were asked, do you want to do another problem? Do you want to try again? Here&#x27;s what&#x27;s really fascinating. The Canadian kids who had done the easy problem, felt confident, felt like they wanted to try the next problem. Whereas the kids who had done the unsolvable problem did not even want to try. They internalized that they must be the problem and that they must be dumb. They are just not maths people. Whereas for the Japanese students, the ones who had done the unsolvable problem believed that they needed to just push harder, work harder, and be more persistent. So they had even more motivation to try the next set problems. We can attempt to transplant incredible pedagogical factors from one education system to another. However, there are infinite contextual factors that will affect this. For example, how we treat our teachers, how the role of the teaching profession is viewed in society. Is it a highly well-paid respectable job like in Singapore? Or is it a lowly taken for granted job like in the UK? You can have the best pedagogical methods in the world on paper, but if it doesn&#x27;t align with the cultural and contextual realities, it&#x27;s not going to work. So, if you are looking for the actual plan. Then we put it in the bag. So, let&#x27;s take a picture.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-29T00:04:03"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZri_Tju290/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>The Collector&#x27;s Fallacy Kills Learning</b>\n<i>Collecting information creates the illusion of learning; real learning requires active processing and retrieval, not saving.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Students who typed notes verbatim learned less conceptually than those who paraphrased (Mueller &amp; Oppenheimer, 2014)\n• Before saving an idea, ask: Can I explain it without looking? Can I connect it to something I already know? Can I turn it into a question?\n• Forcing your brain to retrieve information — not just store it — is what makes learning stick\n\n💾 <i>Explains the cognitive trap of mistaking information collection for learning, backed by research, with a concrete retrieval-based alternative.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZri_Tju290/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#learning #note-taking #productivity</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>The Collector&#x27;s Fallacy Kills Learning</b><br><i>Collecting information creates the illusion of learning; real learning requires active processing and retrieval, not saving.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Students who typed notes verbatim learned less conceptually than those who paraphrased (Mueller &amp; Oppenheimer, 2014)</li><li>Before saving an idea, ask: Can I explain it without looking? Can I connect it to something I already know? Can I turn it into a question?</li><li>Forcing your brain to retrieve information — not just store it — is what makes learning stick</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Explains the cognitive trap of mistaking information collection for learning, backed by research, with a concrete retrieval-based alternative.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZri_Tju290/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#learning #note-taking #productivity</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>The Collector&#x27;s Fallacy is the mistake of thinking storing information equals knowing it. Mueller &amp; Oppenheimer (2014) showed students who typed notes verbatim learned less conceptually than those who paraphrased. The fix: actively process each idea by explaining it, connecting it to existing knowledge, or turning it into a question. Tools like Jungle AI auto-convert notes into flashcards to enforce retrieval practice — the mechanism that actually makes learning stick.\n\nSaving 40 TikToks, screenshots, PDFs and notes feels productive, but that&#x27;s the track. It&#x27;s called the collector&#x27;s fallacy. Your brain mistakes storing information for learning it. But neuroscience and learning research show that your memory gets stronger when you actually process information and not just collect it. Müller and Oppenheimer found that students that typed a lot of their notes often learn less conceptually. This is because they were copying instead of writing the idea in their own words. So instead of saving everything, pick one idea and ask, can I explain this without looking? Can I connect this to something I already know? Can I turn it into a question? As I say in every video, the most efficient way to do this is to use studies like Jungle or any other one you find useful. They automatically turn all your notes into flashcards and quizzes so you&#x27;re not just collecting information, you&#x27;re forcing your brain to retrieve it. That&#x27;s the part that makes learning stick. So you don&#x27;t build a graveyard of ideas that you never use, turn your notes into questions. Because although collecting feels smart, really cool, makes you smarter. And that&#x27;s all. If you like these videos, I have many more. Good luck to them.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-29T00:03:03"
  },
  "https://vt.tiktok.com/ZSQGBuUdD/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Stock Buybacks Explained Simply</b>\n<i>Stock buybacks let corporations use profits to artificially inflate their own stock price — benefiting executives and wealthy shareholders — instead of reinvesting in workers or innovation.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• A stock buyback is when a corporation uses profits to purchase its own shares on the open market, reducing the supply of shares and automatically driving the price up.\n• Buybacks are typically timed near year-end reporting and bonus periods, because executives are compensated based on stock performance.\n• Analogy: it is like buying 10,000 copies of your own novel to land on the NYT bestseller list — you are gaming the metric, not earning it.\n• Buybacks were illegal market manipulation until the SEC legalized them in 1982 under the Reagan Administration.\n• Last year, $800 billion in corporate profits went to buybacks instead of worker wages or benefits.\n• JPMorgan projects corporate share buybacks are on track for a record $1.9 trillion in 2025.\n• The primary beneficiaries are the largest, wealthiest shareholders — not employees.\n\n💾 <i>Concise, plain-language explainer of how stock buybacks work, their history, scale, and who benefits — useful financial literacy reference.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://vt.tiktok.com/ZSQGBuUdD/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#stock_buybacks #financial_literacy #corporate_finance</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Stock Buybacks Explained Simply</b><br><i>Stock buybacks let corporations use profits to artificially inflate their own stock price — benefiting executives and wealthy shareholders — instead of reinvesting in workers or innovation.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>A stock buyback is when a corporation uses profits to purchase its own shares on the open market, reducing the supply of shares and automatically driving the price up.</li><li>Buybacks are typically timed near year-end reporting and bonus periods, because executives are compensated based on stock performance.</li><li>Analogy: it is like buying 10,000 copies of your own novel to land on the NYT bestseller list — you are gaming the metric, not earning it.</li><li>Buybacks were illegal market manipulation until the SEC legalized them in 1982 under the Reagan Administration.</li><li>Last year, $800 billion in corporate profits went to buybacks instead of worker wages or benefits.</li><li>JPMorgan projects corporate share buybacks are on track for a record $1.9 trillion in 2025.</li><li>The primary beneficiaries are the largest, wealthiest shareholders — not employees.</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Concise, plain-language explainer of how stock buybacks work, their history, scale, and who benefits — useful financial literacy reference.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://vt.tiktok.com/ZSQGBuUdD/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#stock_buybacks #financial_literacy #corporate_finance</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Stock buybacks happen when corporations use profits to repurchase their own shares instead of reinvesting in wages or innovation. This reduces share supply and boosts the price, timing that benefits executives whose bonuses are tied to stock performance. The practice was illegal market manipulation until the SEC legalized it in 1982 under Reagan. In the most recent year, $800 billion went to buybacks; JPMorgan projects a record $1.9 trillion in 2025. Critics argue this is artificial inflation that enriches the wealthiest shareholders at workers&#x27; expense.</details>",
    "platform": "TikTok",
    "ts": "2026-06-29T00:03:17"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DVgh8LsDEP9/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>10 Must-Read Book Recommendations</b>\n\n📌 <b>Recommended</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/60784553-the-illusion-of-choice\">The Illusion of Choice</a> — Richard Shotton\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/15055.Oranges_Are_Not_the_Only_Fruit\">Oranges Are Not the Only Fruit</a> — Jeanette Winterson\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/7815.The_Year_of_Magical_Thinking\">The Year of Magical Thinking</a> — Joan Didion\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/en/book/show/355190.Orientalism\">Orientalism</a> — Edward Said\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/48037.Carmilla\">Carmilla</a> — J. Sheridan Le Fanu\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/22628.The_Perks_of_Being_a_Wallflower\">The Perks of Being a Wallflower</a> — Stephen Chbosky\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/30962053-the-idiot\">The Idiot</a> — Elif Batuman\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/485894.The_Metamorphosis\">The Metamorphosis</a> — Franz Kafka\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/52350.The_Stepford_Wives\">The Stepford Wives</a> — Ira Levin\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/11297.Norwegian_Wood\">Norwegian Wood</a> — Haruki Murakami\n\n💾 <i>A diverse roundup of 10 personal book recommendations spanning marketing psychology, literary fiction, gothic horror, postcolonial theory, and coming-of-age classics — useful as a curated reading list.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DVgh8LsDEP9/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#books #reading-list #recommendations</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>10 Must-Read Book Recommendations</b><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/60784553-the-illusion-of-choice\">The Illusion of Choice</a> — Richard Shotton</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/15055.Oranges_Are_Not_the_Only_Fruit\">Oranges Are Not the Only Fruit</a> — Jeanette Winterson</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/7815.The_Year_of_Magical_Thinking\">The Year of Magical Thinking</a> — Joan Didion</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/en/book/show/355190.Orientalism\">Orientalism</a> — Edward Said</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/48037.Carmilla\">Carmilla</a> — J. Sheridan Le Fanu</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/22628.The_Perks_of_Being_a_Wallflower\">The Perks of Being a Wallflower</a> — Stephen Chbosky</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/30962053-the-idiot\">The Idiot</a> — Elif Batuman</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/485894.The_Metamorphosis\">The Metamorphosis</a> — Franz Kafka</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/52350.The_Stepford_Wives\">The Stepford Wives</a> — Ira Levin</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/11297.Norwegian_Wood\">Norwegian Wood</a> — Haruki Murakami</li></ol><br>💾 <i>A diverse roundup of 10 personal book recommendations spanning marketing psychology, literary fiction, gothic horror, postcolonial theory, and coming-of-age classics — useful as a curated reading list.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DVgh8LsDEP9/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#books #reading-list #recommendations</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Compilation reel of 10 book recommendations: The Illusion of Choice (Shotton), Oranges Are Not the Only Fruit (Winterson), The Year of Magical Thinking (Didion), Orientalism (Said), Carmilla (Le Fanu), The Perks of Being a Wallflower (Chbosky), The Idiot (Batuman), The Metamorphosis (Kafka), The Stepford Wives (Levin), and Norwegian Wood (Murakami).\n\nI think everyone should read The Illusion of Choice by Richard Shotton because it&#x27;s really insightful into how marketing tactics affect our behaviour and how we have less choice in buying things than we think. Jeanette Winterson&#x27;s Oranges are Not the Only Fruit. I think it&#x27;s a really interesting plot line, but I think it&#x27;s also a really great way to get into reading books for the writing rather than just reading books for the plot. Joan Didion&#x27;s The Year of Magical Thinking. Even though it&#x27;s a lot about grief, and if that&#x27;s something you haven&#x27;t personally experienced, I think the writing style and how she deals with it is so beautiful. I&#x27;d recommend it to everyone. Orientalism by Edward Said. Will change the way you think about the world? Carmilla by Joseph Sheridan Le Fanu. It&#x27;s the original vampire novel before Dracula, and it&#x27;s just really scary and really fun, and you see where a lot of vampire mythology comes from, and it&#x27;s got lesbian vampires in it, and it&#x27;s a really good book, so I think everyone should read it. I&#x27;m going to go with my personal favourite book, which is Parks and Be a Wallflower. Growing up, it was just a book I love so much, and I think it&#x27;s just such an easy, small read, and I just, yeah, I love that kind of genre. The Idiot by Elle of Bodman. The Metamorphosis. There&#x27;s a lot of very fascinating symbolism to unpick, and it&#x27;s also very entertaining in a very sad and disturbing way. I mean, the most recent book that I read that I thought was really impactful was The Stepford Wives. It&#x27;s about, like, misogyny, but I just think, it&#x27;s such a short fictional story, but it&#x27;s just, wow. Norwegian Word by Hrikai Murakami, I think, is a beautiful book.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-29T00:02:22"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/p/DZqUsBUkeZp/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Caucasus Literature Reading List</b>\n\n📌 <b>Recommended</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/135060.Hadji_Mur_d\">Hadji Murad</a> — Leo Tolstoy\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/226378.A_Hero_of_Our_Time\">A Hero of Our Time</a> — Mikhail Lermontov\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/9123975-the-last-of-the-departed\">The Last of the Departed</a> — Bagrat Shinkuba\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/2808414-the-angel-of-grozny\">The Angel of Grozny</a> — Åsne Seierstad\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/132532225-a-year-among-the-circassians\">A Year Among the Circassians</a> — J.A. Longworth\n\n💾 <i>A Circassian artist&#x27;s personal reading list of Caucasus literature paired with a critical framework for approaching the region&#x27;s complex, multilingual, and politically diverse literary tradition.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DZqUsBUkeZp/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#caucasus #books #circassian</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Caucasus Literature Reading List</b><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/135060.Hadji_Mur_d\">Hadji Murad</a> — Leo Tolstoy</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/226378.A_Hero_of_Our_Time\">A Hero of Our Time</a> — Mikhail Lermontov</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/9123975-the-last-of-the-departed\">The Last of the Departed</a> — Bagrat Shinkuba</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/2808414-the-angel-of-grozny\">The Angel of Grozny</a> — Åsne Seierstad</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/132532225-a-year-among-the-circassians\">A Year Among the Circassians</a> — J.A. Longworth</li></ol><br>💾 <i>A Circassian artist&#x27;s personal reading list of Caucasus literature paired with a critical framework for approaching the region&#x27;s complex, multilingual, and politically diverse literary tradition.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DZqUsBUkeZp/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#caucasus #books #circassian</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Personal, non-exhaustive book list on Caucasus literature by a Circassian diaspora artist. The author frames it as an enriching but emotionally complex body of work — evoking nostalgia, pride, loss, hope, and survival — and stresses that critical reading requires knowing who wrote, when, and from what political standpoint. Books span Tolstoy&#x27;s Hadji Murad to Shinkuba&#x27;s Abkhaz novel on the Ubykh extinction. Carousel image content was not fully accessible; the book list is drawn from post discussion.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-29T00:05:32"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWzMWLlibPB/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>10 Signs of High Agency</b>\n<i>High-agency people share observable behavioral patterns — from immigrant roots and quitting prestige careers to questioning the question itself.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• They or their family are immigrants — brings unusual resourcefulness to get out of tough situations\n• They quit something prestigious (investment banking, consulting) to break free of golden handcuffs and embrace uncertainty\n• Notorious self-learners who teach themselves without needing institutional permission or validation\n• Don&#x27;t trust what others say — validate everything themselves using first principles thinking\n• Grew up with weird, niche teenage hobbies outside the societal norm during peak social pressure years\n• Share niche content online without using social metrics as a gauge of intrinsic content quality\n• Appearance doesn&#x27;t match their interests — defying the stereotypes of their own group\n• They question the question — asking whether the question being answered is even the right one to ask\n\n💾 <i>Concise, observable checklist for identifying high-agency people — useful as a mental model for hiring, relationship-building, or self-assessment.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWzMWLlibPB/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#high-agency #mindset #self-improvement</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>10 Signs of High Agency</b><br><i>High-agency people share observable behavioral patterns — from immigrant roots and quitting prestige careers to questioning the question itself.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>They or their family are immigrants — brings unusual resourcefulness to get out of tough situations</li><li>They quit something prestigious (investment banking, consulting) to break free of golden handcuffs and embrace uncertainty</li><li>Notorious self-learners who teach themselves without needing institutional permission or validation</li><li>Don&#x27;t trust what others say — validate everything themselves using first principles thinking</li><li>Grew up with weird, niche teenage hobbies outside the societal norm during peak social pressure years</li><li>Share niche content online without using social metrics as a gauge of intrinsic content quality</li><li>Appearance doesn&#x27;t match their interests — defying the stereotypes of their own group</li><li>They question the question — asking whether the question being answered is even the right one to ask</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Concise, observable checklist for identifying high-agency people — useful as a mental model for hiring, relationship-building, or self-assessment.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWzMWLlibPB/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#high-agency #mindset #self-improvement</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>The creator outlines increasingly niche observable traits of high-agency individuals: immigrant roots, leaving prestige jobs, self-directed learning, first-principles validation, niche teenage hobbies, sharing content regardless of social metrics, appearance mismatched with interests, and the habit of questioning whether the question itself is worth answering.\n\n10 out of 10 signs that the person you&#x27;re talking to has high agency but it gets increasingly more niche. They or their family are immigrants. Immigrants have a stupid amount of resourcefulness that allows them to get out of the toughest of situations. They quit something of prestige like investment banking or consulting which requires them to break free out of the golden handcuffs and embrace uncertainty. They are notorious self-learners who regularly teach themselves topics without needing the permission of an institution to validate their learnings. They don&#x27;t trust anything anyone says and instead need to validate things themselves using first principles thinking. They grow up with weird teenage hobbies outside of the societal norm during a time period of their life with the highest societal pressures. They send you niche content online without using social metrics as a gauge for intrinsic content quality. Their appearance does not match their interests. Demonstrating an ability to not cave in into the societal norms of their own stereotype. And finally they question the question. Showcasing their ability to think about if the question being answered is even the right question to answer in the first place. Follow for more high agency content.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-29T00:01:52"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZqNVxVS0dS/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>10 Logical Fallacies Explained</b>\n\n📚 <b>Concepts</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ad_hominem\">Ad hominem</a>\n✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Straw_man\">Straw man</a>\n✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/False_dilemma\">False dilemma</a>\n✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slippery_slope\">Slippery slope</a>\n✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argument_from_authority\">Appeal to authority</a>\n✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genetic_fallacy\">Genetic fallacy</a>\n✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argumentum_ad_populum\">Bandwagon fallacy</a>\n✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nirvana_fallacy\">Nirvana fallacy</a>\n✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Texas_sharpshooter_fallacy\">Texas sharpshooter fallacy</a>\n✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motte-and-bailey_fallacy\">Motte-and-bailey fallacy</a>\n\n💾 <i>Compact reference covering 10 logical fallacies in order of increasing obscurity, including several rarely covered in introductory content.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZqNVxVS0dS/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#logical-fallacies #critical-thinking #psychology</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>10 Logical Fallacies Explained</b><br>📚 <b>Concepts</b><br><ol><li>✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ad_hominem\">Ad hominem</a></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Straw_man\">Straw man</a></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/False_dilemma\">False dilemma</a></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slippery_slope\">Slippery slope</a></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argument_from_authority\">Appeal to authority</a></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genetic_fallacy\">Genetic fallacy</a></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argumentum_ad_populum\">Bandwagon fallacy</a></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nirvana_fallacy\">Nirvana fallacy</a></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Texas_sharpshooter_fallacy\">Texas sharpshooter fallacy</a></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motte-and-bailey_fallacy\">Motte-and-bailey fallacy</a></li></ol><br>💾 <i>Compact reference covering 10 logical fallacies in order of increasing obscurity, including several rarely covered in introductory content.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZqNVxVS0dS/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#logical-fallacies #critical-thinking #psychology</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>10 logical fallacies defined in under a minute each: ad hominem, straw man, false dilemma, slippery slope, appeal to authority, genetic fallacy, bandwagon fallacy, nirvana fallacy, Texas sharpshooter, and motte-and-bailey.\n\n10 under 10 logical fallacies, but they get increasingly more niche. Ad homonym, you can&#x27;t win the argument, so you attack the person making it. Straw man, you twist someone&#x27;s words into something easier to argue against instead of addressing what they actually said. False dilemma, you present two options as if they&#x27;re the only ones that exist when there are actually more. Slippery slope, you assume one small decision will automatically lead to the worst possible outcome. Appeal to authority, you believe something is true simply because someone important said it. Genetic fallacy, you reject an idea entirely based on where it came from instead of whether it&#x27;s actually true. Bandwagon fallacy, you believe something is right because everyone else does. Nirvana fallacy, you reject a perfectly good solution because it isn&#x27;t completely perfect. Texas sharpshooter, you cherry pick the evidence that supports what you already believe and ignore everything that contradicts. Mott and Bailey, you argue an extreme position until challenged, then retreat to a safe one and pretend that&#x27;s what you meant, Allah. Follow for more psychology content that challenges everything you believe.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-29T00:01:08"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZ2oqdivHdF/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Coolest Mac Apps Part 1</b>\n\n📌 <b>Recommended</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/paperman-your-screen-as-paper/id6761328838?mt=12\">Paperman</a>\n✅ <a href=\"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/cozy-pomodoro-pixeldoro-mini/id6761088565\">Pixeldoro Mini</a>\n✅ <a href=\"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/clocks-a-fun-standby-mode/id6742508073\">Clocks</a>\n\n💾 <i>Curates three niche Mac apps for eye comfort, focused work, and aesthetic screens that are easy to overlook on the App Store.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZ2oqdivHdF/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#mac #productivity #apps</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Coolest Mac Apps Part 1</b><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li>✅ <a href=\"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/paperman-your-screen-as-paper/id6761328838?mt=12\">Paperman</a></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/cozy-pomodoro-pixeldoro-mini/id6761088565\">Pixeldoro Mini</a></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/clocks-a-fun-standby-mode/id6742508073\">Clocks</a></li></ol><br>💾 <i>Curates three niche Mac apps for eye comfort, focused work, and aesthetic screens that are easy to overlook on the App Store.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZ2oqdivHdF/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#mac #productivity #apps</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Three underrated Mac apps: Paperman adds a matte paper texture to reduce eye strain (five textures, no screen protector needed), Pixeldoro Mini is a free cozy Pomodoro timer with animated pixel backgrounds, lo-fi radio, and a stopwatch, and Clocks offers fun animated greeting clocks usable as screensavers or widgets.\n\nWelcome to the coolest Mac app series part 1. The first app I have for you is Paper Man which basically adds a subtle paper texture to your screen and also maybe eradicates a need for a matte screen protector because your eyes can get tired from looking at the screen all day. You have five textures to choose from but my favorites are Classic Man and Whisper Weave. The second app I have for you is called Pixeldoro Mini. I think it&#x27;s one of the coziest Pomodoro timer app I have come across which is also free. It has animated pixel backgrounds and lo-fi radio and the fact that it&#x27;s there for Mac is chef&#x27;s kiss. What I also love about this app is that I don&#x27;t really have to use the Pomodoro timers to use this app. It also has a stopwatch which makes me love this app even more. The third app is called Clocks which as the name says is a collection of fun greeting clocks. You can set them as screensaver and they look beautiful on your Mac but also feel free to add them as your widgets, customize them and make your screen more expressive. Just comment on set if you want to put all of them in your DMs and follow for more tech videos like these. Bye!</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-29T00:00:39"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZ1mT0vJL_O/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>5 AI Podcasts Worth Your Time</b>\n<i>“if you spend even 30 minutes a day listening to these, you&#x27;ll start thinking like the people building the technology”</i>\n\n📌 <b>Recommended</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/show/2p7zZVwVF6Yk0Zsb4QmT7t\">Latent Space: The AI Engineer Podcast</a> — swyx\n✅ <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/show/4JH4tybY1zX6e5hjCwU6gF\">Dwarkesh Podcast</a> — Dwarkesh Patel\n✅ <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/show/2MAi0BvDc6GTFvKFPXnkCL\">Lex Fridman Podcast</a> — Lex Fridman\n✅ <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/show/1LaCr5TFAgYPK5qHjP3XDp\">Practical AI</a> — Changelog Media\n✅ <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/show/6yHyok3M3BjqzR0VB5MSyk\">The Cognitive Revolution</a> — Nathan Labenz, Erik Torenberg\n\n💾 <i>A curated shortlist of five AI podcasts spanning engineering, researcher interviews, long-form tech discussion, production systems, and research breakdowns.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZ1mT0vJL_O/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#ai #podcasts #learning</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>5 AI Podcasts Worth Your Time</b><br><i>“if you spend even 30 minutes a day listening to these, you&#x27;ll start thinking like the people building the technology”</i><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li>✅ <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/show/2p7zZVwVF6Yk0Zsb4QmT7t\">Latent Space: The AI Engineer Podcast</a> — swyx</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/show/4JH4tybY1zX6e5hjCwU6gF\">Dwarkesh Podcast</a> — Dwarkesh Patel</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/show/2MAi0BvDc6GTFvKFPXnkCL\">Lex Fridman Podcast</a> — Lex Fridman</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/show/1LaCr5TFAgYPK5qHjP3XDp\">Practical AI</a> — Changelog Media</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/show/6yHyok3M3BjqzR0VB5MSyk\">The Cognitive Revolution</a> — Nathan Labenz, Erik Torenberg</li></ol><br>💾 <i>A curated shortlist of five AI podcasts spanning engineering, researcher interviews, long-form tech discussion, production systems, and research breakdowns.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZ1mT0vJL_O/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#ai #podcasts #learning</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Five AI podcast picks covering engineering, top-lab researcher interviews, long-form discussions, production LLM systems, and latest research — presented as the antidote to learning AI through short-form clips.\n\nSome of the best AI education I&#x27;ve gotten wasn&#x27;t from college, it was from podcasts. And here are five that completely changed how I think about AI. First, Latent Space. If you are into AI engineering agents and building products, this one is gold. The second one is Dwarkesh Podcast. He interviews the people actually building the future. Researchers from OpenAI Anthropic and Top Labs and the conversations are insane. Third, Lex Fridman. Long-form discussion with people like Sam Altman. It&#x27;s best if you want to understand where AI is actually heading. The fourth one is Practical AI. Less hype, but more engineering. You learn about production-level systems, LLMs, and real-world AI. And fifth, the Cognitive Revolution. Honestly, this might be the most underrated AI podcast right now. It breaks down the latest research and explains why it matters. Because here&#x27;s the thing. Most people consume AI through 30 secondaries. But if you spend even 30 minutes a day listening to these, you&#x27;ll start thinking like the people building the technology.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-29T00:00:41"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZs6QS2NIFz/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>3 Educational Video Essay YouTubers</b>\n\n📌 <b>Recommended</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/@iamannacorinne\">Anna Howard</a> — Anna Howard\n✅ <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/show/02m1yTsRz6kE1N4sMzG3fS\">Wild Geese</a> — Anna Howard\n⚠️ <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5xmBa5guoIQ\">How Language Shapes the Way We Think</a> — Anna Howard — <i>Found via title-matching YouTube search; author attribution inferred from query context, not confirmed on page</i>\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/@nina.montagne\">Nina Montaigne</a> — Nina Montaigne\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/@mickeyyyy4\">Mickey Galvin</a> — Mickey Galvin\n\n💾 <i>Curated shortlist of three sit-down video essay channels covering philosophy, beauty, creativity, and language — with verified links.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZs6QS2NIFz/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#youtube #video-essays #creators</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>3 Educational Video Essay YouTubers</b><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/@iamannacorinne\">Anna Howard</a> — Anna Howard</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/show/02m1yTsRz6kE1N4sMzG3fS\">Wild Geese</a> — Anna Howard</li><li>⚠️ <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5xmBa5guoIQ\">How Language Shapes the Way We Think</a> — Anna Howard — <i>Found via title-matching YouTube search; author attribution inferred from query context, not confirmed on page</i></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/@nina.montagne\">Nina Montaigne</a> — Nina Montaigne</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/@mickeyyyy4\">Mickey Galvin</a> — Mickey Galvin</li></ol><br>💾 <i>Curated shortlist of three sit-down video essay channels covering philosophy, beauty, creativity, and language — with verified links.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZs6QS2NIFz/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#youtube #video-essays #creators</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Three video essay channels to follow: Anna Howard synthesizes Substack, books, art, and philosophy around big societal concepts and life in your 20s, also hosting the Wild Geese podcast; Nina Montaigne focuses on beauty, creativity, and philosophy with heavy book references; Mickey Galvin covers creativity, media, and being online through a distinctive photo-booth recording style.\n\nHere are three educational YouTubers you should follow to make you smarter. These are more in the sit-down video essay category, so if you would like me to share more of the lifestyle comfort creators that I like, please let me know in the comments. We&#x27;re gonna kick this off with Anna Howard. She hosts the podcast Wild Geese and records all of them over video to post them on YouTube, and that is where I watch everything she has to say. She talks a lot about big concepts that plague our society, life in your 20s, and what I really love is that she brings in articles from Substack, books, art, philosophy. One of my absolute favorite videos that she&#x27;s ever posted is how language shapes the way we think. As a polyglot myself, I related so deeply to what she had to say and didn&#x27;t think of a lot of the connections that she built. Then we have Nina Montaigne. Her video essays are more focused on beauty, creativity, and philosophy, and I particularly love that she makes a lot of book references. She is the embodiment of what you read translates into how you think. And then finally, we have Mickey Galvin. She&#x27;s an actress and filmmaker who does video essays and vlogs on YouTube, and she has a very unique video style. You&#x27;re essentially watching her speak through her photo booth videos as she&#x27;s sharing her screen and popping things up as she talks about them. She talks about creativity, media, entertainment, and just the reflections of being online. And finally, I do have to plug my own YouTube. I just started it. I think I only have a couple videos posted. So if you love the videos that I make here and want to see more of the behind the scenes of my life, that is where you can go to see more of that. And as always, I will see you tomorrow for another one. Ciao, ciao.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-29T00:01:04"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DXYKHebDBww/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Канал «Звучи Лиза» для речи</b>\n\n📌 <b>Recommended</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCqTXX2k1uKaueZ-zWiEpPtg\">Звучи, Лиза!</a>\n\n💾 <i>Рекомендация YouTube-канала по технике речи с описанием конкретного упражнения для развития речевой импровизации.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DXYKHebDBww/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#speech #communication #youtube</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Канал «Звучи Лиза» для речи</b><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCqTXX2k1uKaueZ-zWiEpPtg\">Звучи, Лиза!</a></li></ol><br>💾 <i>Рекомендация YouTube-канала по технике речи с описанием конкретного упражнения для развития речевой импровизации.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DXYKHebDBww/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#speech #communication #youtube</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Рекомендация канала «Звучи, Лиза!» с описанием практического упражнения для речевой импровизации. Упражнение также подходит для практики разговорного английского. Канал рекомендован блогерам и создателям разговорного контента.\n\nЧто я узнала на прошлой неделе вместо скроллинга ленты? Просто отрываю от сердца и отдаю вам канал Звучи Лиза, где вы научитесь красиво говорить. За прошлую неделю я успела посмотреть несколько видео, и я безумно замотивировалась начать работу над своей речью, потому что то, как звучит Лиза, это, конечно, какой-то мед для ушей. Многие упражнения с канала Лизы я также применяю для практики разговорного английского языка. Например, упражнение, где на протяжении 30 секунд нужно говорить на тему одного рандомного слова. По истечении этих 30 секунд ты берешь свое последнее произнесенное слово и начинаешь новый рассказ на эту тему. Я решила сделать для себя такой маленький челлендж и выполнять эти упражнения каждый день для развития своей речевой импровизации. Я думаю, каждый найдет на ее канале то, что нужно именно ему. Особенно это актуально для тех, кто ведет свой блог или снимает разговорный контент и хочет красиво и свободно говорить, в том числе на камеру. Ставьте лайк и подписывайтесь, если было интересно.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-28T23:59:41"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYMsSJOqb_C/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Neuroplasticity Phase 2: Disruption</b>\n<i>Discomfort and stress when starting a new behavior are neurologically required for neuroplasticity to occur — the brain cannot rewire in a state of comfort.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Existing neural pathways are coated in myelin, making habits automatic and energy-cheap — the brain defaults to them to conserve resources.\n• Starting a new behavior is perceived by the brain as a threat; it redirects to formed habits (survival mode) to save energy.\n• The stress and discomfort felt when forming new behaviors are the exact conditions the brain needs for plasticity to occur.\n• Neuromodulators acetylcholine and noradrenaline act as signals that neural pathways need reorganizing.\n• Neuroplasticity cannot happen in states of comfort and familiarity — growth requires disruption.\n\n💾 <i>Explains the neuroscience behind why habit formation feels uncomfortable and reframes discomfort as a necessary signal, not a reason to quit.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYMsSJOqb_C/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#neuroplasticity #habits #productivity</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Neuroplasticity Phase 2: Disruption</b><br><i>Discomfort and stress when starting a new behavior are neurologically required for neuroplasticity to occur — the brain cannot rewire in a state of comfort.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Existing neural pathways are coated in myelin, making habits automatic and energy-cheap — the brain defaults to them to conserve resources.</li><li>Starting a new behavior is perceived by the brain as a threat; it redirects to formed habits (survival mode) to save energy.</li><li>The stress and discomfort felt when forming new behaviors are the exact conditions the brain needs for plasticity to occur.</li><li>Neuromodulators acetylcholine and noradrenaline act as signals that neural pathways need reorganizing.</li><li>Neuroplasticity cannot happen in states of comfort and familiarity — growth requires disruption.</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Explains the neuroscience behind why habit formation feels uncomfortable and reframes discomfort as a necessary signal, not a reason to quit.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYMsSJOqb_C/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#neuroplasticity #habits #productivity</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>To build new habits, you must disrupt the brain&#x27;s existing automatic pathways. The brain perceives new behaviors as threats and tries to revert to old habits to conserve energy. The resulting discomfort is not a problem — it is the biological precondition for neuroplasticity. Neuromodulators acetylcholine and noradrenaline facilitate this rewiring, but only in states of non-comfort.\n\nhow do you become disgustingly productive there are four phases to neuroplasticity but in this video we&#x27;re going to be talking about phase two which is disruption i recommend saving this video because i&#x27;m going to be throwing quite a bit of information at you so you&#x27;ll need to re-watch it so you actually encode the information properly the reason i call this phase disruption is because you&#x27;re literally disrupting your entire identity that you&#x27;ve built on years and years of programming whilst our brains have evolved over the years it still acts to protect and survive you have neural pathways that already exist and are stronger because of the myelin that&#x27;s grown on these pathways so the behavior is more automatic this means they require far less energy so just think about it like this you pick up your phone to scroll without even thinking about it or you can drive to work without even knowing the journey so when you want to start a new behavior you&#x27;re disrupting the program that already exists and the brain goes whoa that&#x27;s a threat and it&#x27;s so it tries to redirect to the formed habits to conserve energy that&#x27;s survival mode so you&#x27;re going to feel stressed and you&#x27;re going to feel uncomfortable when you&#x27;re starting a new behavior but this is exactly the state that you need to be in for the plasticity to occur you have neuromodulators such as acetylcholine and noradrenaline and these act as signals to the brain when neural pathways need reorganizing when you&#x27;re trying to form those new connections and unfortunately this can&#x27;t happen when you&#x27;re in a state of being comfortable and familiarity you</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-28T23:59:37"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DXX4SzZjTH-/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Study Tools for Psych and Neuro</b>\n\n📌 <b>Recommended</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.brainfacts.org/\">BrainFacts.org</a> — Society for Neuroscience\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.turbo.ai/\">Turbo AI</a>\n\n💾 <i>Two verified study tools specifically useful for neuroscience and psychology students that turn dense material into simplified notes, visuals, and audio.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DXX4SzZjTH-/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#neuroscience #study-tools #ai-learning</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Study Tools for Psych and Neuro</b><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.brainfacts.org/\">BrainFacts.org</a> — Society for Neuroscience</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.turbo.ai/\">Turbo AI</a></li></ol><br>💾 <i>Two verified study tools specifically useful for neuroscience and psychology students that turn dense material into simplified notes, visuals, and audio.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DXX4SzZjTH-/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#neuroscience #study-tools #ai-learning</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Short resource-list reel pitching BrainFacts.org (neuroscience explainer site by Society for Neuroscience) and Turbo AI (AI study platform that generates notes, diagrams, and podcasts from course material) as essential tools for psych and neuro students.\n\nIf you&#x27;re in psychology, neuroscience, or anything brain-related, you&#x27;re going to want to know these. Trust me. First, we have BrainFacts.org. It breaks down complex neuroscience topics into super simple explanations. It has visuals with real-world examples, so you actually understand what&#x27;s going on inside the brain instead of just memorizing it. Next is Turbo AI. This thing takes your course content and turns it into clean notes, diagrams, and real-life examples. The chat box is honestly the cheat code. It simplifies complex theories like your five. My favorite feature is the podcast. You can turn dense readings into something you can just listen to while you&#x27;re walking, driving, or at the gym. So instead of forcing yourself to read, you&#x27;re passively learning, and the information sticks way better. You&#x27;re welcome, and follow for more study tips.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-28T23:53:03"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/p/DZz17R5FCgm/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>5 Claude Skills Fix Content</b>\n\n<i>Useful for: Content creators using Claude who want clearer, viral-ready writing that sounds like them rather than like AI.</i>\n\n💾 <i>Practical set of five reusable Claude skills that systematically fix the most common AI content weaknesses: clarity, structure, hooks, originality, and personal voice.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DZz17R5FCgm/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#claude #content-writing #ai-tools</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>5 Claude Skills Fix Content</b><br><i>Useful for: Content creators using Claude who want clearer, viral-ready writing that sounds like them rather than like AI.</i><br>💾 <i>Practical set of five reusable Claude skills that systematically fix the most common AI content weaknesses: clarity, structure, hooks, originality, and personal voice.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DZz17R5FCgm/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#claude #content-writing #ai-tools</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Five custom Claude skills to address AI writing failures: simplify to 8th-grade level, structure like a story, audit hooks against 100 viral examples, strip AI-isms drawn from Wikipedia&#x27;s Signs of AI Writing, and build a voice-DNA file from 20 personal transcripts.\n\n### Slide 1\n*Title slide with orange 3D robot character on keyboard, neutral beige background*\n\nClaude sucks at writing content. These 5 skills fix it.\n\n### Slide 2\n*Skill 01 explanation with before/after comparison showing complex vs. simple language*\n\nSKILL 01 /DUMBIFY\nI built this to rewrite everything at an 8th-grade reading level.\nSimpler language means less mental load.\nIdeas people get instantly go viral.\nBEFORE: Leverage cutting-edge paradigms to optimize synergistic solutions that drive scalable value and maximize stakeholder impact.\nAFTER: Use smart tools. Solve real problems. Make things better. Help more people.\n\n### Slide 3\n*Skill 02 explanation with YouTube video reference and storytelling arc diagram*\n\nSKILL 02 /STORYTELLING\nI turned this YouTube video on writing engaging stories into a skill.\nIt structures your content like a story that holds attention.\nAdicting Storytelling\nHow To Become A Master Storyteller by Kallaway (2.4M views, 1 year ago)\n\n### Slide 4\n*Skill 03 explanation with audience retention graph and hook audit examples*\n\nSKILL 03 /VIRAL-HOOKS\nMost content dies in the first 2 seconds.\nThis skill audits your hook against a library of 100 of the best ones ever written.\nIt tells you why they work, then rewrites yours to go viral as well.\nAUDIENCE RETENTION graph showing GOOD HOOK vs BAD HOOK\nHOOK AUDIT box: Grabs attention quickly, Creates curiosity, Delivers on promise, Triggers emotion\nBEFORE: In this video, I&#x27;m going to talk about...\nAFTER: Most creators never get past this... and it&#x27;s killing their growth.\n\n### Slide 5\n*Skill 04 explanation showing Wikipedia source and before/after AI writing patterns*\n\nSKILL 04 /ANTI-AI-WRITING\nEveryone can smell AI writing now.\nLike the negative parallelism (&quot;it&#x27;s not just X, it&#x27;s Y&quot;).\nI pulled all the patterns from an extensive Wikipedia article and turned them into a skill that strips every one.\nWikipedia:Signs of AI writing\nBEFORE: It&#x27;s not just faster, it&#x27;s smarter.\nAFTER: It works better and gets to the point.\n\n### Slide 6\n*Skill 05 explanation showing transcript analysis process and voice-DNA file components*\n\nSKILL 05 /VOICE-DNA\nEvery other skill makes good content. This one makes it yours.\nI fed my last 20 transcripts to Claude and turned them into a voice-DNA file.\nNow it writes like me. And I&#x27;ll show you how to build yours in 2 minutes.\nProcess: SOURCE TRANSCRIPTS (20 total) → EXTRACT/ANALYZE/PATTERN → VOICE-DNA FILE\nVoice DNA includes: TONE (Direct. Confident. Helpful.), PHRASING (Conversational. Clear.), RHYTHM (Varied. Punchy.), SENTENCE LENGTH (Short to medium.), FAVORITE TRANSITIONS (But here&#x27;s the thing...), PUNCHLINES (Insight. Takeaway. Call-to-action.)\n\n### Slide 7\n*Final slide with call-to-action and author profile photo with name Artem Novitskii*\n\nWANT ALL 5 SKILLS?\nComment &quot;CONTENT&quot;\nand I&#x27;ll send it to you.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-28T23:59:54"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DY_6Mk8SrVY/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>3-Question Copywriting Test</b>\n<i>A three-question test to make any copy more concrete, specific, and ownable.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Can I visualize it? Use imagery you can picture or physically feel — e.g., &#x27;go from the couch to running a 5K&#x27; instead of &#x27;regain fitness&#x27;.\n• Can I falsify it? Use specific, verifiable claims — e.g., &#x27;he&#x27;s 6&#x27;5&quot;, blue eyes, looks like Ryan Gosling&#x27; instead of &#x27;he&#x27;s attractive&#x27;.\n• Can nobody else say it? The most powerful copy is a message only you can claim — uniqueness makes it ownable and memorable.\n\n💾 <i>A simple, repeatable three-question framework for turning vague copy into concrete, specific, and differentiated messaging.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DY_6Mk8SrVY/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#copywriting #marketing #writing</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>3-Question Copywriting Test</b><br><i>A three-question test to make any copy more concrete, specific, and ownable.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Can I visualize it? Use imagery you can picture or physically feel — e.g., &#x27;go from the couch to running a 5K&#x27; instead of &#x27;regain fitness&#x27;.</li><li>Can I falsify it? Use specific, verifiable claims — e.g., &#x27;he&#x27;s 6&#x27;5&quot;, blue eyes, looks like Ryan Gosling&#x27; instead of &#x27;he&#x27;s attractive&#x27;.</li><li>Can nobody else say it? The most powerful copy is a message only you can claim — uniqueness makes it ownable and memorable.</li></ul><br>💾 <i>A simple, repeatable three-question framework for turning vague copy into concrete, specific, and differentiated messaging.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DY_6Mk8SrVY/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#copywriting #marketing #writing</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Three criteria for stronger copy: (1) can you visualize it — use concrete imagery; (2) can you falsify it — use specific, verifiable details; (3) can nobody else say it — make the message uniquely yours.\n\nI watched this viral video on copywriting so you don&#x27;t have to, and this is what stuck with me the most. It&#x27;s a simple three-question test you can check your copy against, and after implementing it, I had immediate improvement. Number one, can I visualize it? Can I literally drop the copy on my foot? The example he gives here is for a fitness program, and instead of using a phrase like regain fitness, use something that you can visualize, like going from the couch to running a 5K. Number two, can I falsify it? Can I actually say whether the sentence is true or false? The example he gives here is if you were setting your friend up on a blind date. Instead of saying, he&#x27;s attractive, he&#x27;s good looking, he has good values, you say, he&#x27;s a man in finance, he&#x27;s 6&#x27;5&quot;, he has blue eyes, and he looks like Ryan Gosling. You get the idea. Third one takes a little more practice to implement, but it&#x27;s can nobody else say it. It&#x27;s most powerful if you are the only one that can say this message. Happy writing. Make sure to check out the full thing if you&#x27;re interested, and follow for more breakdowns.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-28T23:58:47"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZ2HCC4yjn0/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Gladwell&#x27;s Stickiness Factor Explained</b>\n<i>“Once you feel something, it&#x27;s a lot harder to forget.”</i>\n\n📌 <b>Recommended</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/2612.The_Tipping_Point\">The Tipping Point: How Little Things Can Make a Big Difference</a> — Malcolm Gladwell\n✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Tipping_Point\">Stickiness Factor</a> — Malcolm Gladwell\n\n💾 <i>Compact breakdown of Gladwell&#x27;s Stickiness Factor with three concrete, actionable principles for making content memorable and viral.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZ2HCC4yjn0/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#marketing #content #virality</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Gladwell&#x27;s Stickiness Factor Explained</b><br><i>“Once you feel something, it&#x27;s a lot harder to forget.”</i><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/2612.The_Tipping_Point\">The Tipping Point: How Little Things Can Make a Big Difference</a> — Malcolm Gladwell</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Tipping_Point\">Stickiness Factor</a> — Malcolm Gladwell</li></ol><br>💾 <i>Compact breakdown of Gladwell&#x27;s Stickiness Factor with three concrete, actionable principles for making content memorable and viral.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZ2HCC4yjn0/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#marketing #content #virality</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Reel summarizes the Stickiness Factor from The Tipping Point: one clear idea, memorable packaging (phrase, visual, or metaphor), and an emotional hook are the three keys to making content stick and tip into viral spread.\n\nThis guy is so underrated. He wrote this book about how you can get an idea or a video to a tipping point where it just goes crazy and spreads like wildfire. He puts it down to three key factors, but there&#x27;s one that stands out most, which you can apply to your content today. It&#x27;s called the stickiness factor. The main challenge with marketing content is how can I make sure my idea doesn&#x27;t go in one ear and out the other? And the key is the stickiness of your message. First off, you have to have one clear idea that the audience can grab onto. Number two, it has to be packaged in a memorable way. The best way to do this is including a phrase, a visual or a metaphor that people can remember. And number three, create an emotional response. Once you feel something, it&#x27;s a lot harder to forget. So make your message sticky and it might just hit tipping point and go crazy. And make sure to follow for more breakdowns.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-28T23:54:11"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZZ_kJQIc_W/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>4 Resources to Master Copywriting</b>\n<i>“Marketing is the only skill set you need in the 21st century.”</i>\n\n📌 <b>Recommended</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TUMjnmfsPeM\">Learn Copywriting in 76 Minutes – Harry Dry</a> — Harry Dry\n⚠️ <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Eugene+Schwartz+interview+ads+copywriting\">Eugene Schwartz Interview on Writing Better Ads (rare, 1950s-era)</a> — Eugene Schwartz — <i>Described as a rare 1950s video on YouTube. No single canonical YouTube URL confirmed by search; hardtofindseminars.com archives Schwartz material and may host it.</i>\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/13606090-great-leads\">Great Leads: The Six Easiest Ways to Start Any Sales Message</a> — Michael Masterson &amp; John Forde\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyGKFhTf0go\">The Copy Rule I Learned From The Best Copywriter I Know</a> — Alex Hormozi\n\n💾 <i>Short, high-signal list of free and paid copywriting resources spanning foundational 1950s principles to modern practitioners, anchored by the rule of one.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZZ_kJQIc_W/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#copywriting #marketing #resources</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>4 Resources to Master Copywriting</b><br><i>“Marketing is the only skill set you need in the 21st century.”</i><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TUMjnmfsPeM\">Learn Copywriting in 76 Minutes – Harry Dry</a> — Harry Dry</li><li>⚠️ <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Eugene+Schwartz+interview+ads+copywriting\">Eugene Schwartz Interview on Writing Better Ads (rare, 1950s-era)</a> — Eugene Schwartz — <i>Described as a rare 1950s video on YouTube. No single canonical YouTube URL confirmed by search; hardtofindseminars.com archives Schwartz material and may host it.</i></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/13606090-great-leads\">Great Leads: The Six Easiest Ways to Start Any Sales Message</a> — Michael Masterson &amp; John Forde</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyGKFhTf0go\">The Copy Rule I Learned From The Best Copywriter I Know</a> — Alex Hormozi</li></ol><br>💾 <i>Short, high-signal list of free and paid copywriting resources spanning foundational 1950s principles to modern practitioners, anchored by the rule of one.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZZ_kJQIc_W/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#copywriting #marketing #resources</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>A resource list built on the premise that copywriting is the defining 21st-century skill. Four picks: Harry Dry&#x27;s YouTube masterclass on words that sell, a rare Eugene Schwartz interview on treating writing as discipline, Great Leads for copywriting fundamentals, and Hormozi&#x27;s rule-of-one video — all unified by the idea that a confused mind never buys.\n\nMarketing is the only skill set you need in the 21st century. Here are four videos and books that make you disgustingly good at marketing. First, we have this 76-minute copywriting masterclass by Harry Dry. This video teaches you how to use words and structure sentences that influence people to buy. If you learn how to become a good copywriter, you will have clients knocking on your door begging to pay you because they feel like you understand what they need. Number two is this 1950s interview of Eugene Schwartz talking about how to write better ads. This is a rare video, but it is gold. Eugene Schwartz is the mastermind behind billions of dollars of sales in the 1950s, which just goes to show that he mastered things in principle. To him, writing is not a hobby. It is a discipline. He knows that one good ad can attribute millions of dollars in sales, which is why he sometimes spent months refining just one ad. Number three is Great Leads by Michael Masterson. This is a highly tactical version of the principles that Eugene Schwartz teaches and includes everything you need to understand the fundamentals of good copywriting. Plus, it&#x27;s a very easy read, so I&#x27;d highly recommend adding this to your book list. Number four is this copy rule video from Alex Homose. This is a short video you can watch right now that breaks down the rule of one. This is a copywriting principle that a confused mind will never buy. So in all of your copy, you need to have one core message. And here&#x27;s what these four have in common. They all focus on using words to sell. If you can align your marketing message with the thinking patterns of the people you&#x27;re trying to reach and direct them towards what you&#x27;re trying to sell, that is what gets them to buy. I&#x27;ve taken transcripts and summaries from all these four videos and turned them into a Claude Code skill. That way you can become a better copywriter right now. If you want me to send it through, just comment copy below.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-28T23:54:41"
  },
