  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZQtv5TNdYa/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Beginner Electronics to Hackathon Wins</b>\n\n🪜 <b>How to</b>\n1. Get a beginner electronics kit (from Amazon, Tata, or any online store) and learn how every component in it works\n2. Use Instructables, Hackaday, and Arduino Project Hub to find full tutorials for cool projects and build something that genuinely interests you\n3. Enter your build in an online contest — the same sites host competitions you can enter without being in the USA or Europe\n\n<i>Useful for: Beginners in electronics who want to level up from kit projects to winning online contests and hackathons.</i>\n\n💾 <i>Concrete three-step path from beginner electronics kits to contest-winning maker projects, with specific platforms for tutorials and competitions.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZQtv5TNdYa/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#electronics #maker #hackathon</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Beginner Electronics to Hackathon Wins</b><br>🪜 <b>How to</b><br><ol><li>Get a beginner electronics kit (from Amazon, Tata, or any online store) and learn how every component in it works</li><li>Use Instructables, Hackaday, and Arduino Project Hub to find full tutorials for cool projects and build something that genuinely interests you</li><li>Enter your build in an online contest — the same sites host competitions you can enter without being in the USA or Europe</li></ol><br><i>Useful for: Beginners in electronics who want to level up from kit projects to winning online contests and hackathons.</i><br>💾 <i>Concrete three-step path from beginner electronics kits to contest-winning maker projects, with specific platforms for tutorials and competitions.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZQtv5TNdYa/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#electronics #maker #hackathon</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Three steps to go from beginner side projects to hackathon wins: get a beginner electronics kit and learn every component; pick a project you care about using Instructables, Hackaday, or Arduino Project Hub; enter it in an online contest on those same platforms.\n\nHere&#x27;s how I went from building boring beginner side projects to projects that actually win contests and hackathons. So step one is get your hands dirty. For this you&#x27;re actually going to need a beginner electronics kit which you can either get from Amazon or take a lot or any online store but the whole point is get this kit, know how everything works inside it and get your hands dirty. This takes us on to point number two. Build something that matters to you. For me my go-to sites are Instructables, Hackaday and Arduino Project Hub because these sites have full tutorials of cool projects that people have done that you can follow and build for yourself but make sure you build something that matters to you because this is going to take us to point number three is to find online contests. Now it could be online or in person but if you live outside of the USA or Europe finding in-person contests is going to be very hard but that&#x27;s where we switch to online. Good thing is all these sites that I mentioned earlier have these online contests that you can enter. The only problem is that you have to actually put in projects that are good if you want to win. Again, winning isn&#x27;t everything but it does mean something. So get something that you enjoy building, enter it for a contest. If you found this video useful, save it for later and follow for more. See you guys next time.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-28T23:53:06"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZ5yobhRZg4/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Top 5 LinkedIn Formats for Founders</b>\n<i>Five LinkedIn content formats that consistently drive high impressions for tech founders and CEOs.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Origin story: where you were before, the lightbulb moment, the early days, and where you are now.\n• Tweet react: find a relevant tweet and add your take — agree or disagree, but justify it.\n• Industry news commentary: share a headline your ICP would care about, then add expert opinion.\n• Controversial opinion: only works if at least 50% of your space would disagree with it — no lukewarm hot takes.\n• Monthly recap: new hires, ARR growth, new features or service lines — positives are enough.\n\n💾 <i>Comes from someone ghostwriting LinkedIn content at 500k–1M impressions per week for tech CEOs — not theoretical advice.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZ5yobhRZg4/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#linkedin #content #founders</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Top 5 LinkedIn Formats for Founders</b><br><i>Five LinkedIn content formats that consistently drive high impressions for tech founders and CEOs.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Origin story: where you were before, the lightbulb moment, the early days, and where you are now.</li><li>Tweet react: find a relevant tweet and add your take — agree or disagree, but justify it.</li><li>Industry news commentary: share a headline your ICP would care about, then add expert opinion.</li><li>Controversial opinion: only works if at least 50% of your space would disagree with it — no lukewarm hot takes.</li><li>Monthly recap: new hires, ARR growth, new features or service lines — positives are enough.</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Comes from someone ghostwriting LinkedIn content at 500k–1M impressions per week for tech CEOs — not theoretical advice.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZ5yobhRZg4/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#linkedin #content #founders</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Five high-performing LinkedIn formats for founders: origin story, tweet react, industry news commentary, controversial opinion, and monthly recap. Sourced from a ghostwriting operation generating 500k–1M impressions weekly.\n\nWe generate 500,000 to a million impressions ghostwriting LinkedIn content for tech CEOs every week. Here are the top five LinkedIn content formats we see performing for founders right now. Number one is the origin story. This will always work. Share the story of where you were before you started the business, what that little light ball moment was that made you take the leap, what the early days were like, and where you&#x27;re at now. Number two, the tweet react. Find a tweet relevant to what you do and what you sell and add your take to it, whether that&#x27;s agreeing with it or disagreeing with it, as long as you justify it. These are a cheat code on LinkedIn right now. Three, industry news commentary. Kind of similar. You&#x27;re going to find a headline your ICP would care about and then add your take or opinion to it. You&#x27;re both making them aware of the news and adding expert commentary. Number four, the controversial opinion. This only works if 50% of people in your space will not agree with it. You can&#x27;t start a post saying hot take and then give a lukewarm take. Number five, not enough founders do this, by the way, monthly recaps. You don&#x27;t have to talk about the negatives if you don&#x27;t want. That&#x27;s your call. But you certainly can talk about the positives. What new hires did you make? How did ARR grow? What new features or service lines did you add? That type of thing. Anyways, if you want a list of a hundred of these formats, comment lists and I&#x27;ll send them to you.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-28T23:58:33"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/p/DZ5-sXuACTz/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>9 Ways to Yap on Camera</b>\n<i>“casual on camera, calculated in the message”</i>\n\n<i>Useful for: Anyone building a talking-head video presence and wanting to improve delivery, structure, and hooks.</i>\n\n💾 <i>Dense, practical framework for on-camera talking-head video with actionable techniques for structure, delivery, and hooks.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DZ5-sXuACTz/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#content-creation #on-camera #video-delivery</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>9 Ways to Yap on Camera</b><br><i>“casual on camera, calculated in the message”</i><br><i>Useful for: Anyone building a talking-head video presence and wanting to improve delivery, structure, and hooks.</i><br>💾 <i>Dense, practical framework for on-camera talking-head video with actionable techniques for structure, delivery, and hooks.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DZ5-sXuACTz/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#content-creation #on-camera #video-delivery</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>A creator-focused guide to improving on-camera talking-head videos: start mid-thought, stay casual-but-calculated, use the &#x27;so what?&#x27; filter, study outlier videos under 10K, and prioritize volume to find patterns.\n\n### Slide 1\n*Title slide introducing the analysis and framework.*\n\ni analyzed 84 &quot;yap&quot; videos\nhere are 9 ways to get better at yapping on camera\n\n### Slide 2\n*First three practical tips for on-camera presence and body language.*\n\nstart mid-thought\nno one needs the set up.\n\nmake people feel seen or help people see\ndo one or the other\n\nawkward hands → hold something\na drink works\n\n### Slide 3\n*Four more strategies: structure, tone balance, and the role of AI in organizing ideas.*\n\nif you&#x27;re new, start with lists of three\neasiest place to start\n\ncasual but calculated\ncasual on camera, calculated in the message.\n\nhuman mind for ideas, ai to organize\nideas come from you, then organize\n\n### Slide 4\n*The importance of clarity, flexibility in approach, and balancing copy quality with delivery energy.*\n\nask yourself &quot;so what?&quot;\nIf the answer is obviously, you don&#x27;t have a point.\n\nscript, outline, or go off the top\nup to you, do what works\n\nthe hook = dialed copy + energy\nit&#x27;s equal parts of both\n\n### Slide 5\n*Five additional detailed strategies for improving yapping skills through study, testing, and iteration.*\n\nhere are 5 more:\n\n1. don&#x27;t just study big creators, but look for people with less than 10K and study their outlier videos\n2. create a folder of your favorite yap videos and study their first three seconds and text hooks\n3. ask whether or not your video passes the &quot;I believe&quot; test, someone can watch your video and know exactly what you believe.\n4. change the language in the videos that watch so you can disregard the actual words that they&#x27;re saying and focus just on the tonality, delivery, and cadence.\n5. Disregard advice that doesn&#x27;t make sense for you. The goal is for you to take action and if anything slows you down, forget about it. The goal is volume so you can see the data and you can get patterns.\n\n### Slide 6\n*Closing slide with motivational message and Tom Boyd portrait.*\n\nyap mode activated.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-28T23:58:27"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DXvMh_YAJsj/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>5 Psychology Hooks That Always Work</b>\n<i>Five psychological hooks that reliably capture and hold viewer attention on short-form video.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Challenge hook — &#x27;Is it possible to [specific challenge]?&#x27; turns the video into a story with unresolved tension; the viewer stays to close the loop\n• Time compression hook — &#x27;It took me X years, I&#x27;ll teach you in 1 minute&#x27; triggers FOMO by making skipping feel like discarding years of experience\n• Specific results hook — &#x27;What makes [subject] so [specific result]?&#x27; frames a case study and implies authority, viewer stays to learn the secret\n• Question hook — &#x27;How long do you think you have to [action] to [result]?&#x27; forces the viewer to guess, creating an open loop they need resolved\n• The word &#x27;psychology&#x27; alone signals authority and raises attention — it is itself a hook\n\n💾 <i>Compact, reusable reference for five evergreen video hook formulas grounded in psychology.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DXvMh_YAJsj/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#hooks #content-creation #psychology</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>5 Psychology Hooks That Always Work</b><br><i>Five psychological hooks that reliably capture and hold viewer attention on short-form video.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Challenge hook — &#x27;Is it possible to [specific challenge]?&#x27; turns the video into a story with unresolved tension; the viewer stays to close the loop</li><li>Time compression hook — &#x27;It took me X years, I&#x27;ll teach you in 1 minute&#x27; triggers FOMO by making skipping feel like discarding years of experience</li><li>Specific results hook — &#x27;What makes [subject] so [specific result]?&#x27; frames a case study and implies authority, viewer stays to learn the secret</li><li>Question hook — &#x27;How long do you think you have to [action] to [result]?&#x27; forces the viewer to guess, creating an open loop they need resolved</li><li>The word &#x27;psychology&#x27; alone signals authority and raises attention — it is itself a hook</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Compact, reusable reference for five evergreen video hook formulas grounded in psychology.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DXvMh_YAJsj/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#hooks #content-creation #psychology</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>The reel teaches five always-effective video hooks backed by psychology: (1) challenge framing to create story tension, (2) time compression to trigger FOMO, (3) specific results as implied authority via case study, (4) a question to open a loop the viewer must close, and (5) the word &#x27;psychology&#x27; itself as an authority signal.\n\nfive hooks that will always work according to psychology is it possible to goal specific details slash challenge is it possible to run a marathon with no preparation this turns your video instantly into a story by leveraging a challenge there&#x27;s constant tension and the viewer wants to close the loop of what&#x27;s the result it took me 10 years to learn this but i&#x27;ll teach it to you in less than one minute took me 10 years to learn this but i&#x27;ll teach it to you in less than one minute this creates massive fomo and makes it almost impossible to skip trading one minute for 10 years of experience is an irresistible offer what makes subjects specific results so positive what makes dutch men&#x27;s faces so attractive this leverages a case study to establish implied authority the viewer will stay to find out the secret how long do you think you have to insert action to insert result how long do you think you have to work out to burn one pound of fat by asking a question the viewer is instantly engaged to guess for themselves and to stay for the big reveal very strong open loop and finally use the word psychology you just watch this video again to see how powerful this word is</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-28T23:58:37"
  },
