  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZiJ3_JunSZ/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Brain Needs Repetition to Learn</b>\n<i>Spaced study sessions with breaks outperform cramming because the brain needs repeated exposure and downtime to consolidate information.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Cramming into one day is ineffective — the brain needs to see material again the next day to reinforce it\n• Breaks between study sessions let the brain download and store information\n• Revisiting material on subsequent days strengthens retention\n• Studying in one overly long stretch causes you to lose information\n\n💾 <i>Clear, plain-language explanation of why spaced practice beats cramming, useful for anyone building a study habit.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZiJ3_JunSZ/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#learning #memory #studying</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Brain Needs Repetition to Learn</b><br><i>Spaced study sessions with breaks outperform cramming because the brain needs repeated exposure and downtime to consolidate information.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Cramming into one day is ineffective — the brain needs to see material again the next day to reinforce it</li><li>Breaks between study sessions let the brain download and store information</li><li>Revisiting material on subsequent days strengthens retention</li><li>Studying in one overly long stretch causes you to lose information</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Clear, plain-language explanation of why spaced practice beats cramming, useful for anyone building a study habit.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZiJ3_JunSZ/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#learning #memory #studying</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>The brain needs repeated exposure and breaks to process and store information. Cramming into one session is ineffective; revisiting material the next day and taking breaks improves retention.\n\nyour brain needs repetition. It needs time to process and retain information. And so studying over one day to crash for one course is not a good strategy because you need that seeing it again the next day to reiterate it, to reinforce it in your brain. And you need some breaks in between to actually let your brain like download all of that information and store it. Otherwise you lose some of it over too long of stretches.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-28T23:57:35"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYzuZyiiLzZ/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "“<b>When you&#x27;re having fun, you are so much more creative, you make better decisions, you don&#x27;t burn out as fast.</b>”\n— Author not clear\n\n<i>Prioritizing fun over hustle culture leads to more creativity, better decisions, and sustainable business growth.</i>\n💡 <b>Identify one thing that genuinely feels fun and bring it back into your work today.</b>\n\n💾 <i>Concrete personal framework for reintroducing fun into work as a creativity and productivity multiplier, backed by lived experience rather than theory.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYzuZyiiLzZ/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#creativity #burnout #business</i>",
    "markdown": "“<b>When you&#x27;re having fun, you are so much more creative, you make better decisions, you don&#x27;t burn out as fast.</b>”<br>— Author not clear<br><i>Prioritizing fun over hustle culture leads to more creativity, better decisions, and sustainable business growth.</i><br>💡 <b>Identify one thing that genuinely feels fun and bring it back into your work today.</b><br>💾 <i>Concrete personal framework for reintroducing fun into work as a creativity and productivity multiplier, backed by lived experience rather than theory.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYzuZyiiLzZ/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#creativity #burnout #business</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>The creator burned out from hustle culture and rebuilt their work life by niching into enjoyable tasks, chasing curiosities, scheduling play (daily Fortnite at 3pm), working The Artist&#x27;s Way, and treating every project as a low-stakes experiment. Their argument: fun is a productivity and creativity multiplier, not a distraction.\n\nWhat if the reason your business feels stuck isn&#x27;t that you&#x27;re not working hard enough, it&#x27;s because you stopped having fun? I&#x27;m saying this as somebody who tried the hustle culture thing and I will just say it completely burned me out. I lost all my creativity and my business didn&#x27;t even make the kind of money that I thought it would make. I honestly think when you&#x27;re having fun, you are so much more creative, you make better decisions, you don&#x27;t burn out as fast. So at some point a few years ago, I was like, I don&#x27;t actually think I want to start doing things differently. I think I want to prioritize fun. Here&#x27;s some things I did. I niched into work that felt like play to me. So planning an organization is genuinely super fun for me. So I made a whole career out of that. Researching and teaching and talking about topics I love talking about is super fun for me. So I made a whole thing out of that. I started feeding my creativity by chasing curiosities, even if they weren&#x27;t perfectly strategic, reading books on creativity, doing the artist&#x27;s way, experimenting on ideas, product services, even if they weren&#x27;t the perfect thing to do for my business. I started scheduling myself to play Fortnite at 3pm every day because scheduling fun created so much more productivity for me during the day because I had something to look forward to. And I was like, oh, here are the big things I need to get done versus just like writing out the clock until like five or six. I have a whatever I feel like budget in my content where I can post whatever I feel like, not just the strategic validated stuff. I stopped sticking to a rigid routine. So some days I work four hours, some days I work eight. It kind of just depends on my energy and I don&#x27;t shame myself for it anymore. And I treat projects as much as possible as low pressure experiments. So if it flops, like, okay, cool. I learned something. No shame spiral, only a learning upward spiral. So my question to you is what&#x27;s one thing you can bring back into your business that actually feels fun. The first thing that popped into your mind, that&#x27;s the one bring it back today. And also tell me what it is because I love having fun ideas.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-28T23:58:17"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZotm7nxiB_/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "“<b>I don&#x27;t think this part of my journey is asking for intelligence or perfection... but I think it is asking for courage</b>”\n— Author not clear\n\n<i>Intelligent people often overthink themselves out of actions that would lead to growth — sometimes courage matters more than intelligence or perfection.</i>\n💡 <b>Do the awkward, imperfect thing anyway; this stage of the journey asks for courage, not polish.</b>\n\n💾 <i>A concise, lived-experience argument for why action beats overthinking — especially for high-achievers who use intelligence as a shield against discomfort.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZotm7nxiB_/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#overthinking #courage #consistency</i>",
    "markdown": "“<b>I don&#x27;t think this part of my journey is asking for intelligence or perfection... but I think it is asking for courage</b>”<br>— Author not clear<br><i>Intelligent people often overthink themselves out of actions that would lead to growth — sometimes courage matters more than intelligence or perfection.</i><br>💡 <b>Do the awkward, imperfect thing anyway; this stage of the journey asks for courage, not polish.</b><br>💾 <i>A concise, lived-experience argument for why action beats overthinking — especially for high-achievers who use intelligence as a shield against discomfort.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZotm7nxiB_/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#overthinking #courage #consistency</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>The speaker argues that intelligent people often think themselves out of doing things. She shares her own example of posting on social media daily as uncomfortable but necessary practice, concluding that this phase of her journey calls for courage rather than intelligence or perfection.\n\nyeah intelligent people often think so much that they talk themselves out of just doing the things that could lead them to great opportunity or even just revelations and learnings you know sometimes I feel like an idiot for being a scientist and then a lawyer and then a founder like why have I ended up talking on IG reels but I want to be booking two workshops a week for my resilience training business and that&#x27;s a lot of time standing up and talking to people and sharing ideas that will help them so I better practice and what better way to do that than on your phone and maybe I do sound like an idiot it&#x27;s weird to post every day in front of all your friends and maybe I get things wrong but I don&#x27;t think this part of my journey is asking for intelligence or perfection although I&#x27;d like that I&#x27;d like that but I think it is asking for courage so I&#x27;ll see you again soon</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-28T23:57:23"
  },
