  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWe0zgiDlDD/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Triple-Layer ADHD Vlog Format</b>\n<i>A triple-layer Instagram video format — process clip on top, talking head in the middle, process clip on the bottom — drives massive reach and can be used for any vlog content.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Layout: process/activity footage on top, talking-head clip in the middle, process/activity footage on the bottom — all playing simultaneously\n• Originally used by a creator as a YouTube vlog teaser; it went viral, so she adopted it for standalone Instagram vlogs\n• Works for any vlog topic: day-in-the-life, what I eat in a day, travel, etc.\n• Described as an &#x27;ADHD format&#x27; — multiple visual layers running at once keep viewers engaged\n\n💾 <i>Gives a concrete, immediately replicable video layout that reportedly drives large follower growth on Instagram.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWe0zgiDlDD/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#instagram #content-creation #vlog</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Triple-Layer ADHD Vlog Format</b><br><i>A triple-layer Instagram video format — process clip on top, talking head in the middle, process clip on the bottom — drives massive reach and can be used for any vlog content.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Layout: process/activity footage on top, talking-head clip in the middle, process/activity footage on the bottom — all playing simultaneously</li><li>Originally used by a creator as a YouTube vlog teaser; it went viral, so she adopted it for standalone Instagram vlogs</li><li>Works for any vlog topic: day-in-the-life, what I eat in a day, travel, etc.</li><li>Described as an &#x27;ADHD format&#x27; — multiple visual layers running at once keep viewers engaged</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Gives a concrete, immediately replicable video layout that reportedly drives large follower growth on Instagram.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWe0zgiDlDD/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#instagram #content-creation #vlog</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>The reel describes a specific Instagram video format — three simultaneous layers (process/B-roll, talking head, process/B-roll) — that a creator accidentally discovered while teasing a YouTube vlog. It went viral, and she now uses it for all her vlog content. The author calls it an &#x27;ADHD format&#x27; and recommends adopting it.\n\nФормат, который принесет вам буквально тысячи подписчиков. Я это видела у одного креатора, и я не понимаю, почему это еще не разрушилось на весь Инстаграм. Формат влога. Но сверху у нас получается кадр какого-то процесса, посередине разговорное видео и снизу кадр процесса. Девушка вообще-то сделала как анонс своего полного влога на Ютуб. Но она набрала столько просмотров, что она начала делать в таком формате и просто влоги. Как прошел мой день? Что я ем за день? Наше путешествие, абсолютно что угодно можно так показать. И это, знаете, вот такой немножко СДВГ формат, потому что сверху есть видео, снизу есть видео и посередине разговор. Обязательно берите на заметочку и пользуйтесь.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-28T23:57:03"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DaIeQ59tAfu/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>ML Model to Predict World Cup</b>\n\n🪜 <b>How to</b>\n1. Source a GitHub dataset of ~50,000 international matches since 1872 (teams, score, date, match type).\n2. Build an Elo-style strength rating for every team: winner gains points, loser drops, weighted by recency and tournament importance.\n3. Feed both teams&#x27; ratings into a Poisson model to compute expected goals and derive win odds and the most likely score.\n4. Simulate the match 10,000 times and use the win-count percentage as the final prediction.\n\n<i>Useful for: Anyone wanting to build a data-driven sports prediction model from historical match data.</i>\n\n💾 <i>Compact, end-to-end walkthrough of combining Elo ratings and Poisson simulation into a real sports prediction tool.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DaIeQ59tAfu/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#machine-learning #football #prediction</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>ML Model to Predict World Cup</b><br>🪜 <b>How to</b><br><ol><li>Source a GitHub dataset of ~50,000 international matches since 1872 (teams, score, date, match type).</li><li>Build an Elo-style strength rating for every team: winner gains points, loser drops, weighted by recency and tournament importance.</li><li>Feed both teams&#x27; ratings into a Poisson model to compute expected goals and derive win odds and the most likely score.</li><li>Simulate the match 10,000 times and use the win-count percentage as the final prediction.</li></ol><br><i>Useful for: Anyone wanting to build a data-driven sports prediction model from historical match data.</i><br>💾 <i>Compact, end-to-end walkthrough of combining Elo ratings and Poisson simulation into a real sports prediction tool.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DaIeQ59tAfu/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#machine-learning #football #prediction</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Walkthrough of building a football prediction model: compute Elo-style ratings from historical data, run a Poisson model for expected goals, simulate each match 10,000 times, and read off the win probability and most-likely score.\n\nMy friends convinced me to join their prediction pool for the World Cup, so let&#x27;s build a machine learning model to try to beat them. For this project, I will use a dataset on GitHub that has almost every international match ever played. That comes out to about 50,000 international games since 1872. For each game, we get the two teams, the final score, when it was played and what kind of game it was. Using all of that data, I can build a strength index for every team. Basically, after each game, the winner gains rating and the loser drops, with recent matches and more important tournaments counting more points. This way, every team ends up with a single number of how strong they actually are right now. It&#x27;s kind of like a chess elo bit for football. To predict the game, I take the two teams&#x27; ratings and turn that into the expected goals for both sides using a Poisson model. That gives me the odds of a team winning and the most likely score. But because one match could be mostly luck, a single prediction is meaningless. So I simulate the game 10,000 times and count how often each team wins. And that final percentage is the real prediction. So let&#x27;s try it out on the upcoming game of Brazil vs Japan. As we see, Brazil definitely has the higher odds and the model suggests that the 1-0 is the most likely score. So I&#x27;m very excited to see how that game works out. If you want the full code to play around with it, comment World Cup and I&#x27;ll send it over to you. I&#x27;ll send it over. Thank you.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-28T23:57:09"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DaJJ4S2uTPZ/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>10 Best Sports and Psychology Books</b>\n<i>“The team captain is not usually the best player on the team, but they&#x27;re the most important player on the team in ways that are often hard to measure.”</i>\n\n📌 <b>Recommended</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/en/book/show/905.The_Inner_Game_of_Tennis\">The Inner Game of Tennis</a> — W. Timothy Gallwey\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/208942.The_Way_of_the_Champion\">The Way of the Champion</a> — Jerry Lynch\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/57044566-letters-to-a-young-athlete\">Letters to a Young Athlete</a> — Chris Bosh\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/54975.Levels_of_the_Game\">Levels of the Game</a> — John McPhee\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/31869155-the-captain-class\">The Captain Class</a> — Sam Walker\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/8789058-the-way-of-baseball\">The Way of Baseball: Finding Stillness at 95 Mph</a> — Shawn Green\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/214399103-what-you-re-made-for\">What You&#x27;re Made For</a> — George Raveling and Ryan Holiday\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/75406.The_Breaks_of_the_Game\">The Breaks of the Game</a> — David Halberstam\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/2195464.What_I_Talk_About_When_I_Talk_About_Running\">What I Talk About When I Talk About Running</a> — Haruki Murakami\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/41795733-range\">Range: Why Generalists Triumph in a Specialized World</a> — David Epstein\n\n💾 <i>A verified, linked list of 10 highly regarded books on sports psychology, athletic discipline, and team dynamics from a trusted curator.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DaJJ4S2uTPZ/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#sports #books #psychology</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>10 Best Sports and Psychology Books</b><br><i>“The team captain is not usually the best player on the team, but they&#x27;re the most important player on the team in ways that are often hard to measure.”</i><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/en/book/show/905.The_Inner_Game_of_Tennis\">The Inner Game of Tennis</a> — W. Timothy Gallwey</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/208942.The_Way_of_the_Champion\">The Way of the Champion</a> — Jerry Lynch</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/57044566-letters-to-a-young-athlete\">Letters to a Young Athlete</a> — Chris Bosh</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/54975.Levels_of_the_Game\">Levels of the Game</a> — John McPhee</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/31869155-the-captain-class\">The Captain Class</a> — Sam Walker</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/8789058-the-way-of-baseball\">The Way of Baseball: Finding Stillness at 95 Mph</a> — Shawn Green</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/214399103-what-you-re-made-for\">What You&#x27;re Made For</a> — George Raveling and Ryan Holiday</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/75406.The_Breaks_of_the_Game\">The Breaks of the Game</a> — David Halberstam</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/2195464.What_I_Talk_About_When_I_Talk_About_Running\">What I Talk About When I Talk About Running</a> — Haruki Murakami</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/41795733-range\">Range: Why Generalists Triumph in a Specialized World</a> — David Epstein</li></ol><br>💾 <i>A verified, linked list of 10 highly regarded books on sports psychology, athletic discipline, and team dynamics from a trusted curator.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DaJJ4S2uTPZ/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#sports #books #psychology</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Curated list of 10 books spanning sports psychology, team leadership, and athletic mindset — including Tom Brady&#x27;s pick The Inner Game of Tennis, Sam Walker&#x27;s The Captain Class on team captains, Murakami on running, David Epstein&#x27;s Range, and a basketball memoir co-written with Ryan Holiday.\n\nIf you&#x27;re an athlete or you have an athlete in your life, these are the best books about sports and sports psychology that I would recommend. This is Tom Brady&#x27;s favorite book, The Inner Game of Tennis. Absolutely incredible about the mental side of things. Maybe the greatest lacrosse player of all time. This is The Way of the Champion. Little meditations every day on how to be your best. Chris Bosch, Letters to a Young Athlete. Speaking of tennis, John McPhee&#x27;s Levels of the Game about Arthur Ashe, just absolutely incredible. Love this one. This is one of my favorites, Captain&#x27;s Class. Like we think it&#x27;s all about the star players, the sort of marquee players, the franchise players. But no, it&#x27;s actually about the team captains, the one that bring all those people together. The team captain is not usually the best player on the team, but they&#x27;re the most important player on the team in ways that are often hard to measure. John Green&#x27;s The Way of Baseball. I love this book. Recommended this one all the time. This is sort of zen and baseball, two things that maybe don&#x27;t go together as often as you think they would. I was lucky enough to work on this book with the great George Ravlin, who brought Michael Jordan to Nike. It&#x27;s probably the best basketball book ever written, though. Halberstam&#x27;s The Breaks of the Game. Murakami&#x27;s What I Talk About When I Talk About Running. Here&#x27;s another one about writing as a discipline, which I really like. David Epstein&#x27;s Rage. It&#x27;s both a parenting book in disguise. It&#x27;s a business self-help book, but it&#x27;s also fundamentally about sports. This is just a great book that everyone should read, especially if you&#x27;re starting to get really good at something, or your kids are starting to really get good at something. God, I love this one.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-28T23:56:26"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZfH8l6Nc_W/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Luxury Waste Is the Signal</b>\n<i>Luxury goods function as costly status signals because the wasteful spending itself — not the object — is what communicates wealth and high status to others.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Signaling requires spending what others cannot afford — the inaccessibility of the purchase is what makes it a signal.\n• Once a luxury becomes accessible to many, it loses its signaling power and a pricier substitute is needed.\n• Desire that fades when others gain access was never about the object — it was about exclusive status.\n• Conspicuous waste works as a signal precisely because it demonstrates surplus resources no one else can burn.\n• The pursuit of ever-larger signals can become self-destructive if the fire grows out of control.\n• Participating in status-signaling games is a choice — you can decide when to stop.\n\n💾 <i>Explains costly signaling theory through escalating luxury examples with a memorable fire metaphor, making an abstract economic concept immediately intuitive.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZfH8l6Nc_W/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#signaling #status #luxury</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Luxury Waste Is the Signal</b><br><i>Luxury goods function as costly status signals because the wasteful spending itself — not the object — is what communicates wealth and high status to others.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Signaling requires spending what others cannot afford — the inaccessibility of the purchase is what makes it a signal.</li><li>Once a luxury becomes accessible to many, it loses its signaling power and a pricier substitute is needed.</li><li>Desire that fades when others gain access was never about the object — it was about exclusive status.</li><li>Conspicuous waste works as a signal precisely because it demonstrates surplus resources no one else can burn.</li><li>The pursuit of ever-larger signals can become self-destructive if the fire grows out of control.</li><li>Participating in status-signaling games is a choice — you can decide when to stop.</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Explains costly signaling theory through escalating luxury examples with a memorable fire metaphor, making an abstract economic concept immediately intuitive.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZfH8l6Nc_W/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#signaling #status #luxury</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Luxury spending works as a status signal because it demonstrates you can afford to waste money others cannot. Once a luxury becomes broadly accessible, its signal collapses and status-seekers must escalate to pricier goods. This is Michael Spence&#x27;s signaling theory (Nobel 2001). The reel closes with a fire analogy — a controlled fire gives light and warmth, but a fire grown too large kills — and the observation that staying in the status game is ultimately a choice.\n\nThe only way to prove to the world that you&#x27;re worth it is by setting some money on fire, in public, on something entirely useless. Why do you want a Goyard bag? It&#x27;s coated canvas with some print on it. It&#x27;s 3 lakh rupees. Some people don&#x27;t even want diamonds anymore because Labgron has made it so accessible for everyone. Whichever part of your desire dies because other people have access to things that only you once had access to wasn&#x27;t really about the thing anyway. It was about the signal. Simply by looking at faces you can&#x27;t really tell if someone&#x27;s wealthy, successful, intelligent, fashionable, has great taste and so you have to signal, you have to project, you have to let everyone know that you&#x27;re actually a high type. Lighting a fire that others cannot afford to light. The waste isn&#x27;t a side effect, it is the message. Michael Spence won the Nobel Prize for this idea in 2001. Buy a coach, small fire, a lot of people can afford it, you also have it. You want to light a bigger fire? Prada. Bigger fire? Birkin. And if this fire is also not big enough, buy Princess Diana&#x27;s pre-owned Lady Dior at $325,000 because it&#x27;s worth it. Once everyone can afford your fire, you need a bigger one. As long as the fire is in control, it gives you light and warmth. But if the fire gets big enough, it&#x27;ll kill you. Being a social animal is really difficult. But the good thing is, you get to choose how long you want to be in the game for.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-28T23:57:01"
  },
