    "markdown": "<b>Favorite Niche Internet Resources</b><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.sternberg-press.com/\">Sternberg Press</a></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.setmargins.press/\">Set Margins</a></li><li>⚠️ <a href=\"https://issuesmagshop.com/\">Issues</a> — <i>Closest match found is Issues Magazine Shop (issuesmagshop.com), a curated independent magazine retailer. Cannot confirm this is the exact reference in the reel.</i></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.tiat.place/\">Tiat</a></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://socratica.info/\">Socratica</a></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://sublime.app/\">Sublime</a></li><li>⚠️ <a href=\"https://x.com\">Design Twitter</a> — <i>Not a specific URL — refers to the design community on X (formerly Twitter); no canonical page exists.</i></li></ol><br>💾 <i>Curated list of niche sources for cultural criticism, creative technology, and idea discovery that are hard to rediscover once lost.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DUa3xdzlJLK/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#curation #niche #resources</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>A personal list of niche resources: Sternberg Press, Set Margins, and Issues for cultural criticism and creative non-fiction; Tiat and Socratica for art-meets-technology community and building; Sublime and Design Twitter for curated ideas and small internet pockets.\n\nAs someone who finds niches way more interesting than trends, here are some places I keep coming back to. For books and articles, I&#x27;ve been loving Sternberg Press, Set Margins, and Issues. They&#x27;re great for cultural criticism and creative non-fiction. I also find so much inspiration from creative spaces like Tiat and Socratica. These are places where art and technology intersect and all about experimentation and building things. Finally, we have Sublime and Design Twitter if carefully curated. These are great for diving deeper into an idea or finding small pockets of the internet.</details>",
