  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWMPqqMqnk_/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Travel Before the Winning Streak</b>\n<i>Travel right before a major breakthrough is a repeating pattern across Steve Jobs, the Beatles, and Phil Knight, and is supported by a research study of 26,000 artists and scientists.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• A claimed study of 26,000 artists and scientists found travel was the common factor before their greatest ideas (specific study not independently verified — closest found is a Nobel laureate mobility study from Ohio State / NYU Abu Dhabi)\n• Steve Jobs went to India in 1974 before co-founding Apple\n• The Beatles went to Hamburg, Germany, where intensive club performances shaped their sound before global fame\n• Phil Knight traveled the world and landed in Japan, where he discovered the shoe manufacturer that directly led to Nike\n• Creator attributes his own winning streak to a year of travel after a 2017 brain tumor: he later helped produce what he calls the biggest Nike commercial ever, won an Emmy, reached 9M social media followers, and built a large nonprofit\n\n💾 <i>Packages a memorable research claim and three iconic founder examples into a single principle — travel as a deliberate pre-breakthrough strategy — making it easy to recall and repeat.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWMPqqMqnk_/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#travel #creativity #success</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Travel Before the Winning Streak</b><br><i>Travel right before a major breakthrough is a repeating pattern across Steve Jobs, the Beatles, and Phil Knight, and is supported by a research study of 26,000 artists and scientists.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>A claimed study of 26,000 artists and scientists found travel was the common factor before their greatest ideas (specific study not independently verified — closest found is a Nobel laureate mobility study from Ohio State / NYU Abu Dhabi)</li><li>Steve Jobs went to India in 1974 before co-founding Apple</li><li>The Beatles went to Hamburg, Germany, where intensive club performances shaped their sound before global fame</li><li>Phil Knight traveled the world and landed in Japan, where he discovered the shoe manufacturer that directly led to Nike</li><li>Creator attributes his own winning streak to a year of travel after a 2017 brain tumor: he later helped produce what he calls the biggest Nike commercial ever, won an Emmy, reached 9M social media followers, and built a large nonprofit</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Packages a memorable research claim and three iconic founder examples into a single principle — travel as a deliberate pre-breakthrough strategy — making it easy to recall and repeat.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWMPqqMqnk_/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#travel #creativity #success</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>A study (claimed 26,000 subjects, not independently verified) found travel was the common precursor to greatest ideas among artists and scientists. Steve Jobs went to India, the Beatles to Hamburg, Phil Knight to Japan — each starting their defining run afterward. The creator mirrors this: after a 2017 brain tumor forced him to leave his business and travel for a year, he co-created what he describes as the biggest Nike commercial ever, won an Emmy, grew to 9M followers, and launched a major nonprofit.\n\nWhat if I told you that Steve Jobs, the Beatles, and the founder of Nike all had something in common? That right before when they became successful, they all did this one thing. There was a research study done with 26,000 artists and scientists because they wanted to see what happened right before their greatest ideas. And there was one thing in common. Travel. Like, this is crazy. Like, Steve Jobs went to India. The Beatles, they went to Germany. And Phil Knight, the founder of Nike, he went all over the world. And he ended up in a shoe manufacturer in Japan. Like, they all have this one thing in common right before they started, like, their life winning streak. This even happened in my life. It was 2017. I got sick with a brain tumor and I left my business. And I spent an entire year just traveling. And what happened after that started my winning streak in life. I literally helped Nike create the biggest Nike commercial of all time. We won an Emmy. I got 9 million followers on social media. Created one of the biggest nonprofits on the internet. Like, I went and traveled. And then my winning streak began as well. To every dreamer out there who has a business idea, like, give yourself permission to go travel. And just know that that is a fundamental strategy in becoming successful. Yeah.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-29T13:12:27"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYNF3bVRSoc/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Advanced Weekly Instagram Posting Schedule</b>\n\n🪜 <b>How to</b>\n1. 1 medium-effort 30-second reel per week\n2. 1-2 shareable carousels per week\n3. 1 higher-effort 1-minute+ signature series per week\n4. 1-2 lower-effort 7-15 second reels per week\n5. 1 higher-effort 1-minute+ reel per week\n\n<i>Useful for: Content creators who want a structured, effort-balanced weekly posting plan for Instagram growth.</i>\n\n💾 <i>Gives a concrete, effort-tiered weekly posting framework designed to keep the algorithm happy without burning out.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYNF3bVRSoc/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#instagram #content-calendar #growth</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Advanced Weekly Instagram Posting Schedule</b><br>🪜 <b>How to</b><br><ol><li>1 medium-effort 30-second reel per week</li><li>1-2 shareable carousels per week</li><li>1 higher-effort 1-minute+ signature series per week</li><li>1-2 lower-effort 7-15 second reels per week</li><li>1 higher-effort 1-minute+ reel per week</li></ol><br><i>Useful for: Content creators who want a structured, effort-balanced weekly posting plan for Instagram growth.</i><br>💾 <i>Gives a concrete, effort-tiered weekly posting framework designed to keep the algorithm happy without burning out.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYNF3bVRSoc/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#instagram #content-calendar #growth</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>The reel prescribes a 5-format weekly schedule: 1 medium-effort 30-sec reel, 1–2 shareable carousels, 1 higher-effort signature series (1 min+), 1–2 low-effort 7–15 sec reels, and 1 additional higher-effort reel (1 min+). The premise is that the algorithm rewards variety and balance, and that most creators overcomplicate their calendars.\n\nCreating content becomes a whole lot easier when you realize all you need to do is post one medium effort 30 second reel every single week like this one to two shareable carousels like this one higher effort one minute or longer signature series like this one to two lower effort seven to fifteen second reels like this and one higher effort one minute or longer reel like this oh and just like that we strategically balanced out your week and collectively stopped over complicating content</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-29T22:20:49"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/p/DZpvrJej8s2/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Old Instagram vs New Instagram</b>\n<i>Instagram strategy has shifted — creators who win now use format diversity, value-first profiles, algorithm metrics (watch time, shares), evergreen content, and collabs instead of solo posting.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Content format: use the 40/30/20 rule — reels for reach (40%), carousels for depth (30%), stories for sales (20%); each format gets ONE job.\n• Profile aesthetic: value &gt; beauty — a clear profile that says what you do wins over a pretty grid with weak content.\n• What to measure: shares (4–5x weight) and watch time matter far more than likes (1x) to the algorithm.\n• Content shelf life: aim for evergreen content that lasts weeks to months; Instagram now reads captions, on-screen text, and audio to surface it.\n• Account growth: do 2 collabs a month instead of growing alone — a whole new crowd finds you.\n\n💾 <i>Concise side-by-side breakdown of outdated vs. current Instagram tactics across five key creator levers.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DZpvrJej8s2/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#instagram #content-strategy #creator-growth</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Old Instagram vs New Instagram</b><br><i>Instagram strategy has shifted — creators who win now use format diversity, value-first profiles, algorithm metrics (watch time, shares), evergreen content, and collabs instead of solo posting.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Content format: use the 40/30/20 rule — reels for reach (40%), carousels for depth (30%), stories for sales (20%); each format gets ONE job.</li><li>Profile aesthetic: value &gt; beauty — a clear profile that says what you do wins over a pretty grid with weak content.</li><li>What to measure: shares (4–5x weight) and watch time matter far more than likes (1x) to the algorithm.</li><li>Content shelf life: aim for evergreen content that lasts weeks to months; Instagram now reads captions, on-screen text, and audio to surface it.</li><li>Account growth: do 2 collabs a month instead of growing alone — a whole new crowd finds you.</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Concise side-by-side breakdown of outdated vs. current Instagram tactics across five key creator levers.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DZpvrJej8s2/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#instagram #content-strategy #creator-growth</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>thecreative.social breaks down five areas where Instagram strategy has evolved: using the 40/30/20 format rule, prioritising value over grid aesthetics, measuring shares/watch time over likes, creating evergreen content, and growing through monthly collabs rather than solo posting.\n\n<b>Slide 1</b> — <i>Title slide with decorative star stickers and a photo of a woman by sunflowers and a motorbike.</i>\nold instagram vs new instagram\n\n<b>Slide 2</b> — <i>Split layout comparing single-format posting to the 40/30/20 content mix rule, shown with bar charts.</i>\nCONTENT FORMAT\n\nold\nonly reels. post and pray.\none format doing all the work\n\nnew\nthe 40/30/20 rule:\nreels · reach 40%\ncarousels · depth 30%\nstories · sales 20%\n*each format gets ONE job*\n\n<b>Slide 3</b> — <i>Split layout showing a polished aesthetic grid on the left versus a value-focused profile grid on the right.</i>\nPROFILE AESTHETIC\n\nold\nperfect grid\nA pretty grid won&#x27;t save weak content.\n\nnew\nvalue &gt; beauty\na clear profile that says what you do WINS!\n\n<b>Slide 4</b> — <i>Split layout with bar chart showing shares carry 4–5x the algorithmic weight of likes.</i>\nWHAT TO MEASURE\n\nold\nlikes &amp; views = the vanity scoreboard\nfeels good but grows nothing.\n\nnew\nwhat the algorithm counts\nlikes 1x\nwatch time high\nshares 4-5x\n\n<b>Slide 5</b> — <i>Split layout contrasting 24-hour post lifespan with evergreen content that Instagram surfaces via text/audio parsing.</i>\nCONTENT SHELF LIFE\n\nold\n24 hours\nblink and it&#x27;s buried forever\n\nnew\nweeks → months (evergreen)\ninstagram reads your captions, on-screen text, even your audio now.\n\n<b>Slide 6</b> — <i>Split layout with a diagram showing solo growth versus networked collab growth connecting multiple creators.</i>\nACCOUNT GROWTH\n\nold\ngrowing ALONE\ntoo nervous to share your people\n\nnew\nteam up monthly\n2 collabs a month and a whole new crowd finds you.\n\n<b>Slide 7</b> — <i>Closing quote on a handwritten-style paper pinned against a sunset ocean backdrop.</i>\nlet go of what you can&#x27;t control, and focus on what you can grow.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-29T23:12:59"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYIz3G_tu1h/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "“<b>you can reconcile a favorable public image or become a profit machine but almost never at the same time</b>”\n— carmscrolls\n\n<i>The &quot;influencer cliff&quot; — when fans disengage from a creator — most reliably happens when creators cross from relatable person to corporate entity, because people root for people, not brands.</i>\n💡 <b>The moment a creator becomes a brand with a team and a business empire, the parasocial bond that made followers root for them breaks.</b>\n\n💾 <i>Coins the &quot;influencer cliff&quot; concept and gives a structural explanation for why parasocial bonds reliably break when creators scale into brands.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYIz3G_tu1h/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#influencer #parasocial #creator-economy</i>",
    "markdown": "“<b>you can reconcile a favorable public image or become a profit machine but almost never at the same time</b>”<br>— carmscrolls<br><i>The &quot;influencer cliff&quot; — when fans disengage from a creator — most reliably happens when creators cross from relatable person to corporate entity, because people root for people, not brands.</i><br>💡 <b>The moment a creator becomes a brand with a team and a business empire, the parasocial bond that made followers root for them breaks.</b><br>💾 <i>Coins the &quot;influencer cliff&quot; concept and gives a structural explanation for why parasocial bonds reliably break when creators scale into brands.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYIz3G_tu1h/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#influencer #parasocial #creator-economy</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>carmscrolls argues that the influencer cliff happens most predictably when creators move from platform influence to outside business: once they have a team and a brand empire, the parasocial bond breaks because people root for people, not corporations. Top fan-disengagement triggers are feeds that feel entirely commercial and values misaligned with actions. Examples: Alex Cooper, Matilda Djerf, Victoria Paris, Kendall Jenner&#x27;s 818 and Hailey Bieber&#x27;s Rhode.\n\nI think we&#x27;re witnessing the rise of something I&#x27;m going to call the influencer cliff which is when fans or followers fall out of love with an internet figure they once held dear. This has to do with the fragility of parasocial relationships but also the fact that influencer growth trajectories leave little room for anything aside from selling out. It&#x27;s basically a system where you&#x27;re designed to take the bait and then record the entire dismantling for everyone&#x27;s viewing pleasure. So these breakups tend to happen in two ways. One is a gradual uncoupling but the other is sudden and intense as we saw in the case of Bran Flakes or Victoria Paris at Coachella and they recognize Coachella is now several weeks in the very distant past but that festival is a pressure cooker for the unspoken contracts we hold with public figures and what happens when they break them. I came across this creator&#x27;s video recently where she pulled her own followers and asked them what makes them stop liking a creator and the number one reason unsurprisingly is when their entire feed feels like a commercial but number four was also very much related when values don&#x27;t align with actions and this is a kind of half-baked theory at this point but I feel like the influencer cliff often takes place at this juncture when creators try to move from a platform level sphere of influence to viable business in the outside world. We&#x27;ve seen this happen with modern girl bosses like Matilda Jurf and Alex Cooper as the business practices and working conditions inside their girl boss empires gets called into question because at that point they&#x27;ve ascended from relatable and scrappy to full-on corporate entity with a team and people just don&#x27;t root for brands in the same way that they root for a person. Ironically I think some public figures become entrepreneurs so that they don&#x27;t have to do paid partnerships. They can just rep their own brand as we saw with notable activations at Coachella like Kendall Jenner and 818 Tequila or Hailey Bieber and Rose. Strangely enough I feel parasocial sympathy for creators trying to make it in this age of algorithmic exposure when you can reconcile a favorable public image or become a profit machine but almost never at the same time. So</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-29T23:46:34"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/p/DaMRI2mDq2u/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>5 Books for Career Clarity</b>\n\n📌 <b>Recommended</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/13525945-so-good-they-can-t-ignore-you\">So Good They Can&#x27;t Ignore You</a> — Cal Newport\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/34964837-the-happiness-curve\">The Happiness Curve: Why Life Gets Better After 50</a> — Jonathan Rauch\n✅ <a href=\"https://blogmaverick.com/2012/03/18/dont-follow-your-passion-follow-your-effort/\">Don&#x27;t Follow Your Passion, Follow Your Effort</a> — Mark Cuban\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/124950497-ikigai\">Ikigai: The Japanese Secret to a Long and Happy Life</a> — Héctor García and Francesc Miralles\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/26046333-designing-your-life\">Designing Your Life: How to Build a Well-Lived, Joyful Life</a> — Bill Burnett and Dave Evans\n\n💾 <i>Curated shortlist of five books that reframe career decisions around skill-building, experimentation, and purpose rather than passion-chasing.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DaMRI2mDq2u/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#career #books #skill-building</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>5 Books for Career Clarity</b><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/13525945-so-good-they-can-t-ignore-you\">So Good They Can&#x27;t Ignore You</a> — Cal Newport</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/34964837-the-happiness-curve\">The Happiness Curve: Why Life Gets Better After 50</a> — Jonathan Rauch</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://blogmaverick.com/2012/03/18/dont-follow-your-passion-follow-your-effort/\">Don&#x27;t Follow Your Passion, Follow Your Effort</a> — Mark Cuban</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/124950497-ikigai\">Ikigai: The Japanese Secret to a Long and Happy Life</a> — Héctor García and Francesc Miralles</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/26046333-designing-your-life\">Designing Your Life: How to Build a Well-Lived, Joyful Life</a> — Bill Burnett and Dave Evans</li></ol><br>💾 <i>Curated shortlist of five books that reframe career decisions around skill-building, experimentation, and purpose rather than passion-chasing.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DaMRI2mDq2u/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#career #books #skill-building</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Five career-clarity books: Cal Newport on building rare skills over passion, Jonathan Rauch on the U-shaped happiness curve, Mark Cuban on following effort not passion, the Ikigai framework for finding meaningful work, and Burnett &amp; Evans on treating your career like a design prototype.\n\n<b>Slide 1</b> — <i>Cover slide showing three book covers (So Good They Can&#x27;t Ignore You, The Happiness Curve, Designing Your Life) with title text overlay</i>\nIf You Don&#x27;t Know What Career To Pick\nread these 👉\nyour corporate unc\n(7 yrs in big tech)\n\n<b>Slide 2</b> — <i>Book cover for Cal Newport&#x27;s book on orange background with key lesson above</i>\n1. &quot;So Good They Can&#x27;t Ignore You&quot;\nSkill beats passion. Build rare abilities first, then leverage them.\n\nSO GOOD THEY CAN&#x27;T IGNORE YOU\nWhy Skills Trump Passion in the Quest for Work You Love\nCAL NEWPORT\n\n<b>Slide 3</b> — <i>Book cover for Jonathan Rauch&#x27;s book with white cover and U-shaped chart graphic</i>\n2. &quot;The Happiness Curve&quot;\nFeeling lost in your 20s is more common than you think. Don&#x27;t mistake uncertainty for failure.\n\nTHE HAPPINESS CURVE\nWHY LIFE GETS BETTER AFTER 50\nJONATHAN RAUCH\n\n<b>Slide 4</b> — <i>Photo of Mark Cuban speaking into a microphone on an orange background</i>\n3. &quot;Don&#x27;t Follow Your Passion&quot; by Mark Cuban\nPassion follows effort. Fall in love with the process, not the idea.\n\n<b>Slide 5</b> — <i>Book cover for Ikigai with cherry blossom branch on light blue background</i>\n4. &quot;Ikigai&quot;\nWhat do I love? What am I good at? What does the world need? What can I get paid for? Your best career is often where those overlap.\n\nIKIGAI\nThe Japanese Secret to a Long and Happy Life\nHÉCTOR GARCIA AND FRANCESC MIRALLES\n\n<b>Slide 6</b> — <i>Book cover for Designing Your Life with colorful dot pattern on light blue background</i>\n5. &quot;Designing Your Life&quot;\nTreat your career like a design problem. Prototype, test, iterate.\n\nBILL BURNETT &amp; DAVE EVANS\nDESIGNING YOUR LIFE\nHOW TO BUILD A WELL-LIVED, JOYFUL LIFE\n\n<b>Slide 7</b> — <i>CTA slide showing author&#x27;s Instagram profile card with follower count and call to action</i>\nproductmanagermike\nMichael Yitayew | tech | career\n156 posts  30.6K followers  202 following\nhelp you level up in product roles, tech, content\nproduct manager @google @amazon views=mine\nmichaelyitayew96@gmail.com\n\nComment &#x27;Career&#x27; to get all the links.\nSave this for your next career spiral.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-30T00:02:47"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/p/DaLVhNiiI7x/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Three Hobbies to Become Educated</b>\n<i>Three underrated hobbies — reading fiction, learning to read scientific papers, and studying real-world systems — build genuine intellectual depth.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Reading fiction trains your understanding of people, emotions, and decision-making — it isn&#x27;t just entertainment.\n• Learning to read scientific papers means quickly finding the main question and key result without getting lost in technical details.\n• Studying real-world systems (e.g. how prices are set, how elections are structured) means trying to understand the mechanics behind everyday phenomena.\n\n💾 <i>Concise, actionable list of three non-obvious habits that develop intellectual range beyond conventional studying.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DaLVhNiiI7x/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#self-education #habits #intellectual-growth</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Three Hobbies to Become Educated</b><br><i>Three underrated hobbies — reading fiction, learning to read scientific papers, and studying real-world systems — build genuine intellectual depth.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Reading fiction trains your understanding of people, emotions, and decision-making — it isn&#x27;t just entertainment.</li><li>Learning to read scientific papers means quickly finding the main question and key result without getting lost in technical details.</li><li>Studying real-world systems (e.g. how prices are set, how elections are structured) means trying to understand the mechanics behind everyday phenomena.</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Concise, actionable list of three non-obvious habits that develop intellectual range beyond conventional studying.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DaLVhNiiI7x/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#self-education #habits #intellectual-growth</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Three hobbies recommended for deep self-education: reading fiction (builds empathy and decision-making), learning to skim scientific papers (extract key findings fast), and studying everyday systems (understand mechanics behind prices, elections, etc.).\n\n<b>Slide 1</b> — <i>Cover slide with title text over a moody library interior.</i>\nhobbies to become immaculately well-educated\n\n<b>Slide 2</b> — <i>Slide presenting reading fiction as the first hobby, with a brief explanation of its cognitive benefits.</i>\nreading fiction books\n\nReading fiction isn&#x27;t just entertainment, it trains your understanding of people, emotions, and decision-making.\n\n<b>Slide 3</b> — <i>Slide presenting reading scientific papers as the second hobby, emphasizing efficient extraction over full comprehension.</i>\nlearning how to read scientific papers\n\nIt doesn&#x27;t mean understanding every word but learning how to quickly find the main question, the key result, etc. without getting lost in the technical details.\n\n<b>Slide 4</b> — <i>Slide presenting studying real-world systems as the third hobby, with concrete examples of economics and elections.</i>\nstudying real-world systems\n\nPick everyday systems like how prices are set, or how elections are structured, and try to understand the mechanics behind them.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-06-30T00:49:53"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DVgrVTxEdJP/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>TikTok First Instagram Cross-Platform Playbook</b>\n<i>“Look at it like if TikTok is the podcast, Instagram is the promo clip.”</i>\n\n🪜 <b>How to</b>\n1. Open TikTok, go to the search bar, and type &#x27;Creator Search Insights&#x27; — filter for topics labeled &#x27;trending&#x27; or with an up arrow, meaning the topic hasn&#x27;t reached its peak yet\n2. Create the video for TikTok first: make it longer, chatty, hot-take style, and less polished — this is the podcast version\n3. Repurpose that video for Instagram: trim to under 1 minute 30 seconds, make it more polished and concise — this is the promo clip version\n4. Add your other platform handle to your bio on each platform\n5. Use your captions and comments to actively bridge followers over to your other platform\n6. Post to Stories linking or referencing your other platform to close the loop and drive cross-platform growth\n\n<i>Useful for: Creators who want to grow simultaneously on TikTok and Instagram by using each platform&#x27;s algorithm strategically.</i>\n\n💾 <i>Gives a concrete, repeatable cross-platform repurposing workflow — use TikTok&#x27;s own search data to find pre-peak topics, produce once, distribute twice — with clear platform-specific formatting rules.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DVgrVTxEdJP/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#content-strategy #tiktok #instagram</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>TikTok First Instagram Cross-Platform Playbook</b><br><i>“Look at it like if TikTok is the podcast, Instagram is the promo clip.”</i><br>🪜 <b>How to</b><br><ol><li>Open TikTok, go to the search bar, and type &#x27;Creator Search Insights&#x27; — filter for topics labeled &#x27;trending&#x27; or with an up arrow, meaning the topic hasn&#x27;t reached its peak yet</li><li>Create the video for TikTok first: make it longer, chatty, hot-take style, and less polished — this is the podcast version</li><li>Repurpose that video for Instagram: trim to under 1 minute 30 seconds, make it more polished and concise — this is the promo clip version</li><li>Add your other platform handle to your bio on each platform</li><li>Use your captions and comments to actively bridge followers over to your other platform</li><li>Post to Stories linking or referencing your other platform to close the loop and drive cross-platform growth</li></ol><br><i>Useful for: Creators who want to grow simultaneously on TikTok and Instagram by using each platform&#x27;s algorithm strategically.</i><br>💾 <i>Gives a concrete, repeatable cross-platform repurposing workflow — use TikTok&#x27;s own search data to find pre-peak topics, produce once, distribute twice — with clear platform-specific formatting rules.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DVgrVTxEdJP/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#content-strategy #tiktok #instagram</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Find pre-peak trending topics in TikTok Creator Search Insights, produce a longer chatty video for TikTok, then trim and polish it into a sub-90-second Instagram Reel. Reinforce cross-platform presence through bios, captions, comments, and Stories so each platform feeds the other.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-01T02:53:17"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/p/DZvfYhHAVKP/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>5 Substacks That Made Smarter</b>\n\n📌 <b>Recommended</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://socialcontractwithjoewalsh.substack.com/\">The Social Contract with Joe Walsh</a> — Joe Walsh\n✅ <a href=\"https://jameschaiwriting.substack.com/p/10-lessons-how-government-really\">10 Lessons: How Government Really Works</a> — James Chai\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.jackhopkinsnow.com/\">Jack Hopkins Now</a> — Jack Hopkins\n✅ <a href=\"https://sundazing.substack.com/\">Sundazing</a> — Olivia\n✅ <a href=\"https://johnpaulbrammer.substack.com/p/how-i-learned-to-read-way-way-more\">How I Learned to Read Way, Way More</a> — John Paul Brammer\n\n💾 <i>Curated list of five Substack newsletters spanning political literacy, government dynamics, news anxiety, intellectual development, and reading — all with verified canonical links.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DZvfYhHAVKP/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#substack #politics #intellectual-life</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>5 Substacks That Made Smarter</b><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li>✅ <a href=\"https://socialcontractwithjoewalsh.substack.com/\">The Social Contract with Joe Walsh</a> — Joe Walsh</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://jameschaiwriting.substack.com/p/10-lessons-how-government-really\">10 Lessons: How Government Really Works</a> — James Chai</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.jackhopkinsnow.com/\">Jack Hopkins Now</a> — Jack Hopkins</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://sundazing.substack.com/\">Sundazing</a> — Olivia</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://johnpaulbrammer.substack.com/p/how-i-learned-to-read-way-way-more\">How I Learned to Read Way, Way More</a> — John Paul Brammer</li></ol><br>💾 <i>Curated list of five Substack newsletters spanning political literacy, government dynamics, news anxiety, intellectual development, and reading — all with verified canonical links.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DZvfYhHAVKP/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#substack #politics #intellectual-life</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>A carousel listing 5 Substacks: The Social Contract with Joe Walsh (policy vs. personality in political media), James Chai on how government really works, Jack Hopkins Now on crushing excessive political worry, Sundazing on building an intellectual life, and John Paul Brammer on reading way more.\n\n<b>Slide 1</b> — <i>Cover slide with photo of students gathered on US Capitol steps</i>\nthe substacks that made me smarter this week *for people who care about politics*\n\n<b>Slide 2</b> — <i>Article card for The Social Contract with Joe Walsh on coral-orange background, featuring a vintage interview photo</i>\nTHE SOCIAL CONTRACT WITH JOE WALSH\nWhen Was the Last Time Anyone Talked About Policy?\nJoe&#x27;s Midweek Meltdown\nPolicy. When was the last time you picked up a...\n\nhow political coverage has shifted from policy towards personalities, polls, projections and political mud slinging\n\n<b>Slide 3</b> — <i>Article card on sage-teal background with an illustrated painting of a passionate speaker in a chaotic legislative hall</i>\n10 Lessons: How Government Really Works\nWhy you need to waste enough time, sacrifice yourself, and make everything beautiful.\n\nthis is not your average government 101: how to understand the power dynamics that really shape our institutions\n\n<b>Slide 4</b> — <i>Article card for Jack Hopkins Now on olive-green background, featuring a silhouette figure at sunrise over a misty lake</i>\nJACK HOPKINS NOW\nHow to Crush Your Habit of Excessive Political Worry\nRead this if you&#x27;ve ever lost a night&#x27;s sleep over something a person 1,500 miles away said into a microphone.\n\nhere&#x27;s a few REAL, ACTIONABLE tips for anyone who needs to reframe their relationship to news and politics\ndoesn&#x27;t everyone need this right now?\n\n<b>Slide 5</b> — <i>Article card for Sundazing on pink background, showing a cozy aesthetic collage with laptops, books, and soft lighting labeled intellectual lifestyle</i>\nSUNDAZING\nhow to build an intellectual life\nget in loser, we&#x27;re feeding our brain\ni have always lived with a well-fed curiosity.\n\na roadmap &amp; useful tools for building intellect like it&#x27;s any other skill or hobby\n\n<b>Slide 6</b> — <i>Article card on teal background featuring a painterly still-life of antique books, globes, and framed artwork</i>\nHow I Learned to Read Way, Way More\nI had to rethink my relationship to attention\nYour paid support also supports Thriftbooks in...\n\nhow to become a better reader by tapping into pure attention, your flow state and deep curiosity</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-01T20:36:00"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DaRBm20hcna/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>10 ADHD Habits for Ambitious People</b>\n<i>Eight evidence-backed habits that reduce ADHD&#x27;s friction on focus, initiation, and follow-through.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Reduce cognitive load: phone out of the room, tabs closed, desk clean\n• Protect working memory: write down tasks immediately before they evaporate\n• Use implementation intentions: if situation X, then do Y (e.g., if it&#x27;s 9am, open laptop; if shoes are on, walk starts)\n• Externalize executive function: use calendars, timers, checklists, and reminders instead of relying on memory\n• Design for task initiation: pre-load the environment so the first step requires no willpower (clothes laid out, tabs preloaded, gym bag packed)\n• Use dopamine strategically: music, movement, sunlight, and quick wins — not doom scrolling or cheap reward loops\n• Use body doubling for attentional control: coffee shop, library, or a focus call where the room quietly pressures you to lock in\n• Practice metacognition: ask what state you&#x27;re in, what system is failing, and what cue would help you restart\n\n💾 <i>Concise, research-referenced breakdown of ADHD-specific productivity habits that goes beyond generic advice.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DaRBm20hcna/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#adhd #habits #focus</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>10 ADHD Habits for Ambitious People</b><br><i>Eight evidence-backed habits that reduce ADHD&#x27;s friction on focus, initiation, and follow-through.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Reduce cognitive load: phone out of the room, tabs closed, desk clean</li><li>Protect working memory: write down tasks immediately before they evaporate</li><li>Use implementation intentions: if situation X, then do Y (e.g., if it&#x27;s 9am, open laptop; if shoes are on, walk starts)</li><li>Externalize executive function: use calendars, timers, checklists, and reminders instead of relying on memory</li><li>Design for task initiation: pre-load the environment so the first step requires no willpower (clothes laid out, tabs preloaded, gym bag packed)</li><li>Use dopamine strategically: music, movement, sunlight, and quick wins — not doom scrolling or cheap reward loops</li><li>Use body doubling for attentional control: coffee shop, library, or a focus call where the room quietly pressures you to lock in</li><li>Practice metacognition: ask what state you&#x27;re in, what system is failing, and what cue would help you restart</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Concise, research-referenced breakdown of ADHD-specific productivity habits that goes beyond generic advice.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DaRBm20hcna/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#adhd #habits #focus</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Eight ADHD-tailored habits: reduce cognitive load, protect working memory, use if-then implementation intentions, externalize executive function, design for easy task initiation, use dopamine strategically, body double for focus, and practice metacognition. Closing line: build fences in the jungle, not a cure.\n\n10 out of 10 habits every ambitious person with ADHD should practice, but it gets increasingly more niche. Reduce cognitive load. Phone out the room, tabs closed, and desk clean because your brain can&#x27;t fight 19 stimuli and pretend it&#x27;s discipline. Protect your working memory. Write down the task before it evaporates. Because you and I know ADHD turns I&#x27;ll Remember That into historical fiction. Use implementation intentions. If situation X happens, then I&#x27;ll do Y behavior. If it&#x27;s 9am, then laptop open. If shoes are on, then walk starts. Externalize executive function. Calendars, timers, checklists, reminders. Barclays ADHD model points to behavioral inhibition and executive function. So stop trying to hold your life together with fives and short-term memory. Design for task initiation. Clothes laid out. Tabs preloaded. Gym backpack. Make the first step so easy that your brain has no time to appeal. Use dopamine strategically. Don&#x27;t doom scroll. Don&#x27;t use cheap reward loops. Music, movement, sunlight, and quick wins. Give your reward system something clean to treat. Body double for attentional control. Coffee shop, library, focus call. Anything where the room quietly pressures you to lock in. Finally, practice metacognition. Ask yourself, what state am I in? What system is failing? And what cue would help me restart? Remember, the goal isn&#x27;t to cure your monkey brain. It&#x27;s to build fences in the jungle.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-03T23:49:10"
  },
