  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYxGFapR0aJ/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Build Your LLM Second Brain</b>\n<i>Build an LLM-powered second brain that ingests your reading and automatically surfaces connections, contradictions, and through lines — improving retention via elaborative encoding.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Top founders and AI engineers use AI to enhance their thinking, not offload it\n• A second brain stores your reading; an AI agent then finds connections, contradictions, and through lines across all sources automatically\n• The system applies elaborative encoding automatically — linking new information to existing knowledge — which is why it also improves long-term retention\n• Setup takes about 10 minutes and requires zero technical skills; Claude can walk you through it based on Karpathy&#x27;s approach\n• Use cases include saving founder profiles and biographies and asking career advice questions against the full corpus\n\n💾 <i>Practical case for using AI as a thinking amplifier via a personal knowledge base, with a verified Karpathy-inspired setup path and the elaborative encoding rationale for why it works.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYxGFapR0aJ/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#second-brain #ai-tools #learning</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Build Your LLM Second Brain</b><br><i>Build an LLM-powered second brain that ingests your reading and automatically surfaces connections, contradictions, and through lines — improving retention via elaborative encoding.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Top founders and AI engineers use AI to enhance their thinking, not offload it</li><li>A second brain stores your reading; an AI agent then finds connections, contradictions, and through lines across all sources automatically</li><li>The system applies elaborative encoding automatically — linking new information to existing knowledge — which is why it also improves long-term retention</li><li>Setup takes about 10 minutes and requires zero technical skills; Claude can walk you through it based on Karpathy&#x27;s approach</li><li>Use cases include saving founder profiles and biographies and asking career advice questions against the full corpus</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Practical case for using AI as a thinking amplifier via a personal knowledge base, with a verified Karpathy-inspired setup path and the elaborative encoding rationale for why it works.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYxGFapR0aJ/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#second-brain #ai-tools #learning</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>An LLM second brain ingests your articles, book notes, and podcasts, then automatically surfaces connections and contradictions across them — applying elaborative encoding to improve retention. Setup takes about 10 minutes using Claude, no technical skills required, following Andrej Karpathy&#x27;s LLM Knowledge Base approach.\n\nI was recently at a dinner with some of the smartest people I know, billion-dollar founders, AI engineers, and they&#x27;re all using AI this one way. Most people use AI to avoid thinking, hence this AI brainwrap problem. But what I learned at this dinner is that these guys are using AI to enhance their own thinking. And here&#x27;s how it works. A second brain is essentially a collection of all the articles, podcasts, book notes, etc. that you read that you kind of store in your second brain. An AI agent reads all this, and instead of just summarizing it, it actually finds the connections, the contradictions, and other interesting kind of through lines through all of these different sources. I especially love this because if you&#x27;re like me and someone who reads a bunch of stuff but sometimes forgets, this also helps with elaborative encoding. It&#x27;s a method for improving your memory by linking new pieces of information to things that already exist in your memory. And the cool part about the second brain is that it does that automatically and helps you see connections that you might not otherwise have made yourself. Right now, I&#x27;m just using it for my personal readings, essays, etc. so I can retain information more, but there are so many other interesting use cases that people talked about at this dinner. If you comment below, I&#x27;ll send you the instructions I used. It&#x27;s pretty easy, and you&#x27;ll probably get through with Claude.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-04T13:50:42"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DaZ42VHz_NL/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Train Willpower Like a Muscle</b>\n<i>Willpower can be trained like a muscle — a review of 63 studies found physical exercise and mindfulness show the strongest effects.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• 4 training types studied: physical exercise, cognitive training, mindfulness, video games\n• Exercise and mindfulness showed the strongest effects on willpower\n• The more you train willpower, the more efficient it becomes\n• Electrically stimulating the anterior mid-cingulate cortex also appears to increase willpower (not a DIY method)\n\n💾 <i>Compact, source-backed neuroscience summary of willpower training with actionable method hierarchy (exercise &gt; mindfulness &gt; cognitive &gt; games).</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DaZ42VHz_NL/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#willpower #neuroscience #self-control</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Train Willpower Like a Muscle</b><br><i>Willpower can be trained like a muscle — a review of 63 studies found physical exercise and mindfulness show the strongest effects.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>4 training types studied: physical exercise, cognitive training, mindfulness, video games</li><li>Exercise and mindfulness showed the strongest effects on willpower</li><li>The more you train willpower, the more efficient it becomes</li><li>Electrically stimulating the anterior mid-cingulate cortex also appears to increase willpower (not a DIY method)</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Compact, source-backed neuroscience summary of willpower training with actionable method hierarchy (exercise &gt; mindfulness &gt; cognitive &gt; games).</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DaZ42VHz_NL/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#willpower #neuroscience #self-control</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>A neuroscience review of 63 studies found willpower behaves like a muscle and can be strengthened through training. Physical exercise and mindfulness had the strongest effects; cognitive training and video games also helped. Electrical stimulation of the anterior mid-cingulate cortex is another avenue under research.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-05T20:44:22"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DXpJk2pjKWo/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "“<b>I would rather fall short of a big goal and consider myself a failure than lower the goals to the level which I can accomplish all of them just so I can feel like a success.</b>”\n— Simon Sinek\n\n<i>Sinek argues that aiming high and falling short is more honorable than shrinking your goals just to guarantee a feeling of success.</i>\n\n💾 <i>A sharp reframe on failure — Sinek positions lowering your goals to avoid failure as a worse outcome than genuinely falling short of an ambitious one.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DXpJk2pjKWo/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#ambition #failure #mindset</i>",
    "markdown": "“<b>I would rather fall short of a big goal and consider myself a failure than lower the goals to the level which I can accomplish all of them just so I can feel like a success.</b>”<br>— Simon Sinek<br><i>Sinek argues that aiming high and falling short is more honorable than shrinking your goals just to guarantee a feeling of success.</i><br>💾 <i>A sharp reframe on failure — Sinek positions lowering your goals to avoid failure as a worse outcome than genuinely falling short of an ambitious one.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DXpJk2pjKWo/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#ambition #failure #mindset</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Sinek makes the case that real ambition requires accepting failure as a possibility; deliberately lowering goals just to feel successful is a form of self-deception.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-06T13:23:40"
