  "https://www.instagram.com/p/DaOaAxQkRE6/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Film a Month of B-Roll in One Day</b>\n\n🪜 <b>How to</b>\n1. Start with a plan: make a list of repeatable, useful shots you can film in one space (working at your desk, drinking coffee, reading or journaling, typing on a laptop, making tea, scrolling on your phone) — build a personal stock library from your real life.\n2. Shoot wide, medium, and tight: film every scene in 3 ways — close-ups (add detail and curiosity), mid shots (show emotion and action), wide shots (set the scene). Combine all 3 for smoother edits and better flow.\n3. Repeat the action: choose a simple action, break it into 3–5 steps, and reshoot each step from multiple angles (overhead, POV, low-angle) — this gives you enough footage to stitch a mini story together in post.\n4. Film your real life: turn everyday tasks into content — your routine (breakfast, journaling, workouts), your process (editing, brainstorming, behind the scenes), personal moments (books, meditation, slow mornings). Let people into your world, it builds trust fast.\n5. Don&#x27;t forget the stills: while the angle is set up, take an extra 60 seconds to set a self-timer and get a still of the same setup — keeps your carousels and thumbnails on-brand.\n6. Organize for reuse: create labeled folders on your laptop or phone, back up on external drives, reuse clips across reels, carousels, and shorts — consistency gets easier when you&#x27;ve already got the footage.\n\n<i>Useful for: Content creators who want to batch-shoot B-roll once and reuse clips for weeks instead of constantly reshooting.</i>\n\n💾 <i>A concrete 6-step system for batch-filming B-roll that reduces daily shooting overhead and builds a reusable personal stock library.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DaOaAxQkRE6/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#b-roll #content-creation #batch-filming</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Film a Month of B-Roll in One Day</b><br>🪜 <b>How to</b><br><ol><li>Start with a plan: make a list of repeatable, useful shots you can film in one space (working at your desk, drinking coffee, reading or journaling, typing on a laptop, making tea, scrolling on your phone) — build a personal stock library from your real life.</li><li>Shoot wide, medium, and tight: film every scene in 3 ways — close-ups (add detail and curiosity), mid shots (show emotion and action), wide shots (set the scene). Combine all 3 for smoother edits and better flow.</li><li>Repeat the action: choose a simple action, break it into 3–5 steps, and reshoot each step from multiple angles (overhead, POV, low-angle) — this gives you enough footage to stitch a mini story together in post.</li><li>Film your real life: turn everyday tasks into content — your routine (breakfast, journaling, workouts), your process (editing, brainstorming, behind the scenes), personal moments (books, meditation, slow mornings). Let people into your world, it builds trust fast.</li><li>Don&#x27;t forget the stills: while the angle is set up, take an extra 60 seconds to set a self-timer and get a still of the same setup — keeps your carousels and thumbnails on-brand.</li><li>Organize for reuse: create labeled folders on your laptop or phone, back up on external drives, reuse clips across reels, carousels, and shorts — consistency gets easier when you&#x27;ve already got the footage.</li></ol><br><i>Useful for: Content creators who want to batch-shoot B-roll once and reuse clips for weeks instead of constantly reshooting.</i><br>💾 <i>A concrete 6-step system for batch-filming B-roll that reduces daily shooting overhead and builds a reusable personal stock library.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DaOaAxQkRE6/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#b-roll #content-creation #batch-filming</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Plan repeatable shots, film each scene at three focal lengths, break actions into multi-angle steps, capture real-life moments, grab stills while the setup is ready, then organize clips into labeled folders for ongoing reuse.\n\n<b>Slide 1</b> — <i>Cover slide — man with camera bag at golden hour near the ocean, large title text over the photo.</i>\nHow To Film A Month&#x27;s Worth of B-Roll in One Day\nA SIMPLE WORKFLOW TO SAVE YOU HOURS EVERY WEEK →\nThis is Day 015/200 of helping you level up your content and personal brand\n\n<b>Slide 2</b> — <i>Close-up of hands on a laptop keyboard, checklist of everyday indoor shot ideas overlaid.</i>\n01 Start with a plan\nMake a list of repeatable, useful shots you can film in one space.\n✔ Working at your desk\n✔ Drinking coffee\n✔ Reading or journaling\n✔ Typing on a laptop\n✔ Making tea\n✔ Scrolling on your phone\nBuild a personal stock library from your real life.\n\n<b>Slide 3</b> — <i>Man standing on a beachside railing at dusk, text overlay explaining the three focal-length rule.</i>\n02 Shoot wide, medium, &amp; tight\nFilm every scene in 3 ways:\n→ Close-ups: Add detail and curiosity\n→ Mid shots: Show emotion and action\n→ Wide shots: Set the scene\nPro Tip: Combine all 3 for smoother edits and better flow.\n\n<b>Slide 4</b> — <i>POV shot of someone lying in grass looking up at tall palm trees in fog, instructional text overlay.</i>\n03 Repeat the action\nDon&#x27;t shoot everything from one angle. Break actions into steps, and reshoot each step from multiple angles.\n→ Choose a simple action: e.g. walking to your desk\n→ Break it into 3–5 steps: e.g. pull out laptop, set it down, open it, etc.\n→ Mix up shot types: e.g. Overhead, POV, low-angle\nThis gives you enough footage to stitch a mini story together in post.\n\n<b>Slide 5</b> — <i>Laptop, AirPods case, and a notebook with a stamp on a train seat, emergency exit sign visible through the window.</i>\n04 Film your real life\nTurn everyday tasks into content gold:\n→ Your routine (breakfast, journaling, workouts)\n→ Your process (editing, brainstorming, behind the scenes)\n→ Personal moments (books, meditation, slow mornings)\nLet people into your world, it builds trust fast.\n\n<b>Slide 6</b> — <i>Woman posing outside a Colombian café storefront at night, advice about shooting stills while already set up.</i>\n05 Don&#x27;t forget the stills\nYou already have the angle set up, so take an extra 60s to set a selftimer on your camera and get a still of the same set up.\n&quot;This keeps your carousels and thumbnails on-brand.&quot;\n\n<b>Slide 7</b> — <i>Mac Finder window showing categorized footage folders (Exports, Content Creation, Drone, Travel, Work/Office, etc.), file organization advice overlaid.</i>\n06 Organize for reuse\nBuild a content bank so you&#x27;re never starting from scratch.\n→ Create labeled folders on your laptop or phone\n→ Back it up on external drives\n→ Reuse clips across reels, carousels, and shorts\nConsistency gets easier when you&#x27;ve already got the footage.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-06T23:07:41"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/p/DZ-sfH2gWOx/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>33 Negative Hooks That Boost Reach</b>\n\n📌 <b>Recommended</b>\n<i>Not clear from the Reel.</i>\n\n💾 <i>A ready-to-use bank of 33 fill-in-the-blank negative hook templates, organized into five categories (stop, why, mistake, never, callout), that can be applied to any niche to stop the scroll.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DZ-sfH2gWOx/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#hooks #copywriting #content</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>33 Negative Hooks That Boost Reach</b><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li><i>Not clear from the Reel.</i></li></ol><br>💾 <i>A ready-to-use bank of 33 fill-in-the-blank negative hook templates, organized into five categories (stop, why, mistake, never, callout), that can be applied to any niche to stop the scroll.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DZ-sfH2gWOx/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#hooks #copywriting #content</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>33 negative hook templates in fill-in-the-blank format, organized by psychological angle — stop, why, mistake, never, and callout. Each hook uses bracketed placeholders like [action], [noun], and [dream result] to make them instantly adaptable to any niche or content type.\n\n<b>Slide 1</b> — <i>Cover slide with dark background, title in large bold white and orange type, subtitle in italic white text.</i>\n33 negative hooks that boost reach.\nNegativity stops the scroll. Here are 33 fill-in-the-blank hooks that quietly boost your reach.\n@marketing.shekhar\n\n<b>Slide 2</b> — <i>Slide listing 6 &#x27;stop&#x27; hooks numbered 01–06 on a dark background with orange numbered labels and highlighted placeholders.</i>\nSTART WITH STOP / 33 negative hooks\nThe stop list.\n01 Stop [action] if you actually want to [dream result].\n02 Here are [#] [noun] as a [title] I would never own.\n03 How to stop [action] for good.\n04 If you don&#x27;t have [item/action], do [item/action].\n05 If you [action] like this, then you&#x27;re doing it wrong.\n06 Don&#x27;t make the mistake of [action], [action], [action].\n@marketing.shekhar 02/08\n\n<b>Slide 3</b> — <i>Slide listing 6 &#x27;why&#x27; hooks numbered 07–12, same dark-background style with orange highlighted placeholders.</i>\nWHY YOU&#x27;RE INVISIBLE / 33 negative hooks\nThe why hooks.\n07 Why does nobody talk about the cost of [action].\n08 [#] things you should never put in your [noun].\n09 Don&#x27;t start doing [action] until you learn how to do this.\n10 [#] things you should never put in the [noun].\n11 It&#x27;s time to throw away your [item]. You don&#x27;t need it.\n12 [#] days/months/years ago I quit [habit].\n@marketing.shekhar 03/08\n\n<b>Slide 4</b> — <i>Slide listing 6 &#x27;mistake&#x27; hooks numbered 13–18, dark background, orange highlights.</i>\nBLEEDING YOUR REACH / 33 negative hooks\nThe mistake hooks.\n13 Never [action] first and then [action].\n14 Stop buying your [noun] like this, and start buying them like this.\n15 [#] years as a [occupation], and you still don&#x27;t believe me.\n16 If you want to [dream result], [action] is the worst thing you can do.\n17 Here&#x27;s why I never use a [noun].\n18 If you&#x27;ve ever secretly [action] and hope no one noticed this is for you.\n@marketing.shekhar 04/08\n\n<b>Slide 5</b> — <i>Slide listing 6 &#x27;never&#x27; hooks numbered 19–24, dark background, orange highlights.</i>\nNOBODY TELLS YOU / 33 negative hooks\nThe never hooks.\n19 If I were in my [age range] here is exactly how I would avoid [bad result].\n20 Ranking the most popular [noun] from worst to best.\n21 The worst [noun] that you will regret buying for your [noun] is right here.\n22 I am going to show you how to [verb] your [noun] so they don&#x27;t [pain point].\n23 They said, &quot;[famous cliché or quote]&quot; That&#x27;s a lie.\n24 My [noun] never [pain point] no matter what I do.\n@marketing.shekhar 05/08\n\n<b>Slide 6</b> — <i>Slide listing 6 &#x27;callout&#x27; hooks numbered 25–30, dark background, orange highlights.</i>\nHARD TRUTHS / 33 negative hooks\nThe callout hooks.\n25 To everyone just doing [#] [action]. Stop that.\n26 Your life is boring because you don&#x27;t [action].\n27 There&#x27;s probably a reason why most people don&#x27;t [action].\n28 They say you can&#x27;t [result] with [noun] but they&#x27;re wrong.\n29 [noun] used to have [trait], now they don&#x27;t.\n30 If you don&#x27;t have [thing], do [thing] instead.\n@marketing.shekhar 06/08\n\n<b>Slide 7</b> — <i>Slide highlighting the 3 sharpest hooks (31–33) with gold left-border accents, dark background.</i>\nSAVE THESE 3 / the sharpest of the 33\n31 It&#x27;s time to throw away your [item], you don&#x27;t need it anymore.\n32 You wouldn&#x27;t get [bad result] if you just [action].\n33 They say you can&#x27;t [result] with [noun]. They&#x27;re wrong.\n@marketing.shekhar 07/08\n\n<b>Slide 8</b> — <i>CTA slide offering a 1,000 viral hooks library via DM, with a large orange follow button at the bottom.</i>\nFREE SWIPE FILE\nWant all 1,000?\n1,000 VIRAL HOOKS LIBRARY\nComment HOOKS and we&#x27;ll DM you the full library, sorted by goal, fill-in-the-blank ready, so you never stare at a blank caption again.\nFollow @marketing.shekhar →</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-08T01:22:18"
  },
