  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Da3tRpKhhWX/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>5-Part Video Script Framework</b>\n<i>“I&#x27;m going to walk you through probably the greatest script writing technique.”</i>\n\n🪜 <b>How to</b>\n1. Hook — pattern interrupt; sole job is to stop the scroll.\n2. Super hook — drive the pain point or dream outcome to bridge viewers to the climax; invoke curiosity without resolving it yet.\n3. Thesis — one sentence: the belief system or core claim; pop the first layer of curiosity and give the answer to the opening question.\n4. Illustration — example, metaphor, story, or statistic to reinforce the thesis; embed a rehook before it (&#x27;I&#x27;m going to teach you how to do this in a second, but first…&#x27;) to sustain watch time.\n5. Application — two or three steps, a framework, or a one-step takeaway; the practical closing deliverable.\n\n<i>Useful for: Video creators who want a repeatable structure for scripting reels and short-form content.</i>\n\n💾 <i>Concise breakdown of a 5-part script structure with the reasoning behind each beat, useful as a reusable scripting checklist.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/Da3tRpKhhWX/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#scripting #content-creation #short-form-video</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>5-Part Video Script Framework</b><br><i>“I&#x27;m going to walk you through probably the greatest script writing technique.”</i><br>🪜 <b>How to</b><br><ol><li>Hook — pattern interrupt; sole job is to stop the scroll.</li><li>Super hook — drive the pain point or dream outcome to bridge viewers to the climax; invoke curiosity without resolving it yet.</li><li>Thesis — one sentence: the belief system or core claim; pop the first layer of curiosity and give the answer to the opening question.</li><li>Illustration — example, metaphor, story, or statistic to reinforce the thesis; embed a rehook before it (&#x27;I&#x27;m going to teach you how to do this in a second, but first…&#x27;) to sustain watch time.</li><li>Application — two or three steps, a framework, or a one-step takeaway; the practical closing deliverable.</li></ol><br><i>Useful for: Video creators who want a repeatable structure for scripting reels and short-form content.</i><br>💾 <i>Concise breakdown of a 5-part script structure with the reasoning behind each beat, useful as a reusable scripting checklist.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/Da3tRpKhhWX/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#scripting #content-creation #short-form-video</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>A tutorial on a 5-step script structure designed to maximize retention: start with a pattern-interrupt hook, layer on a super hook that builds pain or desire without resolving it, pop curiosity with a one-sentence thesis, reinforce with an illustration preceded by a rehook, and close with actionable application steps.\n\nI&#x27;m going to walk you through probably the greatest script writing technique. It&#x27;s not the only one. And to be very clear, whenever you are writing scripts or thinking about yapping and structuring through bullet points, same thing to me, you want to have diversity. So you should not fall back on this all the time. This is a simple, basic, but extremely effective way to do it. So number one, we have the hook. This whole point is to pattern interrupt. I don&#x27;t care. Call it your ICP, pattern interrupt. Number two is a super hook, which is driving the pain point or the dream outcome that&#x27;s going to bridge people to the climax of the video. And the whole point is we have, we have invoked curiosity with the hook and we have edged them. We have not given them the curiosity or the pop of the curiosity through the super hook. So we&#x27;re, we&#x27;re ramping up watch time. Then we explain in one sentence, the thesis, the belief system, we pop the curiosity, we give them the information, the answer to the first question they were having, but then we immediately go into an illustration, which is an example, a metaphor, a story, a statistic. And in between there, we add a rehook that says, and I&#x27;m going to teach you how to do this tactically here in a second. So we explain, we say, I&#x27;m going to teach you how to do this in a second, but first I want to tell you a story or, but first I want to tell you a statistic, but first I want to tell you a quote, but first I want to tell you X, Y, Z. So then we give them the illustration to help re-explain, reinforce the topic that we started. And then we give them the application at the bottom. That&#x27;s the last thing, two steps, three steps, a one step takeaway. Maybe it&#x27;s a framework. Maybe it&#x27;s some secret sauce, whatever it is. I&#x27;ll talk a little bit. Paid on Okay, let&#x27;s move. All right, let&#x27;s move. Okay, so go, mam.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-17T15:29:15"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbA7dKVx4fC/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>LinkedIn Growth Fundamentals Checklist</b>\n<i>Consistent LinkedIn reach comes from posting frequently, engaging outbound daily, and reviewing what works — no hacks or engagement pods needed.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Post 3–5 times per week\n• Use more founder pictures when relevant\n• Write more things that AI cannot write\n• Leave 15 outbound comments per day\n• Send 20 outbound connections per day\n• Review content weekly: track which angles, formats, hooks, and media are working\n\n💾 <i>Practical, numbers-backed LinkedIn growth checklist from an agency that ghostwrites for 40+ AI startup founders.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbA7dKVx4fC/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#linkedin #content-strategy #b2b</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>LinkedIn Growth Fundamentals Checklist</b><br><i>Consistent LinkedIn reach comes from posting frequently, engaging outbound daily, and reviewing what works — no hacks or engagement pods needed.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Post 3–5 times per week</li><li>Use more founder pictures when relevant</li><li>Write more things that AI cannot write</li><li>Leave 15 outbound comments per day</li><li>Send 20 outbound connections per day</li><li>Review content weekly: track which angles, formats, hooks, and media are working</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Practical, numbers-backed LinkedIn growth checklist from an agency that ghostwrites for 40+ AI startup founders.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbA7dKVx4fC/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#linkedin #content-strategy #b2b</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>A tactical LinkedIn checklist — post 3–5x/week, use the tweet screenshot method and newsjacking, include founder photos, write AI-proof content, leave 15 comments and send 20 connections daily, review content weekly.\n\nStart posting three to five times per week. Use the tweet screenshot method. Use the newsjacking method. Use more founder pictures when relevant. Write more things that AI cannot write. Leave 15 outbound comments per day. Send 20 outbound connections per day. Review content weekly to see what angles, formats, hooks, and media are working. And follow me for more.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-20T20:25:31"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/p/Da1EKIODuqa/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>5 Critical AI Research Papers</b>\n\n📌 <b>Recommended</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://hbr.org/2026/02/ai-doesnt-reduce-work-it-intensifies-it\">AI Doesn&#x27;t Reduce Work—It Intensifies It</a> — Aruna Ranganathan and Xingqi Maggie Ye\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aec8352\">Sycophantic AI decreases prosocial intentions and promotes dependence</a> — Myra Cheng, Cinoo Lee, Pranav Khadpe, Sunny Yu, Dyllan Han, Dan Jurafsky\n✅ <a href=\"https://osf.io/preprints/psyarxiv/yk25n_v1\">Thinking—Fast, Slow, and Artificial: How AI is Reshaping Human Reasoning and the Rise of Cognitive Surrender</a> — Shaw and Nave\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-01100-y\">Scientists invented a fake disease. AI told people it was real</a> — Chris Stokel-Walker\n✅ <a href=\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/00222429251314491\">Lower Artificial Intelligence Literacy Predicts Greater AI Receptivity</a> — Stephanie M. Tully, Chiara Longoni, Gil Appel\n\n💾 <i>Concise access to five verified research papers that challenge common AI productivity and safety assumptions with empirical evidence.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/Da1EKIODuqa/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#ai-literacy #ai-research #critical-ai</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>5 Critical AI Research Papers</b><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li>✅ <a href=\"https://hbr.org/2026/02/ai-doesnt-reduce-work-it-intensifies-it\">AI Doesn&#x27;t Reduce Work—It Intensifies It</a> — Aruna Ranganathan and Xingqi Maggie Ye</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aec8352\">Sycophantic AI decreases prosocial intentions and promotes dependence</a> — Myra Cheng, Cinoo Lee, Pranav Khadpe, Sunny Yu, Dyllan Han, Dan Jurafsky</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://osf.io/preprints/psyarxiv/yk25n_v1\">Thinking—Fast, Slow, and Artificial: How AI is Reshaping Human Reasoning and the Rise of Cognitive Surrender</a> — Shaw and Nave</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-01100-y\">Scientists invented a fake disease. AI told people it was real</a> — Chris Stokel-Walker</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/00222429251314491\">Lower Artificial Intelligence Literacy Predicts Greater AI Receptivity</a> — Stephanie M. Tully, Chiara Longoni, Gil Appel</li></ol><br>💾 <i>Concise access to five verified research papers that challenge common AI productivity and safety assumptions with empirical evidence.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/Da1EKIODuqa/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#ai-literacy #ai-research #critical-ai</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Carousel of five peer-reviewed papers: AI intensifies rather than reduces workload (HBR); sycophantic AI reduces prosocial behavior and promotes dependence (Science); people follow wrong AI answers ~80% of the time (Wharton); AI chatbots confirmed a fake disease invented by researchers (Nature); lower AI literacy predicts higher AI receptivity (SAGE).\n\n<b>Slide 1</b> — <i>Cover slide: creator mirror selfie in floral dress with title and tagline.</i>\n@CAHDORIA\n5 AI research papers I read this week, and you should too\nby One of 100 Most Brilliant Women in AI Ethics\nTHE BAD, THE UGLY, AND THE DANGEROUS TRUTH ABOUT AI.\n\n<b>Slide 2</b> — <i>Slide 1: HBR article card overlaid on outdoor selfie with annotated bullet-point notes.</i>\n@CAHDORIA\nAI And Machine Learning\nAI Doesn&#x27;t Reduce Work—It Intensifies It\nby Aruna Ranganathan and Xingqi Maggie Ye\nFebruary 9, 2026\nHARVARD BUSINESS REVIEW\nNotes:\n• an 8-month study of ~200 tech employees found that instead of saving time, AI made them work more\n• workers took on extra tasks, blurred work and personal time, and juggled multiple AI threads at once\n• the result was burnout, fatigue, and worse decisions, not the promised productivity boost\n\n<b>Slide 3</b> — <i>Slide 2: Science journal paper header overlaid on mirror selfie with annotated notes.</i>\n@CAHDORIA\nSycophantic AI decreases prosocial intentions and promotes dependence\nMYRA CHENG, CINOO LEE, PRANAV KHADPE, SUNNY YU, DYLLAN HAN, AND DAN JURAFSKY\nSCIENCE · 26 Mar 2026 · Vol 391, Issue 6792 · DOI: 10.1126/science.aec8352\nNotes:\n• across 11 top AI models, AI agrees with users 49% more often than humans do, EVEN when users described illegal or harmful actions\n• a single chat with a sycophantic AI made people less willing to apologize and more sure they were right [!!!!]\n• and YET, people preferred and trusted the sycophantic models more\n\n<b>Slide 4</b> — <i>Slide 3: Wharton preprint header on cowhide rug background with dog and annotated notes.</i>\n@CAHDORIA\nThinking—Fast, Slow, and Artificial: How AI is Reshaping Human Reasoning and the Rise of Cognitive Surrender\nhttps://doi.org/10.31234/osf.io/yk25n_v1\nThe Wharton School Research Paper\n10/10\nNotes:\n• people followed the wrong AI answers roughly 80% of the time\n• with a &quot;correct AI&quot;, people got way more answers right; but with a &quot;wrong AI&quot;, they got more answers wrong than people working with no AI at all\n• people felt more confident in their answers after using AI, EVEN when the AI had gotten them wrong\n\n<b>Slide 5</b> — <i>Slide 4: Nature news feature header on outdoor puddle background with dog and annotated notes.</i>\n@CAHDORIA\nNEWS FEATURE | 07 April 2026\nScientists invented a fake disease. AI told people it was real\nBixonimania doesn&#x27;t exist except in a clutch of obviously bogus academic papers. So why did AI chatbots warn people about this fictional illness?\nBy Chris Stokel-Walker\nNotes:\n• researchers invented &quot;bixonimania&quot; and uploaded fake preprints\n• within weeks, ChatGPT, Gemini, Copilot, and Perplexity were telling users the disease was real\n• the fake disease got cited in a real peer-reviewed paper (later retracted) as a real disease\n\n<b>Slide 6</b> — <i>Slide 5: SAGE journal paper header with dog on fluffy rug and annotated notes.</i>\n@CAHDORIA\nLower Artificial Intelligence Literacy Predicts Greater AI Receptivity\nStephanie M. Tully, Chiara Longoni, and Gil Appel\nVolume 89, Issue 5 | https://doi.org/10.1177/00222429251314491\nNotes:\n• across 4 surveys + 6 studies, the LESS people know about AI, the MORE they want to use it\n• the more you actually learn about AI, the less it dazzles you, and the better you can use it safely\nthis is exactly why critical AI literacy matters so much!\n\n<b>Slide 7</b> — <i>Slide 6: Creator bio and follow CTA, mirror selfie in floral dress.</i>\nFOLLOW ME FOR MORE!\nHi, I&#x27;m Catha! I&#x27;m an AI ethicist, named one of the 100 Most Brilliant Women in AI Ethics. I help thousands of people understand and protect themselves from AI. My goal? To show that learning about AI doesn&#x27;t have to be boring: it can be useful, engaging, and cool! Follow along, and let&#x27;s dive in!\nComment &quot;PAPERS&quot; and I will send you the links!\n@CAHDORIA\n\n<b>Slide 8</b> — <i>Slide 7: Dark collage of tech founders and vintage tech interfaces with bold yellow closing message.</i>\nCRITICAL AI LITERACY\nFOLLOW FOR MORE TIPS\nPOWER HATES A READER.\nSHARE THESE AI RESEARCH PAPERS WITH EVERYONE YOU KNOW. CRITICAL AI LITERACY WILL HELP THEM NOT BE DAZZLED BY THE FAKE PROMISES OF AI.\n(DON&#x27;T FORGET TO FOLLOW FOR MORE!)\n@CAHDORIA\nSAVE</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-20T22:05:36"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbCbR6dhfjv/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>6 Habits for Better Storytelling</b>\n<i>Build storytelling instincts through six daily micro-habits that train observation, memory, improvisation, and on-camera delivery.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Screenshot any text that made you feel something into one album — your storytelling bank so you never run out of material\n• Write down every person you meet and occasionally narrate their story to yourself, practicing turning real people into stories repeatedly\n• Photograph specific small details (a design on a building, a crack on the sidewalk) — not wide shots — to train your eye for the details that make stories come alive\n• Pick a random word each day and tell yourself a one-minute story about it so you can turn anything into a story on the spot\n• Flip your phone face down and listen to a video of yourself to analyze your tone, rhythm, and inflection\n• Watch the same video on mute to study your facial expressions and body language separately\n\n💾 <i>Six concrete, non-obvious habits that build storytelling instinct across observation, memory, improvisation, and delivery — actionable without any tools.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbCbR6dhfjv/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#storytelling #habits #creativity</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>6 Habits for Better Storytelling</b><br><i>Build storytelling instincts through six daily micro-habits that train observation, memory, improvisation, and on-camera delivery.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Screenshot any text that made you feel something into one album — your storytelling bank so you never run out of material</li><li>Write down every person you meet and occasionally narrate their story to yourself, practicing turning real people into stories repeatedly</li><li>Photograph specific small details (a design on a building, a crack on the sidewalk) — not wide shots — to train your eye for the details that make stories come alive</li><li>Pick a random word each day and tell yourself a one-minute story about it so you can turn anything into a story on the spot</li><li>Flip your phone face down and listen to a video of yourself to analyze your tone, rhythm, and inflection</li><li>Watch the same video on mute to study your facial expressions and body language separately</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Six concrete, non-obvious habits that build storytelling instinct across observation, memory, improvisation, and delivery — actionable without any tools.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbCbR6dhfjv/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#storytelling #habits #creativity</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Six habits: build a screenshot storytelling bank, narrate people you meet to yourself, photograph small details, do daily one-minute random-word stories, listen back to your videos for tone and rhythm, then watch them muted for body language.\n\n10 out of 10 habits to be a disgustingly good storyteller, but they become increasingly more niche. Screenshot any text that made you feel something and drop into one album. This becomes your storytelling bank so you never run out of stuff to tell. Write down the name of everyone you meet and occasionally tell yourself their story. You&#x27;re practicing turning a real person into a story over and over, so by the time that you sit down to tell one, you&#x27;ve done it already a hundred times. Take photos of specific little things, not just the wide shot. A specific design on a building, a crack on the sidewalk. You&#x27;re training your eye to notice the small details and those small details are what make a story come to life. Give yourself a random word and tell yourself a story about it for one minute long every day. You get good at turning anything into a story on the spot so that you&#x27;re never stuck. Flip your phone face down and listen to your video back. Analyze your tone, rhythm, and inflection to see where you can improve. Then take the same video and now watch it on mute so that you can pay attention to your facial reactions and your body language.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-20T22:06:45"
  },
