  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbESOapo-p3/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Storytelling Tips From a Director</b>\n<i>Great social media storytelling uses a three-part structure — hook, story, takeaway — written like a text to a friend, not a polished script.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Storytelling isn&#x27;t about being the best writer — it&#x27;s about making people feel something\n• Develop your story before editing; record your voiceover first — it sets the foundation for everything\n• Think of your script as a diary entry, not a script\n• AI can organize your thoughts but can&#x27;t tell your story — the why, the feelings, the meaning are yours alone\n• Your authenticity is the one thing no one else can copy\n• Three-part story structure: Hook (what is my story about?), Story (what am I learning or what&#x27;s challenging me?), Takeaway (what am I an expert in or how can someone reflect on this?)\n• Write like you&#x27;re texting a friend or leaving a voice note\n• Talk about how you&#x27;re feeling, not just what&#x27;s happening\n\n💾 <i>Practical, experience-backed framework for authentic short-form video storytelling, distilled from commercial directing work.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbESOapo-p3/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#storytelling #content-creation #authenticity</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Storytelling Tips From a Director</b><br><i>Great social media storytelling uses a three-part structure — hook, story, takeaway — written like a text to a friend, not a polished script.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Storytelling isn&#x27;t about being the best writer — it&#x27;s about making people feel something</li><li>Develop your story before editing; record your voiceover first — it sets the foundation for everything</li><li>Think of your script as a diary entry, not a script</li><li>AI can organize your thoughts but can&#x27;t tell your story — the why, the feelings, the meaning are yours alone</li><li>Your authenticity is the one thing no one else can copy</li><li>Three-part story structure: Hook (what is my story about?), Story (what am I learning or what&#x27;s challenging me?), Takeaway (what am I an expert in or how can someone reflect on this?)</li><li>Write like you&#x27;re texting a friend or leaving a voice note</li><li>Talk about how you&#x27;re feeling, not just what&#x27;s happening</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Practical, experience-backed framework for authentic short-form video storytelling, distilled from commercial directing work.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbESOapo-p3/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#storytelling #content-creation #authenticity</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>A commercial director breaks down how to tell better stories on social media: treat your script as a diary entry, record your voiceover before editing, focus on feelings not just events, and use a three-part structure (hook → story → takeaway) written like a text to a friend.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-23T16:44:08"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZKw1Rnh0UH/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Travel Like an Anthropologist</b>\n<i>Shift travel focus from consuming landmarks to understanding the human stories and cultural patterns behind a place.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Spend time in ordinary places — grocery stores, public parks, local bookstores, public transit — where a culture&#x27;s default settings reveal themselves\n• Create a small daily ritual (same café, same market stall) and notice how your sense of place deepens over time\n• Observe how daily life unfolds: how people greet each other, conversations happening around you\n• Keep a small notebook and write down things that surprise, confuse, or challenge your assumptions as they happen\n• Travel with humility — ask before photographing, respect boundaries and cultural norms, and don&#x27;t treat traditions or community spaces as tourist attractions\n• Reframe from &#x27;what should I see?&#x27; to &#x27;what can this place teach me?&#x27;\n\n💾 <i>A concrete reframe for travel that turns sightseeing into genuine cultural observation and learning.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZKw1Rnh0UH/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#travel #anthropology #mindset</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Travel Like an Anthropologist</b><br><i>Shift travel focus from consuming landmarks to understanding the human stories and cultural patterns behind a place.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Spend time in ordinary places — grocery stores, public parks, local bookstores, public transit — where a culture&#x27;s default settings reveal themselves</li><li>Create a small daily ritual (same café, same market stall) and notice how your sense of place deepens over time</li><li>Observe how daily life unfolds: how people greet each other, conversations happening around you</li><li>Keep a small notebook and write down things that surprise, confuse, or challenge your assumptions as they happen</li><li>Travel with humility — ask before photographing, respect boundaries and cultural norms, and don&#x27;t treat traditions or community spaces as tourist attractions</li><li>Reframe from &#x27;what should I see?&#x27; to &#x27;what can this place teach me?&#x27;</li></ul><br>💾 <i>A concrete reframe for travel that turns sightseeing into genuine cultural observation and learning.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZKw1Rnh0UH/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#travel #anthropology #mindset</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Instead of rushing between attractions, spend time in ordinary places, create small daily rituals, observe social norms, keep a notebook of surprising moments, and travel with humility — asking what a place can teach you rather than what to check off a list.\n\nThe next time you visit a new city, try exploring it like an anthropologist. Most tourists focus on the landmarks, but anthropologists focus on people because they are primarily focused on understanding how a society works, what people value, and how culture shows up in everyday life. That means that in order to travel like an anthropologist, you must shift your focus from consuming sites to understanding the human stories behind a place. Instead of rushing between attractions, spend time in ordinary places, like go to a grocery store or sit in a public park, browse a local bookstore, ride public transit. These are the places where a culture&#x27;s default settings tend to start to reveal themselves. You could visit the same cafe every morning or order fruit from the same market stall. Create a little daily ritual and see how your experience in the place changes. In terms of observing, I think you want to prioritize paying attention attention to how daily life unfolds. So you&#x27;re going to want to notice things like how people greet each other and maybe eavesdrop to hear some of the conversations that are happening around you. And definitely keep notes. So keep a small notebook with you so that you can write down things that surprise you or confuse you or moments that challenged your assumptions as they happen. And most importantly, travel with humility. Remember, you are a guest. So ask before taking photographs, respect boundaries and cultural norms, and accept that traditions, festivals or community spaces do not exist solely for your consumption. Reframe your travels from just being about what should I see and check off this list and instead start thinking about, okay, what can this place teach me? That is when you move from being a tourist to being a student of the world.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-23T18:27:38"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZS0gBTPeGH/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "“<b>In the Bay, your net worth is less interesting than the problem you&#x27;re solving.</b>”\n— sorhan.hq\n\n<i>Each major city runs a different status game — wealth in New York, legacy and institutional proximity in London, what you&#x27;re building in San Francisco.</i>\n💡 <b>The city you choose selects the status game you play — pick the one that rewards what you actually do.</b>\n\n💾 <i>Reframes city choice as a status-game selection, useful for founders and career builders deciding where to operate.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZS0gBTPeGH/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#status #cities #founder</i>",
    "markdown": "“<b>In the Bay, your net worth is less interesting than the problem you&#x27;re solving.</b>”<br>— sorhan.hq<br><i>Each major city runs a different status game — wealth in New York, legacy and institutional proximity in London, what you&#x27;re building in San Francisco.</i><br>💡 <b>The city you choose selects the status game you play — pick the one that rewards what you actually do.</b><br>💾 <i>Reframes city choice as a status-game selection, useful for founders and career builders deciding where to operate.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZS0gBTPeGH/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#status #cities #founder</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Each city runs a distinct status game. New York rewards wealth, London rewards closeness to legacy institutions and old networks, San Francisco rewards what you&#x27;re building (money and class are irrelevant there), Miami rewards money and visibility, Boston rewards academic achievement, and LA rewards who you know. The core insight: the Bay Area is unusual because technical ambition and the problem you&#x27;re solving matter more than net worth.\n\nwhat defines power in different cities. In York, what defines power is how wealthy you are. The richer you are, the more powerful you are. In London, money doesn&#x27;t really matter. If Bill Gates was to go to London, he&#x27;d be considered upper middle class. What defines power in London is how close you are, how close you are in relation to the queen. But in San Francisco, what&#x27;s interesting is, money doesn&#x27;t really matter, everyone has it. Class doesn&#x27;t matter at all, it&#x27;s an aristocratic place. Culture doesn&#x27;t matter, no one can look at how people dress and whatnot. What does matter is what you&#x27;re building. Are you building the future? And so therefore, how powerful you are in a city, and how much status you have is, depending on whether they think you&#x27;re building something cool, you&#x27;re building the future. And in Miami, if you&#x27;re a gangster rapper. Miami is money, LA is who you know. Boston is academic. Boston is academics.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-23T23:50:43"
  },
  "https://vt.tiktok.com/ZSXWHsfYP/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Why Top Education Can&#x27;t Be Copied</b>\n<i>Top-ranked education systems cannot be simply imported into other countries because the cultural values around effort, intelligence, and teaching are impossible to transplant.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Western cultures tend to view academic ability as innate (&#x27;I&#x27;m just not a maths person&#x27;); many Asian cultures genuinely believe success is merely the result of effort and persistence.\n• England&#x27;s Shanghai Maths programme trained teachers in new methods but couldn&#x27;t import the culture of growth mindset that makes those methods effective.\n• When given an unsolvable problem, Japanese students grew more motivated to try harder; Canadian students assumed they were the problem and didn&#x27;t want to continue.\n• How teachers are paid and respected in society (Singapore vs. UK) is a contextual factor just as important as pedagogical method.\n\n💾 <i>Makes the case with concrete research that culture — not method — is the real lever in education, reframing how to think about any institutional change.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://vt.tiktok.com/ZSXWHsfYP/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#education #culture #growth-mindset</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Why Top Education Can&#x27;t Be Copied</b><br><i>Top-ranked education systems cannot be simply imported into other countries because the cultural values around effort, intelligence, and teaching are impossible to transplant.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Western cultures tend to view academic ability as innate (&#x27;I&#x27;m just not a maths person&#x27;); many Asian cultures genuinely believe success is merely the result of effort and persistence.</li><li>England&#x27;s Shanghai Maths programme trained teachers in new methods but couldn&#x27;t import the culture of growth mindset that makes those methods effective.</li><li>When given an unsolvable problem, Japanese students grew more motivated to try harder; Canadian students assumed they were the problem and didn&#x27;t want to continue.</li><li>How teachers are paid and respected in society (Singapore vs. UK) is a contextual factor just as important as pedagogical method.</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Makes the case with concrete research that culture — not method — is the real lever in education, reframing how to think about any institutional change.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://vt.tiktok.com/ZSXWHsfYP/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#education #culture #growth-mindset</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Even the best pedagogical methods fail without the cultural foundation that makes them work. England&#x27;s Shanghai Maths experiment shows you can train teachers in new methods but can&#x27;t import a growth mindset culture. A 2001 Heine et al. study of Japanese vs. Canadian students illustrates the gap: Japanese students responded to failure with increased persistence; Canadians shut down. Teacher pay and social prestige are additional contextual factors that determine whether methods succeed.</details>",
    "platform": "TikTok",
    "ts": "2026-07-24T00:47:24"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbH1rrJAykC/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "“<b>it is so deeply important that you understand how ai works even if you don&#x27;t like it and actually it is more important that you understand how it works if you don&#x27;t like it</b>”\n— carol.yaps\n\n<i>Understanding AI technically is no longer optional — policy, taxation, and cultural conversations all require it, and non-technical people must be part of those discussions.</i>\n💡 <b>You cannot meaningfully participate in AI policy debates without understanding the technical mechanics underneath them.</b>\n\n💾 <i>Concrete, technically grounded argument for why AI literacy is now a prerequisite for civic and policy participation, with a specific fiscal scenario most people haven&#x27;t thought through.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbH1rrJAykC/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#ai-policy #ai-literacy #taxation</i>",
    "markdown": "“<b>it is so deeply important that you understand how ai works even if you don&#x27;t like it and actually it is more important that you understand how it works if you don&#x27;t like it</b>”<br>— carol.yaps<br><i>Understanding AI technically is no longer optional — policy, taxation, and cultural conversations all require it, and non-technical people must be part of those discussions.</i><br>💡 <b>You cannot meaningfully participate in AI policy debates without understanding the technical mechanics underneath them.</b><br>💾 <i>Concrete, technically grounded argument for why AI literacy is now a prerequisite for civic and policy participation, with a specific fiscal scenario most people haven&#x27;t thought through.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbH1rrJAykC/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#ai-policy #ai-literacy #taxation</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Opinion reel arguing AI literacy is now civic literacy. Uses the &#x27;AI agent taxation&#x27; debate as a case study: AI replacing workers could gut income tax revenue, proposed agent-tax solutions face technical evasion problems (API rerouting, on-device models), and these discussions require technical fluency to participate in meaningfully.\n\nit is so deeply important that you understand how ai works even if you don&#x27;t like it and actually it is more important that you understand how it works if you don&#x27;t like it and i read this article today which i think is a perfect example of why because now to even have conversations about like the future of policy and culture and taxation you need to understand some of the technicals of ai let me walk you through this example so the second order effect of ai automating away jobs is that income tax disappears income tax brings in like 7x more than corporate tax and it is mostly from white collar work right so if ai agents replace us then the us is going to have a huge income deficit an interesting alternative that a lot of people are talking about is that instead of taxing humans what if we tax ai agents and like what this could look like hypothetically is that for each ai agent that replaces a human the government basically takes a cut from the company but there&#x27;s like technical issues here right like one ai agents at the end of the day are just a bunch of api calls and that is geographically very flexible so a company could just like reroute all of its calls through a country that taxes less or maybe this would even encourage companies to relocate altogether and the second thing is that there is a large possibility that ai usage in the future is going to be mostly run on local devices using like open way models instead of in the cloud like claude or chat gbt and that actually makes uses tracking like much harder because there&#x27;s no like shared surface area like the data center to monitor this is barely scratching the surface but i mostly want to use it as an example to show how it is becoming basically impossible to separate conversations about policy and ethics from the actual like technical mechanics of ai if we do want to guide you know future policy in the right direction we should not only let ai pill tech bros have these conversations and i have so many thoughts about how we can avoid like a physical collapse with ai but that is for another video</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-24T18:43:46"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbJNnvKxEAx/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>TV Show Psychology for Creators</b>\n<i>Unforgettable creators run all four human storylines simultaneously — love, identity, inner life, mission — just like every bingeable TV show, instead of repeating one niche.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Every bingeable TV show runs four simultaneous storylines: relationships (who they love), origin story (who they are and where they come from), inner battle (how they hold themselves together or fall apart), and mission (the thing they&#x27;re chasing)\n• Those four storylines map exactly onto the four parts of human experience: who you love, who you are, how you live, and what you offer — jun_yuh calls this the Storytelling Wheel\n• Most creators pick only one storyline — their niche — and repeat the same tips, the teaching, the selling; that&#x27;s a character with one trait and nobody ever falls in love with that\n• The Mere Exposure Effect: the more someone sees you across all four dimensions (love, identity, fitness, earnings), the more they trust you and the less they can look away\n• Running all four storylines in conjunction means you never run out of content ideas\n\n💾 <i>Provides a concrete four-part storytelling framework drawn from TV show narrative psychology that content creators can apply directly to avoid one-dimensional content.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbJNnvKxEAx/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#storytelling #content-creation #psychology</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>TV Show Psychology for Creators</b><br><i>Unforgettable creators run all four human storylines simultaneously — love, identity, inner life, mission — just like every bingeable TV show, instead of repeating one niche.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Every bingeable TV show runs four simultaneous storylines: relationships (who they love), origin story (who they are and where they come from), inner battle (how they hold themselves together or fall apart), and mission (the thing they&#x27;re chasing)</li><li>Those four storylines map exactly onto the four parts of human experience: who you love, who you are, how you live, and what you offer — jun_yuh calls this the Storytelling Wheel</li><li>Most creators pick only one storyline — their niche — and repeat the same tips, the teaching, the selling; that&#x27;s a character with one trait and nobody ever falls in love with that</li><li>The Mere Exposure Effect: the more someone sees you across all four dimensions (love, identity, fitness, earnings), the more they trust you and the less they can look away</li><li>Running all four storylines in conjunction means you never run out of content ideas</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Provides a concrete four-part storytelling framework drawn from TV show narrative psychology that content creators can apply directly to avoid one-dimensional content.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbJNnvKxEAx/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#storytelling #content-creation #psychology</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>The reel introduces the Storytelling Wheel: four storylines from TV shows (relationships, origin story, inner battle, mission) that map onto the four human dimensions (love, identity, inner life, mission). Most creators only run one storyline — their niche — which is why they stay forgettable. Running all four triggers the Mere Exposure Effect and turns a creator into someone audiences can&#x27;t stop watching.\n\nI&#x27;ve studied 137 TV shows, and this one psychology principle made me my family&#x27;s first millionaire, and it&#x27;s made your favorite characters impossible to forget. Every show you have ever binged runs on the exact same four storylines all at the same time. There&#x27;s the relationships, who they love, the origin story, who they really are and where they come from, the inner battle, how they hold themselves together or fall apart, and the mission, the thing they&#x27;re chasing. But the deep underlying psychology here is that those four storylines perfectly mirror the exact four parts of the human experience. Who you love, who you are, how you live, and what you offer. That&#x27;s my storytelling wheel. Meaning, every unforgettable character ever written is secretly built on the four parts your own life is, and it&#x27;s the exact same for the most unforgettable creators. But, most creators still ignore the psychology of their viewer and choose one storyline, aka their niche. And they keep repeating the same tips, the teaching, the selling, and they only have that one single thread. That&#x27;s like a character with one trait, or a story with one single plotline. And nobody in the history of television has ever fallen in love with a one trait character. But let me break down the actual psychology behind this. It&#x27;s called the mirror exposure effect. The more someone sees you, the more they trust you, and the less they can look away. And when they see your love, identity, fitness, and earnings, they&#x27;ll show up over and over again until you&#x27;re someone they can&#x27;t forget. Because I don&#x27;t want you to think about it like you&#x27;re making a post. I want you to think about it like you&#x27;re writing a story. The wheel, and how to run all four storylines in conjunction without ever running out of ideas, that&#x27;s exactly what we break down within the Storytelling Content Challenge. Over 6,000 of you in six days have already joined the wait list. If you want in, comment series, and I&#x27;ll send it over to you. I&#x27;ll send it over to you. I&#x27;ll send it over to you.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-24T20:40:49"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbJMu7dxQgn/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Pitch Brands Like a Marketer</b>\n<i>“pitch brands like a strategic marketer, not a fan”</i>\n\n🪜 <b>How to</b>\n1. Define who your audience is and explain exactly why they are the customers that brand wants to reach\n2. Prove your content converts — include case studies and stats from past partnerships\n3. Pitch 2-3 specific content ideas built for that brand so they can picture the campaign before spending a dollar\n4. Build the deck in Gamma: feed it your website and past partnerships, then tailor it per brand; use their ready-made templates if you have no brand guidelines yet\n\n<i>Useful for: Creators who want to land brand deals by presenting a business case instead of a fan letter</i>\n\n💾 <i>Actionable 3-part brand pitch framework from a creator with verified six-figure partnerships at Meta, Fiverr, ChatGPT, Anthropic, LinkedIn, Adobe, Notion, and others — shown in the actual deck.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbJMu7dxQgn/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#brand-deals #creator #pitching</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Pitch Brands Like a Marketer</b><br><i>“pitch brands like a strategic marketer, not a fan”</i><br>🪜 <b>How to</b><br><ol><li>Define who your audience is and explain exactly why they are the customers that brand wants to reach</li><li>Prove your content converts — include case studies and stats from past partnerships</li><li>Pitch 2-3 specific content ideas built for that brand so they can picture the campaign before spending a dollar</li><li>Build the deck in Gamma: feed it your website and past partnerships, then tailor it per brand; use their ready-made templates if you have no brand guidelines yet</li></ol><br><i>Useful for: Creators who want to land brand deals by presenting a business case instead of a fan letter</i><br>💾 <i>Actionable 3-part brand pitch framework from a creator with verified six-figure partnerships at Meta, Fiverr, ChatGPT, Anthropic, LinkedIn, Adobe, Notion, and others — shown in the actual deck.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbJMu7dxQgn/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#brand-deals #creator #pitching</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Three things every brand pitch needs: proof your audience matches their target customers, evidence your content converts (case studies + stats), and 2-3 pre-built content ideas for that specific brand. Build it all in Gamma using your website and past partnership data.\n\nI have made multiple six figures in partnerships this year as a creator because I pitch brands like a strategic marketer, not a fan. This is episode 6 of my smart 5-9 creator series where I show you how I use my experience across finance, tech, and marketing to build a multiple six-figure brand of my own. Most creators who start out, including me, pitch brands by saying, I love your brand, we should collab with their follower account. But as a marketer in tech who has also been pitched partnerships, I know that brand deals come out of a marketing team&#x27;s budget, which means they need to see a business case. So here are the three things that I put in every brand pitch. Who my audience is and why they&#x27;re the exact customers the brand is trying to reach. Prove that my content converts, like case studies and stats from past partnerships. Two or three content ideas I have specifically for the brand so they can already see the campaign before they&#x27;ve spent a single dollar. I built this entire deck in Gamma, I gave it my website, my past partnerships, and it builds a deck that I can then tailor to any brand. I use my own branding guidelines, but if you don&#x27;t have that, you can use their library of ready-made templates. This is what my full deck looks like. I&#x27;ll share it in the captions so you can steal it and make it your own.</details>",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-24T20:42:09"
  },
