  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbNAoGkN3_a/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>9 Content Concepts That Print Money</b>\n<i>“if you know any of these and you do content you are missing out on thousands of dollars a month”</i>\n\n📚 <b>Concepts</b>\n⚠️ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Methods_of_neuro-linguistic_programming\">Pattern Interrupt</a> — <i>No dedicated Wikipedia page exists for &#x27;pattern interrupt&#x27;; linked to the NLP methods article that mentions the technique. The concept is widely used in sales and content creation to break a viewer&#x27;s autopilot state.</i>\n⚠️ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clickbait\">Curiosity Loop</a> — <i>No dedicated Wikipedia page. Frame 1 shows the loop as: 1. Highlight a problem → 2. Explain it can be solved → 3. Buyer seeks diagnosis call to learn the solution. Closest Wikipedia concept is the &#x27;curiosity gap&#x27; discussed in the Clickbait article.</i>\n⚠️ Hook Stacking — <i>No canonical page found. Refers to layering multiple attention hooks (visual, verbal, text) simultaneously in the opening seconds of a short-form video.</i>\n✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leading_lines\">Leading Lines</a>\n⚠️ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Negative_space\">Dead Space</a> — <i>Frame 2 shows it alongside a golden-ratio composition overlay labeled &#x27;The power of Composition.&#x27; Dead space in this context means intentional empty area in the frame; closest authoritative article is &#x27;Negative space&#x27; on Wikipedia, but the exact term is not used there.</i>\n✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Authority_bias\">Authority Positioning</a>\n✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_proof\">Social Proof</a>\n⚠️ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viral_marketing\">Trojan Horse</a> — <i>No dedicated Wikipedia page for &#x27;Trojan Horse&#x27; as a content strategy. Wikipedia&#x27;s Viral marketing article notes: &#x27;like a Trojan Horse, stories carry messages and ideas along for the ride.&#x27; The technique wraps a sales message inside entertainment or value content.</i>\n✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In-group_and_out-group\">Us vs Them Narrative</a>\n\n💾 <i>Compact named checklist of nine proven content-psychology techniques for short-form video creators monetizing through affiliate platforms.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbNAoGkN3_a/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#content-creation #tiktok-shop #persuasion</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>9 Content Concepts That Print Money</b><br><i>“if you know any of these and you do content you are missing out on thousands of dollars a month”</i><br>📚 <b>Concepts</b><br><ol><li>⚠️ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Methods_of_neuro-linguistic_programming\">Pattern Interrupt</a> — <i>No dedicated Wikipedia page exists for &#x27;pattern interrupt&#x27;; linked to the NLP methods article that mentions the technique. The concept is widely used in sales and content creation to break a viewer&#x27;s autopilot state.</i></li><li>⚠️ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clickbait\">Curiosity Loop</a> — <i>No dedicated Wikipedia page. Frame 1 shows the loop as: 1. Highlight a problem → 2. Explain it can be solved → 3. Buyer seeks diagnosis call to learn the solution. Closest Wikipedia concept is the &#x27;curiosity gap&#x27; discussed in the Clickbait article.</i></li><li>⚠️ Hook Stacking — <i>No canonical page found. Refers to layering multiple attention hooks (visual, verbal, text) simultaneously in the opening seconds of a short-form video.</i></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leading_lines\">Leading Lines</a></li><li>⚠️ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Negative_space\">Dead Space</a> — <i>Frame 2 shows it alongside a golden-ratio composition overlay labeled &#x27;The power of Composition.&#x27; Dead space in this context means intentional empty area in the frame; closest authoritative article is &#x27;Negative space&#x27; on Wikipedia, but the exact term is not used there.</i></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Authority_bias\">Authority Positioning</a></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_proof\">Social Proof</a></li><li>⚠️ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viral_marketing\">Trojan Horse</a> — <i>No dedicated Wikipedia page for &#x27;Trojan Horse&#x27; as a content strategy. Wikipedia&#x27;s Viral marketing article notes: &#x27;like a Trojan Horse, stories carry messages and ideas along for the ride.&#x27; The technique wraps a sales message inside entertainment or value content.</i></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In-group_and_out-group\">Us vs Them Narrative</a></li></ol><br>💾 <i>Compact named checklist of nine proven content-psychology techniques for short-form video creators monetizing through affiliate platforms.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbNAoGkN3_a/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#content-creation #tiktok-shop #persuasion</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>mila.sokolskiy claims that knowing these 9 techniques — pattern interrupt, curiosity loop, hook stacking, leading lines, dead space, authority positioning, social proof, trojan horse, and us-vs-them narrative — is worth thousands of dollars a month for content creators. The caption ties this to TikTok Shop affiliate selling: post videos, drive purchases, earn a commission.\n\nlet&#x27;s see if you actually understand content creation but it&#x27;s extremely niche pattern interrupt curiosity loop hook stacking leading lines dead space authority positioning social proof trojan horse and us first them narrative if you know any of these and you do content you are missing out on thousands of dollars a month i left more details in the caption</details>",
