    "digest": "<b>Neuroscience Essays to Read</b>\n\n📌 <b>Recommended</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://aeon.co/essays/why-zero-could-unlock-how-the-brain-perceives-absence\">Why nothing matters</a> — Benjy Barnett\n✅ <a href=\"https://aeon.co/essays/deja-vu-a-window-on-the-past-and-a-key-to-human-survival\">Déjà vu</a> — Anne Cleary\n✅ <a href=\"https://psyche.co/ideas/this-is-how-your-brain-distinguishes-reality-from-imagination\">This is how your brain distinguishes reality from imagination</a> — Shayla Love\n✅ <a href=\"https://psyche.co/ideas/the-neural-reward-that-makes-avoiding-your-fears-feel-so-good\">The neural reward that makes avoiding your fears feel so good</a> — Muhammad Badarnee and Mohammed R Milad\n\n💾 <i>Curated list of four verified neuroscience essays from Aeon and Psyche on perception of absence, déjà vu, reality vs imagination, and fear avoidance reward circuitry.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DbBWNUGDrtP/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#neuroscience #essays #cognition</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Neuroscience Essays to Read</b><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li>✅ <a href=\"https://aeon.co/essays/why-zero-could-unlock-how-the-brain-perceives-absence\">Why nothing matters</a> — Benjy Barnett</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://aeon.co/essays/deja-vu-a-window-on-the-past-and-a-key-to-human-survival\">Déjà vu</a> — Anne Cleary</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://psyche.co/ideas/this-is-how-your-brain-distinguishes-reality-from-imagination\">This is how your brain distinguishes reality from imagination</a> — Shayla Love</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://psyche.co/ideas/the-neural-reward-that-makes-avoiding-your-fears-feel-so-good\">The neural reward that makes avoiding your fears feel so good</a> — Muhammad Badarnee and Mohammed R Milad</li></ol><br>💾 <i>Curated list of four verified neuroscience essays from Aeon and Psyche on perception of absence, déjà vu, reality vs imagination, and fear avoidance reward circuitry.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DbBWNUGDrtP/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#neuroscience #essays #cognition</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Four curated neuroscience essays from Aeon and Psyche with authors and direct verified links, spanning absence perception, déjà vu, reality vs imagination, and avoidance behavior reward circuits.\n\n<b>Slide 1</b> — <i>Title card over ocean view from a white balcony</i>\nneuroscience essays to read instead of scrolling\n\n<b>Slide 2</b> — <i>Essay card for &#x27;Why nothing matters&#x27; by Benjy Barnett, published on Aeon</i>\nEssay / Neuroscience\nWhy nothing matters\nIt took centuries for people to embrace the zero. Now it&#x27;s helping neuroscientists understand how the brain perceives absences\nBenjy Barnett\nsource: aeon\n\n<b>Slide 3</b> — <i>Essay card for &#x27;Déjà vu&#x27; by Anne Cleary, published on Aeon</i>\nEssay / Consciousness and altered states\nDéjà vu\nHave you been here before? The eerie sensation is the shadow of your mind searching inward for clues to its own survival\nAnne Cleary\nsource: aeon\n\n<b>Slide 4</b> — <i>Essay card for Shayla Love&#x27;s piece on reality vs imagination, published on Psyche</i>\nThis is how your brain distinguishes reality from imagination\nby Shayla Love\nsource: psyche\n\n<b>Slide 5</b> — <i>Essay card for Badarnee and Milad&#x27;s piece on the reward circuitry of fear avoidance, published on Psyche</i>\nThe neural reward that makes avoiding your fears feel so good\nby Muhammad Badarnee and Mohammed R Milad, behavioural neuroscientists\nsource: psyche</details>",
