  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Da3JsPjJ0kc/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "“<b>Maybe you don&#x27;t need to become someone else, maybe you just need enough room to recognize you.</b>”\n— storieswithkiki\n\n<i>Moving abroad doesn&#x27;t create a new self — it removes the external expectations that were shaping the old one.</i>\n💡 <b>Being unknown somewhere new isn&#x27;t erasure; it&#x27;s permission to stop performing who you had been.</b>\n\n💾 <i>Reframes the loneliness of major life changes as a natural part of identity shedding, not a sign of failure.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/Da3JsPjJ0kc/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#identity #moving-abroad #self-discovery</i>",
    "markdown": "“<b>Maybe you don&#x27;t need to become someone else, maybe you just need enough room to recognize you.</b>”<br>— storieswithkiki<br><i>Moving abroad doesn&#x27;t create a new self — it removes the external expectations that were shaping the old one.</i><br>💡 <b>Being unknown somewhere new isn&#x27;t erasure; it&#x27;s permission to stop performing who you had been.</b><br>💾 <i>Reframes the loneliness of major life changes as a natural part of identity shedding, not a sign of failure.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/Da3JsPjJ0kc/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#identity #moving-abroad #self-discovery</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>A personal monologue on how moving abroad created space to stop performing a socially-shaped self. She reframes the loneliness of big transitions not as proof of a wrong choice, but as the gap where an old identity quietly dissolves.\n\nMoving abroad didn&#x27;t help me find myself, it gave me space to stop performing who I had been. I wrote about this on my substack after thinking about my first morning in Vancouver. I was standing in the shower of an almost empty house completely heartbroken, wondering why I had unraveled a perfectly good life on purpose. Because I think a lot of us believe that the right decision will immediately feel right, that if the move, the job or the new beginning is meant for us, we won&#x27;t feel lonely inside it. But moving abroad made me a beginner again. Nobody knew my history, nobody knew the version of me from school, the overachiever, the responsible one, the person I had learned to be because other people already expected to meet her. And at first, being unknown felt like erasure. But then it started to feel like permission, because if nobody knew who I had been, nobody could tell me who I wasn&#x27;t. I could be quieter, softer, bolder, less explainable. I didn&#x27;t become a completely different person, I became less edited. And maybe that&#x27;s why leaving can feel lonely and freeing at the same time. The loneliness isn&#x27;t always proof that you chose wrong. Sometimes it&#x27;s the space where an old identity stops answering for you. Maybe you don&#x27;t need to become someone else, maybe you just need enough room to recognize you.</details>",
    "blocks": {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "blockquote",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": [
                {
                  "type": "bold",
                  "text": "“Maybe you don't need to become someone else, maybe you just need enough room to recognize you.”"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": [
                {
                  "type": "italic",
                  "text": "— storieswithkiki"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "italic",
              "text": "Moving abroad doesn't create a new self — it removes the external expectations that were shaping the old one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            "💡 ",
            {
              "type": "bold",
              "text": "Being unknown somewhere new isn't erasure; it's permission to stop performing who you had been."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "blockquote",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "💾 Reframes the loneliness of major life changes as a natural part of identity shedding, not a sign of failure."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "divider"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "url",
              "text": "🔗 Original reel",
              "url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Da3JsPjJ0kc/"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "details",
          "summary": "📄 Full transcript",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A personal monologue on how moving abroad created space to stop performing a socially-shaped self. She reframes the loneliness of big transitions not as proof of a wrong choice, but as the gap where an old identity quietly dissolves."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Moving abroad didn't help me find myself, it gave me space to stop performing who I had been. I wrote about this on my substack after thinking about my first morning in Vancouver. I was standing in the shower of an almost empty house completely heartbroken, wondering why I had unraveled a perfectly good life on purpose. Because I think a lot of us believe that the right decision will immediately feel right, that if the move, the job or the new beginning is meant for us, we won't feel lonely inside it. But moving abroad made me a beginner again. Nobody knew my history, nobody knew the version of me from school, the overachiever, the responsible one, the person I had learned to be because other people already expected to meet her. And at first, being unknown felt like erasure. But then it started to feel like permission, because if nobody knew who I had been, nobody could tell me who I wasn't. I could be quieter, softer, bolder, less explainable. I didn't become a completely different person, I became less edited. And maybe that's why leaving can feel lonely and freeing at the same time. The loneliness isn't always proof that you chose wrong. Sometimes it's the space where an old identity stops answering for you. Maybe you don't need to become someone else, maybe you just need enough room to recognize you."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "text": "#identity #moving-abroad #self-discovery"
        }
      ]
    },
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-28T00:08:51"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DXw0Yt4t0Sk/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Curiosity Mapping Monthly Reflection Practice</b>\n<i>Use a monthly mind map to trace where your curiosity has been — start with an event, film, or book that pulled your attention, then branch outward to wherever your mind went.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Start with a central node: an event, something you watched, or something you read that sparked curiosity\n• Branch outward from there to wherever your mind went — associations, questions, themes\n• No rules; the exercise is personal and entirely your own\n• Reflection is at the core of building curiosity\n\n💾 <i>A low-friction personal journaling method that makes one month&#x27;s intellectual curiosity visible and traceable through mind mapping, demonstrated with real cultural references.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DXw0Yt4t0Sk/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#curiosity #journaling #reflection</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Curiosity Mapping Monthly Reflection Practice</b><br><i>Use a monthly mind map to trace where your curiosity has been — start with an event, film, or book that pulled your attention, then branch outward to wherever your mind went.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Start with a central node: an event, something you watched, or something you read that sparked curiosity</li><li>Branch outward from there to wherever your mind went — associations, questions, themes</li><li>No rules; the exercise is personal and entirely your own</li><li>Reflection is at the core of building curiosity</li></ul><br>💾 <i>A low-friction personal journaling method that makes one month&#x27;s intellectual curiosity visible and traceable through mind mapping, demonstrated with real cultural references.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DXw0Yt4t0Sk/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#curiosity #journaling #reflection</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Creator shows her monthly curiosity mind-mapping practice: start with something that pulled your attention (a film, book, event) and branch out to wherever your mind went, no rules. Her own map for the month includes The Drama (A24 film), In the Swarm by Byung-Chul Han (parasociality, lack of distance in social media), and personal threads on her role as a content creator, performance vs. authenticity, routine vs. spontaneity, and mental stability.\n\nIn the spirit of becoming more curious, let&#x27;s do a mind mapping of where our head was this past month. I&#x27;ve talked in a previous video about how at the core of building your curiosity is reflection. So this exercise is the application of that. There are no rules, but if you do want guidelines, I would start out with like a big node of an event or something you watch or read. Anything that pulled your curiosity or wherever your mind went and then branch out from that. I&#x27;m gonna make mine and then I&#x27;ll show you. This is what I came up with. Let&#x27;s go over it. I started here. I watched two things this past month, the drama and the violin beyond the villa. And it both reminded me of unreliable narrators. I also read the book In the Storm, which deals with social media and the lack of distance, i.e. parasociality. And obviously with social media, my relationship to it as a content creator. Have fun with this exercise. Just remember that it is for you and no one else.</details>",
    "blocks": {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "size": 1,
          "text": "Curiosity Mapping Monthly Reflection Practice"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
