  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbZDkbeoJ1k/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>3-Layer College Essay Framework</b>\n\n🪜 <b>How to</b>\n1. Layer 1 — The Big Story: identify one thing that changed or is currently changing you; this is the narrative baseline, the straight line from A to B\n2. Layer 2 — Key Events: add 3–5 emotionally powerful events that happened during that change to create turbulence and risk in the story\n3. Layer 3 — Sensory Moments: choose 1–2 key seconds from each event and describe them using at least one sensory detail (sight, smell, sound, taste, or touch) to immerse the reader in a fully 3D experience\n\n<i>Useful for: Anyone writing a Common App college essay who needs to know what to write about and how to make it stick with an admissions officer.</i>\n\n💾 <i>Gives a concrete, 3-step structure that solves both the topic and the craft problem of a college application essay.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbZDkbeoJ1k/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#college-essay #storytelling #writing</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>3-Layer College Essay Framework</b><br>🪜 <b>How to</b><br><ol><li>Layer 1 — The Big Story: identify one thing that changed or is currently changing you; this is the narrative baseline, the straight line from A to B</li><li>Layer 2 — Key Events: add 3–5 emotionally powerful events that happened during that change to create turbulence and risk in the story</li><li>Layer 3 — Sensory Moments: choose 1–2 key seconds from each event and describe them using at least one sensory detail (sight, smell, sound, taste, or touch) to immerse the reader in a fully 3D experience</li></ol><br><i>Useful for: Anyone writing a Common App college essay who needs to know what to write about and how to make it stick with an admissions officer.</i><br>💾 <i>Gives a concrete, 3-step structure that solves both the topic and the craft problem of a college application essay.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbZDkbeoJ1k/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#college-essay #storytelling #writing</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>The framework answers two questions: what to write about and how to make it memorable. Layer 1 is the big story — one thing that changed you, from A to B. Layer 2 adds 3–5 emotionally powerful key events to create turbulence. Layer 3 picks 1–2 sensory-detail seconds per event to put the reader inside the scene, achieving &#x27;show don&#x27;t tell.&#x27; Neuroscience research supports why sensory details work: reading about them activates the same neural circuits as experiencing them.\n\nIf you want to get into universities like this, you need to learn storytelling. Hi, I&#x27;m Phoebe. You&#x27;re a resident Oxford grad and Harvard PhD, and here is the storytelling framework that helped me write college essays that got me into multiple Ivy League schools. This is called my three-layer storytelling framework, and it answers two questions that I know are stopping you from actually writing a great college essay. Number one, what do I write about? And number two, how do I write it so it&#x27;s actually memorable? First, it tells you what to write about in your essay. One thing that changed or is changing you in some way. This is the first base layer, our big story. But do you want your reader, the admissions officer, to have such a flat boring journey from A to B? No, we want to give it some layers, some texture. So we&#x27;re going to make our story more exciting by adding some waves. The second layer, three to five key events during your change that are the most emotionally powerful. Now there is some turbulence and some risk to the story. But how do we make our reader really feel it? We add realism, immerse them in a fully 3D experience. So choose one to two key seconds from each of your events and describe them using at least one sensory detail. Sight, smell, sound, taste, or touch. There&#x27;s plenty of neuroscience research suggesting why such sensory details are so effective in storytelling and writing. For instance, reading about such things is enough to light up the same neural circuits as experiencing them in person. And so now with this final third layer, you&#x27;re finally showing and not telling. If you want five essay plans to see how this framework applies to real Common App essays, comment essay down below so I can DM it to you.</details>",
    "blocks": {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "size": 1,
          "text": "3-Layer College Essay Framework"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "size": 3,
          "text": "🪜 How to"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "bold",
              "text": "1. "
            },
            "Layer 1 — The Big Story: identify one thing that changed or is currently changing you; this is the narrative baseline, the straight line from A to B"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "bold",
              "text": "2. "
            },
            "Layer 2 — Key Events: add 3–5 emotionally powerful events that happened during that change to create turbulence and risk in the story"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "bold",
              "text": "3. "
            },
            "Layer 3 — Sensory Moments: choose 1–2 key seconds from each event and describe them using at least one sensory detail (sight, smell, sound, taste, or touch) to immerse the reader in a fully 3D experience"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "italic",
              "text": "Useful for: Anyone writing a Common App college essay who needs to know what to write about and how to make it stick with an admissions officer."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "blockquote",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "💾 Gives a concrete, 3-step structure that solves both the topic and the craft problem of a college application essay."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "divider"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "url",
              "text": "🔗 Original reel",
              "url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbZDkbeoJ1k/"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "details",
          "summary": "📄 Full transcript",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The framework answers two questions: what to write about and how to make it memorable. Layer 1 is the big story — one thing that changed you, from A to B. Layer 2 adds 3–5 emotionally powerful key events to create turbulence. Layer 3 picks 1–2 sensory-detail seconds per event to put the reader inside the scene, achieving 'show don't tell.' Neuroscience research supports why sensory details work: reading about them activates the same neural circuits as experiencing them."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If you want to get into universities like this, you need to learn storytelling. Hi, I'm Phoebe. You're a resident Oxford grad and Harvard PhD, and here is the storytelling framework that helped me write college essays that got me into multiple Ivy League schools. This is called my three-layer storytelling framework, and it answers two questions that I know are stopping you from actually writing a great college essay. Number one, what do I write about? And number two, how do I write it so it's actually memorable? First, it tells you what to write about in your essay. One thing that changed or is changing you in some way. This is the first base layer, our big story. But do you want your reader, the admissions officer, to have such a flat boring journey from A to B? No, we want to give it some layers, some texture. So we're going to make our story more exciting by adding some waves. The second layer, three to five key events during your change that are the most emotionally powerful. Now there is some turbulence and some risk to the story. But how do we make our reader really feel it? We add realism, immerse them in a fully 3D experience. So choose one to two key seconds from each of your events and describe them using at least one sensory detail. Sight, smell, sound, taste, or touch. There's plenty of neuroscience research suggesting why such sensory details are so effective in storytelling and writing. For instance, reading about such things is enough to light up the same neural circuits as experiencing them in person. And so now with this final third layer, you're finally showing and not telling. If you want five essay plans to see how this framework applies to real Common App essays, comment essay down below so I can DM it to you."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "text": "#college-essay #storytelling #writing"
        }
      ]
    },
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-07-31T01:12:18"
  },
