  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dbb70IQqJ-z/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>3 Techniques to Manage Multiple Interests</b>\n<i>Use three structured frameworks to ruthlessly prioritize your top interests and deliberately avoid the rest, instead of letting all interests compete equally for your time.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• The 20 goals you don&#x27;t circle in the 5/25 Rule aren&#x27;t a &#x27;do later&#x27; list — they become an avoid list, because their appeal is exactly what makes them dangerous distractions\n• Interest stacking means combining interests into one project (e.g., writing about what you&#x27;re reading, or running while listening to a history podcast)\n• Lifelong Learning Compass splits interests into three tiers: Mastery (one craft to truly produce in), Erudition (interests you only read about, no output), Range (a few skills to be intentionally average at)\n• Everything outside your defined tiers is deferred to &#x27;another time&#x27; — not abandoned, just scheduled away\n\n💾 <i>Distills three actionable, named frameworks for managing too many interests into one short video, with the full source article linked for deeper reading.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dbb70IQqJ-z/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#focus #interests #productivity</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>3 Techniques to Manage Multiple Interests</b><br><i>Use three structured frameworks to ruthlessly prioritize your top interests and deliberately avoid the rest, instead of letting all interests compete equally for your time.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>The 20 goals you don&#x27;t circle in the 5/25 Rule aren&#x27;t a &#x27;do later&#x27; list — they become an avoid list, because their appeal is exactly what makes them dangerous distractions</li><li>Interest stacking means combining interests into one project (e.g., writing about what you&#x27;re reading, or running while listening to a history podcast)</li><li>Lifelong Learning Compass splits interests into three tiers: Mastery (one craft to truly produce in), Erudition (interests you only read about, no output), Range (a few skills to be intentionally average at)</li><li>Everything outside your defined tiers is deferred to &#x27;another time&#x27; — not abandoned, just scheduled away</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Distills three actionable, named frameworks for managing too many interests into one short video, with the full source article linked for deeper reading.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dbb70IQqJ-z/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#focus #interests #productivity</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Three frameworks for managing multiple interests: the 5/25 Rule (write 25 goals, circle top 5, actively avoid the other 20), Interest Stacking (combine interests into one project), and the Lifelong Learning Compass (sort all interests into Mastery, Erudition, and Range tiers — everything else is deferred).\n\n10 out of 10 advice I&#x27;m using to manage multiple interests based on this popular sub-stack article except it gets increasingly more niche. The first idea is called the 5 by 25 rule and it&#x27;s by Warren Buffett. So in it you basically write out 25 things you really want to do and then you circle the 5 that you really want to do the most. But the interesting bit is the other 20 don&#x27;t just become a list of things for you to do later, they actually become a list of things for you to avoid because the problem with them is that they&#x27;re actually interesting and that&#x27;s what makes them dangerous distractions. And the second idea is called interest stacking and this is where you combine your interests into one project. This could be creating content about what you&#x27;re reading or it could be running while listening to a history podcast or whatever. And the most niche idea is what&#x27;s called a lifelong learning compass. In this one you sort every one of your interests into tiers where one tier is a craft that you actually want to master and another tier is a few interests that you only read about but you don&#x27;t actually produce or make anything in. And the last tier is 5 skills that you want to be average at on purpose and every interest outside of this is just for another time. And I personally specifically need this advice because I&#x27;m known to try 50 million different things all at once. I end up getting overwhelmed and then I just give up on all of them. And in reality I think I need to accept that I can only do so much and not everything everywhere all at once. I hope this helps.</details>",
    "blocks": {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "size": 1,
          "text": "3 Techniques to Manage Multiple Interests"
        },
