  "https://vt.tiktok.com/ZS4SQF8Su/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Stop Building Knowledge Graveyards</b>\n<i>Saving content to lists (Collector&#x27;s Fallacy) triggers a dopamine reward as if you had actually learned — you haven&#x27;t — and the unfinished lists then breed chronic mental tension via the Zeigarnik effect.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• To-read and to-watch lists feel productive but are just graveyards — they make you feel smarter without actual learning\n• Saving a book or article rewards your brain with dopamine as if you had already read it — you haven&#x27;t\n• Long unfinished lists create a low-level tension in the brain (Zeigarnik effect) that makes you feel guilty for not completing them\n• It&#x27;s likely impossible to get through all the lists you&#x27;ve built, yet your brain constantly perceives a gap between what you want to do and what you&#x27;re doing\n\n💾 <i>Concise explanation of two named cognitive mechanisms (Collector&#x27;s Fallacy + Zeigarnik effect) behind why information-hoarding habits feel good but actively make you feel worse.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://vt.tiktok.com/ZS4SQF8Su/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#collectors-fallacy #neuroscience #productivity</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Stop Building Knowledge Graveyards</b><br><i>Saving content to lists (Collector&#x27;s Fallacy) triggers a dopamine reward as if you had actually learned — you haven&#x27;t — and the unfinished lists then breed chronic mental tension via the Zeigarnik effect.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>To-read and to-watch lists feel productive but are just graveyards — they make you feel smarter without actual learning</li><li>Saving a book or article rewards your brain with dopamine as if you had already read it — you haven&#x27;t</li><li>Long unfinished lists create a low-level tension in the brain (Zeigarnik effect) that makes you feel guilty for not completing them</li><li>It&#x27;s likely impossible to get through all the lists you&#x27;ve built, yet your brain constantly perceives a gap between what you want to do and what you&#x27;re doing</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Concise explanation of two named cognitive mechanisms (Collector&#x27;s Fallacy + Zeigarnik effect) behind why information-hoarding habits feel good but actively make you feel worse.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://vt.tiktok.com/ZS4SQF8Su/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#collectors-fallacy #neuroscience #productivity</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Short educational video on two cognitive biases behind digital hoarding: the Collector&#x27;s Fallacy — the false belief that having access to knowledge equals acquiring it — and the Zeigarnik effect, which makes long unfinished lists a source of chronic brain tension and guilt.</details>",
    "blocks": {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "size": 1,
          "text": "Stop Building Knowledge Graveyards"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "italic",
              "text": "Saving content to lists (Collector's Fallacy) triggers a dopamine reward as if you had actually learned — you haven't — and the unfinished lists then breed chronic mental tension via the Zeigarnik effect."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "size": 3,
          "text": "🔑 Key points"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            {
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "To-read and to-watch lists feel productive but are just graveyards — they make you feel smarter without actual learning"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Saving a book or article rewards your brain with dopamine as if you had already read it — you haven't"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Long unfinished lists create a low-level tension in the brain (Zeigarnik effect) that makes you feel guilty for not completing them"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It's likely impossible to get through all the lists you've built, yet your brain constantly perceives a gap between what you want to do and what you're doing"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "blockquote",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "💾 Concise explanation of two named cognitive mechanisms (Collector's Fallacy + Zeigarnik effect) behind why information-hoarding habits feel good but actively make you feel worse."
