  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbWyp4kvKjA/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "“<b>Nobody cries over perfect content. They cry over honest human expression.</b>”\n— kolbykirschner\n\n<i>AI will make content nearly free and abundant, shifting the competitive advantage entirely to people with stories, taste, curiosity, and a genuine point of view.</i>\n💡 <b>In the AI age, winning belongs not to whoever makes the most, but to whoever makes people feel something.</b>\n\n💾 <i>A sharp historical frame for why authentic human voice becomes more valuable, not less, as AI floods content supply.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbWyp4kvKjA/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#ai #creativity #human-expression</i>",
    "markdown": "“<b>Nobody cries over perfect content. They cry over honest human expression.</b>”<br>— kolbykirschner<br><i>AI will make content nearly free and abundant, shifting the competitive advantage entirely to people with stories, taste, curiosity, and a genuine point of view.</i><br>💡 <b>In the AI age, winning belongs not to whoever makes the most, but to whoever makes people feel something.</b><br>💾 <i>A sharp historical frame for why authentic human voice becomes more valuable, not less, as AI floods content supply.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbWyp4kvKjA/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#ai #creativity #human-expression</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>When information becomes cheap, meaning becomes rare. The Gutenberg moment led to the Renaissance; the AI moment should lead to the same — a new premium on stories, taste, and honest human expression over volume and perfection.\n\nThe 2030s won&#x27;t be remembered as an AI revolution. They&#x27;ll be remembered as the beginning of a new renaissance. Everyone is talking about AI. They say we&#x27;re living through unprecedented times. But we&#x27;ve lived through this before. In 1440, Johannes Gutenberg invented the printing press. For the first time in history, knowledge could spread faster than anyone could have imagined. Within a century, Europe went from 30,000 books to over 200 million books. And something very strange happened. Information became abundant. But meaning became rare. Then came the renaissance. A rebirth of art, craftsmanship, original thinking. The people who mattered weren&#x27;t the ones that made the most noise. They were the ones that created the work that could survive time. And now, history is repeating itself. AI will make content almost free. Anyone will be able to create anything. Which means, the advantage won&#x27;t belong to the people who can make the most. It will belong to the people who can make us feel something. The people with stories, with taste, with curiosity, with a point of view. Because nobody cries over perfect content. They cry over honest human expression. They&#x27;re moved by honest human expression. AI won&#x27;t replace great artists and great humans. But it will force great artists to become even better. Let&#x27;s build the modern renaissance together.</details>",
    "blocks": {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "blockquote",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": [
                {
                  "type": "bold",
                  "text": "“Nobody cries over perfect content. They cry over honest human expression.”"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": [
                {
                  "type": "italic",
                  "text": "— kolbykirschner"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "italic",
              "text": "AI will make content nearly free and abundant, shifting the competitive advantage entirely to people with stories, taste, curiosity, and a genuine point of view."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            "💡 ",
            {
              "type": "bold",
              "text": "In the AI age, winning belongs not to whoever makes the most, but to whoever makes people feel something."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "blockquote",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "💾 A sharp historical frame for why authentic human voice becomes more valuable, not less, as AI floods content supply."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "divider"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "url",
              "text": "🔗 Original reel",
              "url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbWyp4kvKjA/"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "details",
          "summary": "📄 Full transcript",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When information becomes cheap, meaning becomes rare. The Gutenberg moment led to the Renaissance; the AI moment should lead to the same — a new premium on stories, taste, and honest human expression over volume and perfection."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The 2030s won't be remembered as an AI revolution. They'll be remembered as the beginning of a new renaissance. Everyone is talking about AI. They say we're living through unprecedented times. But we've lived through this before. In 1440, Johannes Gutenberg invented the printing press. For the first time in history, knowledge could spread faster than anyone could have imagined. Within a century, Europe went from 30,000 books to over 200 million books. And something very strange happened. Information became abundant. But meaning became rare. Then came the renaissance. A rebirth of art, craftsmanship, original thinking. The people who mattered weren't the ones that made the most noise. They were the ones that created the work that could survive time. And now, history is repeating itself. AI will make content almost free. Anyone will be able to create anything. Which means, the advantage won't belong to the people who can make the most. It will belong to the people who can make us feel something. The people with stories, with taste, with curiosity, with a point of view. Because nobody cries over perfect content. They cry over honest human expression. They're moved by honest human expression. AI won't replace great artists and great humans. But it will force great artists to become even better. Let's build the modern renaissance together."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "text": "#ai #creativity #human-expression"
        }
      ]
    },
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-08-04T01:15:39"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dbli0ifpqlR/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>3 Essays on AI Visual Culture</b>\n\n📌 <b>Recommended</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://excavating.ai/\">Excavating AI: The Politics of Images in Machine Learning Training Sets</a> — Kate Crawford, Trevor Paglen\n✅ <a href=\"https://newleftreview.org/issues/ii140/articles/hito-steyerl-mean-images\">Mean Images</a> — Hito Steyerl\n✅ <a href=\"https://direct.mit.edu/octo/article/doi/10.1162/OCTO.a.545/137249/Latent-Spaces-AI-Art-and-the-Archive\">Latent Spaces: AI, Art, and the Archive</a> — Antonio Somaini\n\n💾 <i>A curated scholarly reading list that offers critical, art-historical counterpoint to mainstream discourse on AI and aesthetics — assigned in a Columbia University seminar.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dbli0ifpqlR/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#ai #visual_culture #art_history</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>3 Essays on AI Visual Culture</b><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li>✅ <a href=\"https://excavating.ai/\">Excavating AI: The Politics of Images in Machine Learning Training Sets</a> — Kate Crawford, Trevor Paglen</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://newleftreview.org/issues/ii140/articles/hito-steyerl-mean-images\">Mean Images</a> — Hito Steyerl</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://direct.mit.edu/octo/article/doi/10.1162/OCTO.a.545/137249/Latent-Spaces-AI-Art-and-the-Archive\">Latent Spaces: AI, Art, and the Archive</a> — Antonio Somaini</li></ol><br>💾 <i>A curated scholarly reading list that offers critical, art-historical counterpoint to mainstream discourse on AI and aesthetics — assigned in a Columbia University seminar.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dbli0ifpqlR/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#ai #visual_culture #art_history</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Three scholarly texts — Excavating AI (Crawford &amp; Paglen), Mean Images (Steyerl), and Latent Spaces (Somaini) — examining the politics, aesthetics, and mathematical logic behind how AI systems process and generate images.\n\nThree essential essays that explain how AI is changing our visual culture. I&#x27;m Amelia. I&#x27;m getting my master&#x27;s in art history at Columbia, and these texts were assigned reading in a seminar I took called AI Imaging in Art. First up, we have Excavating AI, the Politics of Images in Machine Learning Training Sets. This is by Kate Crawford, who researches the social implications of artificial intelligence, and Trevor Paglin, a multidisciplinary artist whose work tends to deal with surveillance, geography, and data collection. This piece examines the image databases used to train machine learning systems and exposes the problematic nature of the categorization and labeling used to make images legible for machines. Next, we have Hito Steyrell&#x27;s Mean Images. Steyrell is a filmmaker who also writes about contemporary media and technology. And in Mean Images, Steyrell discusses how generated images are actually statistical renderings, not depictions of real objects. She chronicles the very dangerous history of mean images, especially as they relate to portraiture. And then we&#x27;ll end with Antonio Someni&#x27;s Theory of Latent Spaces. Someni is a French film, visual culture, and media theorist. And in this text, Someni discusses the implication of latent space, a mathematical space where AI systems essentially compress and simplify things like image, text, and video into the same lower-dimensional forms so that they can be read by a machine. And not only does this have a homogenizing effect, but also a selective or biased one, wherein the latent space certain features are prioritized while others are ignored.</details>",
    "blocks": {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "size": 1,
          "text": "3 Essays on AI Visual Culture"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "size": 3,
          "text": "📌 Recommended"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
