    "ts": "2026-08-06T01:48:32"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dbei41aSxvY/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "“<b>the more you rest the more tired you become</b>”\n— tayneboniface\n\n<i>Inertia applies to human productivity — extended rest kills momentum and makes restarting far harder than staying in motion.</i>\n💡 <b>The best way to recharge is to keep moving and maintain momentum, not to fully stop.</b>\n\n💾 <i>Reframes extended rest as a momentum trap using Newton&#x27;s First Law, offering a clear mental model for why staying in motion beats stopping to recover.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dbei41aSxvY/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#momentum #inertia #discipline</i>",
    "markdown": "“<b>the more you rest the more tired you become</b>”<br>— tayneboniface<br><i>Inertia applies to human productivity — extended rest kills momentum and makes restarting far harder than staying in motion.</i><br>💡 <b>The best way to recharge is to keep moving and maintain momentum, not to fully stop.</b><br>💾 <i>Reframes extended rest as a momentum trap using Newton&#x27;s First Law, offering a clear mental model for why staying in motion beats stopping to recover.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dbei41aSxvY/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#momentum #inertia #discipline</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Resting too much kills momentum — Newton&#x27;s First Law explains why objects and people at rest resist change, and why keeping moving is actually the better way to recharge.\n\nthe more you rest the more tired you become know what i&#x27;m talking about if you&#x27;ve ever had that stretch of time where you&#x27;ve stayed in the house for a few days even a week and even the simplest tasks like taking a walk or going to the grocery store they start to feel impossible but it&#x27;s not out of laziness and it&#x27;s actually pretty simple to counteract if we just look at newton&#x27;s first law of motion inertia an object in motion will stay in motion an object at rest will stay at rest and both objects will resist change when we&#x27;re actively pursuing a goal we build momentum which reduces the effort required to maintain that rate of progress but when we relax when we take a break that momentum comes to a halt and it requires far more effort to get back to that same position the best way to recharge really is just to keep moving and maintain that momentum</details>",
    "blocks": {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "blockquote",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": [
                {
                  "type": "bold",
                  "text": "“the more you rest the more tired you become”"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": [
                {
                  "type": "italic",
                  "text": "— tayneboniface"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "italic",
              "text": "Inertia applies to human productivity — extended rest kills momentum and makes restarting far harder than staying in motion."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            "💡 ",
            {
              "type": "bold",
              "text": "The best way to recharge is to keep moving and maintain momentum, not to fully stop."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "blockquote",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "💾 Reframes extended rest as a momentum trap using Newton's First Law, offering a clear mental model for why staying in motion beats stopping to recover."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "divider"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "url",
              "text": "🔗 Original reel",
              "url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dbei41aSxvY/"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "details",
          "summary": "📄 Full transcript",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Resting too much kills momentum — Newton's First Law explains why objects and people at rest resist change, and why keeping moving is actually the better way to recharge."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "the more you rest the more tired you become know what i'm talking about if you've ever had that stretch of time where you've stayed in the house for a few days even a week and even the simplest tasks like taking a walk or going to the grocery store they start to feel impossible but it's not out of laziness and it's actually pretty simple to counteract if we just look at newton's first law of motion inertia an object in motion will stay in motion an object at rest will stay at rest and both objects will resist change when we're actively pursuing a goal we build momentum which reduces the effort required to maintain that rate of progress but when we relax when we take a break that momentum comes to a halt and it requires far more effort to get back to that same position the best way to recharge really is just to keep moving and maintain that momentum"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "text": "#momentum #inertia #discipline"
