  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DVjVewzEfSg/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "“<b>You don&#x27;t have easy and you don&#x27;t have hard. You have familiar and you have unfamiliar.</b>”\n— thomasisntreal\n\n<i>Easy and hard are illusions — what we call talent or natural ability is just accumulated familiarity from repeated exposure to similar things.</i>\n💡 <b>Stop calling things hard; call them unfamiliar, then go do them more until they aren&#x27;t.</b>\n\n💾 <i>Reframes difficulty from fixed ability to learned familiarity, making any new skill feel approachable rather than gatekept by talent.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DVjVewzEfSg/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#mindset #learning #growth</i>",
    "markdown": "“<b>You don&#x27;t have easy and you don&#x27;t have hard. You have familiar and you have unfamiliar.</b>”<br>— thomasisntreal<br><i>Easy and hard are illusions — what we call talent or natural ability is just accumulated familiarity from repeated exposure to similar things.</i><br>💡 <b>Stop calling things hard; call them unfamiliar, then go do them more until they aren&#x27;t.</b><br>💾 <i>Reframes difficulty from fixed ability to learned familiarity, making any new skill feel approachable rather than gatekept by talent.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DVjVewzEfSg/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#mindset #learning #growth</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Talk-to-camera monologue reframing difficulty: nothing is inherently easy or hard, only familiar or unfamiliar. Becoming good at anything is purely a matter of doing it repeatedly and not quitting after failure.\n\nI&#x27;m going to develop your prefrontal cortex really quickly, so give me a second. You don&#x27;t have easy and you don&#x27;t have hard. You have familiar and you have unfamiliar. You have things that you&#x27;ve done before or things that are like things you&#x27;ve done before and things that you&#x27;ve never done before or things that are not really like anything that you&#x27;ve done before. Easy and hard don&#x27;t exist. They don&#x27;t. You&#x27;re not naturally good at things. You&#x27;ve just done things that are similar to that. Or your body is maybe made for it, like a tall person with basketball, right? But even a tall person that plays basketball still needs to learn like hand drills. They still need to have good hand-eye coordination. There is no easy way of getting there or hard way of getting there. There is people that do it and there&#x27;s people that don&#x27;t do it. There&#x27;s people that practice and fail and never try it again. And there&#x27;s people that practice and fail and they continue doing it and they become really good at it because that&#x27;s how every single thing works. That&#x27;s how speaking works into this phone that I&#x27;m talking to you right now. I became good at this by doing it more.</details>",
    "blocks": {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "blockquote",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": [
                {
                  "type": "bold",
                  "text": "“You don't have easy and you don't have hard. You have familiar and you have unfamiliar.”"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": [
                {
                  "type": "italic",
                  "text": "— thomasisntreal"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "italic",
              "text": "Easy and hard are illusions — what we call talent or natural ability is just accumulated familiarity from repeated exposure to similar things."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            "💡 ",
            {
              "type": "bold",
              "text": "Stop calling things hard; call them unfamiliar, then go do them more until they aren't."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "blockquote",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "💾 Reframes difficulty from fixed ability to learned familiarity, making any new skill feel approachable rather than gatekept by talent."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
