          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Talk-to-camera monologue reframing difficulty: nothing is inherently easy or hard, only familiar or unfamiliar. Becoming good at anything is purely a matter of doing it repeatedly and not quitting after failure."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "I'm going to develop your prefrontal cortex really quickly, so give me a second. You don't have easy and you don't have hard. You have familiar and you have unfamiliar. You have things that you've done before or things that are like things you've done before and things that you've never done before or things that are not really like anything that you've done before. Easy and hard don't exist. They don't. You're not naturally good at things. You've just done things that are similar to that. Or your body is maybe made for it, like a tall person with basketball, right? But even a tall person that plays basketball still needs to learn like hand drills. They still need to have good hand-eye coordination. There is no easy way of getting there or hard way of getting there. There is people that do it and there's people that don't do it. There's people that practice and fail and never try it again. And there's people that practice and fail and they continue doing it and they become really good at it because that's how every single thing works. That's how speaking works into this phone that I'm talking to you right now. I became good at this by doing it more."
            }
          ]
        },
