        },
        {
          "type": "details",
          "summary": "📄 Full transcript",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Dirty fuel is fear-driven achievement where self-worth depends on results; clean fuel is acting from wholeness. The shift requires emotional rewiring of subconscious beliefs, not just mindset shifts."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "I have a deep belief that if you're an overachiever, the quality of your life is determined by how you answer the following question. What's actually driving you? Because I think most overachievers are being driven by something that they don't fully understand. I call it dirty fuel. Dirty fuel is the chip on your shoulder. It's the voice that says, Fuck you, I will prove you wrong. It's powerful. In fact, it's usually what creates ambitious people in the first place. But it comes with a cost. Because it's kind of like putting rocket fuel in a car engine. The acceleration is incredible, but slowly the engine pays the price. Because underneath all of the work ethic and the discipline, that fuel is fear. Usually a subconscious fear that says, If I don't achieve X, then I am not worthy of Y. X might be money, status, recognition, success. And Y is usually something much deeper, like love, respect, belonging, peace. And when your self-worth becomes tied to your achievement, the stakes become impossibly fucking high. Setbacks feel devastating. Switching off feels uncomfortable. Rest feels lazy. Nothing ever quite feels enough. Because you're not just trying to build something, You're trying to outrun something. But there is some good news. And the good news is there is another fuel source you can tap into if you so wish to. A fuel source that I refer to as clean fuel. Clean fuel, in a sentence, is this. I am already enough. So let's go use that enoughness to do something incredible with the limited time that I have been given. Clean fuel is this sweet cocktail of play, curiosity, service, mastery, and a genuine love of the game. You can move as fast as you like, but now it comes from wholeness, not lack. But here is the rather annoying part. You can't just think your way into clean fuel. The beliefs that formed your dirty fuel weren't formed logically. They were formed emotionally, usually through uncomfortable childhood experiences. Those experiences created subconscious beliefs about who you are, what you're worth, and what you need to do to feel enough. Those beliefs are your dirty fuel. And it is only on the other side of bringing those into awareness, and doing the work to rewire them, that you can tap into that clean fuel. Now a quick shameless plug. In my one-to-one coaching, I actually help overachievers uncover and reprogram those very subconscious beliefs, so that they can start operating from clean fuel. And I do so using hypnotherapy. I'm about to open a little bit more space, so if you want to know more, you can DM me the words clean fuel, and I'll send you more details."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "text": "#overachiever #self-worth #motivation"
        }
      ]
    },
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-08-09T11:07:34"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Db3gd7nR09D/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>4 Ways to Build Viral Sense</b>\n\n🪜 <b>How to</b>\n1. Hide engagement metrics while doom scrolling and guess the like and view count of each video to consciously assess its viral quality.\n2. On a video with a high ratio of comments, guess what people are commenting about before opening the comments — forces you to identify which element is most interesting to the average viewer.\n3. Paste a viral video link into a content AI like Stanley, ask it to transcribe the video and psychologically break down why it performed well using the Viral Content Analyzer prompt (comment &quot;guide&quot; under the original reel to receive the full prompt).\n4. On your feed and explore page, actively engage (like, comment, click Interested) only with viral videos and never engage with non-viral ones — trains the algorithm to show you only high-performing content.\n\n<i>Useful for: Content creators who want to train an intuitive sense for what makes videos go viral on social media.</i>\n\n💾 <i>Gives four actionable, non-obvious exercises for training viral intuition, including a specific on-screen AI prompt framework for reverse-engineering why any piece of content performed well.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/Db3gd7nR09D/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#viral-sense #content-strategy #ai-tools</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>4 Ways to Build Viral Sense</b><br>🪜 <b>How to</b><br><ol><li>Hide engagement metrics while doom scrolling and guess the like and view count of each video to consciously assess its viral quality.</li><li>On a video with a high ratio of comments, guess what people are commenting about before opening the comments — forces you to identify which element is most interesting to the average viewer.</li><li>Paste a viral video link into a content AI like Stanley, ask it to transcribe the video and psychologically break down why it performed well using the Viral Content Analyzer prompt (comment &quot;guide&quot; under the original reel to receive the full prompt).</li><li>On your feed and explore page, actively engage (like, comment, click Interested) only with viral videos and never engage with non-viral ones — trains the algorithm to show you only high-performing content.</li></ol><br><i>Useful for: Content creators who want to train an intuitive sense for what makes videos go viral on social media.</i><br>💾 <i>Gives four actionable, non-obvious exercises for training viral intuition, including a specific on-screen AI prompt framework for reverse-engineering why any piece of content performed well.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/Db3gd7nR09D/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#viral-sense #content-strategy #ai-tools</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Four practical drills to consciously develop viral intuition — from guessing metrics and comment sections to using an AI content tool (Stanley) with a detailed 5-section breakdown prompt, and training the Instagram algorithm to surface only high-performing videos.\n\n10 out of 10 ways to build disgustingly strong viral sense, but it gets increasingly more niche. When doom scrolling, hide the engagement metrics of a video and guess how many likes and views that video has in order to consciously assess its viral quality. When watching a video that is a high ratio of comments, try and guess what people are commenting about in the comment section before opening it up in order to force yourself to identify what element in the video is the most interesting to the average viewer. When coming across a viral video that you are unsure why performed well but want to use as inspiration, copy its link and paste it into an AI built for content like Stanley, then ask it to transcribe the video and psychologically break down and analyze why the video performed well using this prompt. If you want the prompt guide, just comment guide and I&#x27;ll send it over. And finally, when assessing your feed and explore page, actively engage with viral videos only by liking, commenting and clicking interested and don&#x27;t engage with non-viral videos. This forces your algorithm to only show you viral videos and if you watch viral videos long enough, your viral sense will develop.</details>",
    "blocks": {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "size": 1,
          "text": "4 Ways to Build Viral Sense"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "size": 3,
          "text": "🪜 How to"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "bold",
              "text": "1. "
            },
            "Hide engagement metrics while doom scrolling and guess the like and view count of each video to consciously assess its viral quality."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "bold",
              "text": "2. "
            },
            "On a video with a high ratio of comments, guess what people are commenting about before opening the comments — forces you to identify which element is most interesting to the average viewer."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "bold",
              "text": "3. "
            },
            "Paste a viral video link into a content AI like Stanley, ask it to transcribe the video and psychologically break down why it performed well using the Viral Content Analyzer prompt (comment \"guide\" under the original reel to receive the full prompt)."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "bold",
              "text": "4. "
            },
            "On your feed and explore page, actively engage (like, comment, click Interested) only with viral videos and never engage with non-viral ones — trains the algorithm to show you only high-performing content."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "italic",
              "text": "Useful for: Content creators who want to train an intuitive sense for what makes videos go viral on social media."
            }
