  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbX7zRcD9pJ/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>3 Hacks to Beat Doom Scrolling</b>\n<i>Three practical habits to fight doom scrolling: leave the house, block apps with Brick, and plan your day the night before with a checklist.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Leave the house — staying home creates comfort that makes all-day scrolling easy; being out keeps you moving and less phone-focused\n• Write a nightly to-do list (checklist) the night before to avoid an aimless day; checking off tasks removes the overwhelm that triggers doom scrolling\n\n💾 <i>Combines environmental design, a physical app-blocker, and nightly planning into a low-cost, actionable system for reducing phone overuse.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbX7zRcD9pJ/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#productivity #doomscrolling #focus</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>3 Hacks to Beat Doom Scrolling</b><br><i>Three practical habits to fight doom scrolling: leave the house, block apps with Brick, and plan your day the night before with a checklist.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Leave the house — staying home creates comfort that makes all-day scrolling easy; being out keeps you moving and less phone-focused</li><li>Write a nightly to-do list (checklist) the night before to avoid an aimless day; checking off tasks removes the overwhelm that triggers doom scrolling</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Combines environmental design, a physical app-blocker, and nightly planning into a low-cost, actionable system for reducing phone overuse.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbX7zRcD9pJ/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#productivity #doomscrolling #focus</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Three tips: (1) leave the house so you stop scrolling on the couch, (2) tap a Brick device before going out to block chosen apps until you return home, (3) write a checklist the night before so you have a plan to follow instead of reaching for your phone.\n\nHere are three productivity hacks that I use as a Berkeley student with a really bad doom scrolling addiction Like really bad first hack is I don&#x27;t stay home because then I get way too comfortable Just scrolling the entire day away on the couch You kind of lose track of time when you&#x27;re home sitting in the same position all day So leaving the house will literally just get you moving and it&#x27;ll make you less interested in looking at your phone The second thing i&#x27;ve been using recently is this thing called a brick If I tap my phone to it, it literally blocks all the apps on my phone that I choose And I can&#x27;t unblock them until I come home and re-tap the brick So for days where I know I want to enjoy my day and I don&#x27;t want to be on social media I leave the house and I tap the brick before I go out lastly something that really helps me is not having an aimless day What I mean by that is the night before I will literally use either apple notes or notion And write down a to-do list for my day with like a little checklist that I can check off Because it&#x27;s so satisfying to check off those boxes because when i&#x27;m overwhelmed with what tasks I want to do I find myself doom scrolling this prevents that because I just go through the list directly I used to be really good at staying off social media But this year it kind of took a turn for the worst and so i&#x27;m hoping to fix this Please comment any recommendations because it is a really big problem for me</details>",
    "blocks": {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "size": 1,
          "text": "3 Hacks to Beat Doom Scrolling"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "italic",
              "text": "Three practical habits to fight doom scrolling: leave the house, block apps with Brick, and plan your day the night before with a checklist."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "size": 3,
          "text": "🔑 Key points"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            {
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Leave the house — staying home creates comfort that makes all-day scrolling easy; being out keeps you moving and less phone-focused"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Write a nightly to-do list (checklist) the night before to avoid an aimless day; checking off tasks removes the overwhelm that triggers doom scrolling"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "blockquote",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "💾 Combines environmental design, a physical app-blocker, and nightly planning into a low-cost, actionable system for reducing phone overuse."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "divider"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "url",
              "text": "🔗 Original reel",
              "url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbX7zRcD9pJ/"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "details",
          "summary": "📄 Full transcript",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Three tips: (1) leave the house so you stop scrolling on the couch, (2) tap a Brick device before going out to block chosen apps until you return home, (3) write a checklist the night before so you have a plan to follow instead of reaching for your phone."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here are three productivity hacks that I use as a Berkeley student with a really bad doom scrolling addiction Like really bad first hack is I don't stay home because then I get way too comfortable Just scrolling the entire day away on the couch You kind of lose track of time when you're home sitting in the same position all day So leaving the house will literally just get you moving and it'll make you less interested in looking at your phone The second thing i've been using recently is this thing called a brick If I tap my phone to it, it literally blocks all the apps on my phone that I choose And I can't unblock them until I come home and re-tap the brick So for days where I know I want to enjoy my day and I don't want to be on social media I leave the house and I tap the brick before I go out lastly something that really helps me is not having an aimless day What I mean by that is the night before I will literally use either apple notes or notion And write down a to-do list for my day with like a little checklist that I can check off Because it's so satisfying to check off those boxes because when i'm overwhelmed with what tasks I want to do I find myself doom scrolling this prevents that because I just go through the list directly I used to be really good at staying off social media But this year it kind of took a turn for the worst and so i'm hoping to fix this Please comment any recommendations because it is a really big problem for me"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "text": "#productivity #doomscrolling #focus"
        }
      ]
    },
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-08-10T16:32:19"
  },
