  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYqj5y7xxdo/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>The Trivium Against Brain Rot</b>\n<i>The Trivium — an ancient framework of grammar, logic, and rhetoric — offers a 3-step antidote to information overload: consume in small doses, reflect by connecting ideas to your own life, then paraphrase everything into your own words.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Human knowledge now doubles every 12 hours, but our ability to process it keeps shrinking\n• Saving posts and consuming summaries at 2x speed creates the illusion of understanding, not real understanding\n• Plato warned over 2,000 years ago that relying too much on outside tools weakens our ability to think — AI accelerates the same problem\n• If you can&#x27;t explain a concept using your own stories and examples, you don&#x27;t fully understand it yet\n\n💾 <i>A concrete 3-step framework grounded in ancient classical education to fight information overload and rebuild the habit of original thought.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYqj5y7xxdo/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#brain-rot #learning #trivium</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>The Trivium Against Brain Rot</b><br><i>The Trivium — an ancient framework of grammar, logic, and rhetoric — offers a 3-step antidote to information overload: consume in small doses, reflect by connecting ideas to your own life, then paraphrase everything into your own words.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Human knowledge now doubles every 12 hours, but our ability to process it keeps shrinking</li><li>Saving posts and consuming summaries at 2x speed creates the illusion of understanding, not real understanding</li><li>Plato warned over 2,000 years ago that relying too much on outside tools weakens our ability to think — AI accelerates the same problem</li><li>If you can&#x27;t explain a concept using your own stories and examples, you don&#x27;t fully understand it yet</li></ul><br>💾 <i>A concrete 3-step framework grounded in ancient classical education to fight information overload and rebuild the habit of original thought.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYqj5y7xxdo/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#brain-rot #learning #trivium</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Knowledge doubles every 12 hours but our processing capacity hasn&#x27;t kept up — saving content and skimming summaries creates false understanding. The Trivium (grammar, logic, rhetoric), used in antiquity to train rigorous thinkers, maps to a modern practice: 15 minutes of input, reflection that connects ideas to your own life, then full paraphrase in your own words. You don&#x27;t truly understand an idea until you can explain it through your own stories and examples.\n\nStudies estimate that human knowledge used to double every century. Today, it&#x27;s predicted to double every 12 hours. But while information keeps growing, our ability to process it keeps shrinking. We save posts, collect AI summaries, and still struggle to form our own opinions. This isn&#x27;t new. Over 2,000 years ago, Plato warned that relying too much on outside tools would weaken our ability to think, and AI is only accelerating the problem. I&#x27;m often asked how I come up with content ideas, and I recently came across an old framework that puts it into words better than I ever could. It&#x27;s called the Trivium, the same system that trained minds like Marcus Aurelius and Aristotle. And the process is easier than you think. Start by consuming in small doses, 15 minutes max, then stop and write down what you remember. Next, take notes on how you feel about it. Connect it to something from your own life, like a book you&#x27;ve read, a conversation you had, or a pattern you keep seeing. The last step is where original thinking happens. Paraphrase all of it into your own words. Because if you can&#x27;t explain a concept using your own stories and examples, you might not fully understand it yet. And that&#x27;s the real danger of modern consumption. We&#x27;re collecting more ideas than ever, but processing almost none of them. And the muscle you don&#x27;t use is the one you lose. Comment, note, and I&#x27;ll send you the article that breaks this down.</details>",
    "blocks": {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "size": 1,
          "text": "The Trivium Against Brain Rot"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "italic",
              "text": "The Trivium — an ancient framework of grammar, logic, and rhetoric — offers a 3-step antidote to information overload: consume in small doses, reflect by connecting ideas to your own life, then paraphrase everything into your own words."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "size": 3,
          "text": "🔑 Key points"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
