        {
          "type": "details",
          "summary": "📄 Full transcript",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Three takeaways: cities serve two purposes (income and finding a partner); their high cost of living is social club membership you pay regardless of usage; the trap is working to afford staying there just to keep working. Original video: 'Use the city: get in, get out' by Orion Taraban."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So I just watched this video about how you need to use the city and I found it really interesting. I even took some notes for us. So I'm just gonna go ahead and give you what I think are the three most useful takeaways from it and something else the guy said at the end, which I think will give you a lot of perspective. I don't usually make content about this kind of topic, but I decided to anyway because I think it'll help some people in their 20s and 30s. So number one, cities are good for two things, making money and finding a partner. Once you have done both, get out. Two, the reason cities are so expensive and the cost of living is so high is because cities are basically social clubs and you're paying a premium to access the perks of that club. Things like bars, restaurants, social events, and yes, people as well, which goes back to point number one. And you're paying that premium regardless of whether or not you use those perks. Number three, and there's a reason I made this. Number three, you have to use the city strategically and intentionally or else it will use you. Now, something kind of funny that the guy said as well. Many of you will be working very hard to make enough money to live in the city another month, which will afford you the opportunity to work very hard to make enough money to live in the city another month. So I'm not trying to talk you out of moving to the city if you're a young guy or girl in your early 20s. And if you're already living in a city, I'm not saying you should pack up and leave tomorrow. I still live in the city myself, but have a goal and some intention behind what you're doing there. I still live in the city another month from John"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "text": "#city-living #intentionality #life-strategy"
        }
      ]
