  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbWmQjYPBvi/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Maffetone &amp; Norwegian Training Methods</b>\n<i>Use the Maffetone Method (180 minus your age as max HR) for easy runs to build aerobic efficiency, and the Norwegian Method for controlled intervals — together they build compounding speed you can actually recover from.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Maffetone Method: take 180 minus your age to get the heart rate ceiling for most of your easy runs\n• As aerobic fitness builds over weeks and months, you run faster and faster at that same heart rate\n• Norwegian Method: the goal is not to destroy yourself every workout — intervals should be controlled\n• Finish each interval session feeling like you had one more rep left in the tank\n• One method builds the aerobic base; the other builds speed you can actually recover from\n\n💾 <i>Concise breakdown of two complementary, research-backed aerobic training frameworks — Maffetone&#x27;s 180-minus-age formula and the Norwegian controlled-interval principle — with the actionable rules stated plainly.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbWmQjYPBvi/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#aerobic-base #running #heart-rate-training</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Maffetone &amp; Norwegian Training Methods</b><br><i>Use the Maffetone Method (180 minus your age as max HR) for easy runs to build aerobic efficiency, and the Norwegian Method for controlled intervals — together they build compounding speed you can actually recover from.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Maffetone Method: take 180 minus your age to get the heart rate ceiling for most of your easy runs</li><li>As aerobic fitness builds over weeks and months, you run faster and faster at that same heart rate</li><li>Norwegian Method: the goal is not to destroy yourself every workout — intervals should be controlled</li><li>Finish each interval session feeling like you had one more rep left in the tank</li><li>One method builds the aerobic base; the other builds speed you can actually recover from</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Concise breakdown of two complementary, research-backed aerobic training frameworks — Maffetone&#x27;s 180-minus-age formula and the Norwegian controlled-interval principle — with the actionable rules stated plainly.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbWmQjYPBvi/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#aerobic-base #running #heart-rate-training</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Two complementary training methods: Maffetone uses 180 minus your age as the heart rate cap for easy runs — aerobic efficiency improves so you run faster at the same HR over time. The Norwegian Method prescribes intervals controlled enough that you could always do one more rep, building speed you can sustain and recover from.</details>",
    "blocks": {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "size": 1,
          "text": "Maffetone & Norwegian Training Methods"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "italic",
              "text": "Use the Maffetone Method (180 minus your age as max HR) for easy runs to build aerobic efficiency, and the Norwegian Method for controlled intervals — together they build compounding speed you can actually recover from."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "size": 3,
          "text": "🔑 Key points"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            {
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Maffetone Method: take 180 minus your age to get the heart rate ceiling for most of your easy runs"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "As aerobic fitness builds over weeks and months, you run faster and faster at that same heart rate"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Norwegian Method: the goal is not to destroy yourself every workout — intervals should be controlled"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Finish each interval session feeling like you had one more rep left in the tank"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "One method builds the aerobic base; the other builds speed you can actually recover from"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "blockquote",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "💾 Concise breakdown of two complementary, research-backed aerobic training frameworks — Maffetone's 180-minus-age formula and the Norwegian controlled-interval principle — with the actionable rules stated plainly."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "divider"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "url",
              "text": "🔗 Original reel",
              "url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbWmQjYPBvi/"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "details",
          "summary": "📄 Full transcript",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Two complementary training methods: Maffetone uses 180 minus your age as the heart rate cap for easy runs — aerobic efficiency improves so you run faster at the same HR over time. The Norwegian Method prescribes intervals controlled enough that you could always do one more rep, building speed you can sustain and recover from."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "text": "#aerobic-base #running #heart-rate-training"
        }
      ]
    },
