  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcNgSjahG2_/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Martial Arts Trains Prefrontal Cortex</b>\n<i>Consistent martial arts training physically strengthens the prefrontal cortex, improving executive function and self-control over time.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Holding back power and checking yourself mid-technique during drills is repeated impulse-control exercise that trains the brain&#x27;s control circuitry.\n• Practitioners&#x27; calmer stress responses are neurological, not personality — the prefrontal cortex has grown stronger through training.\n• The shift isn&#x27;t &#x27;nothing gets to you&#x27; — it&#x27;s that you have built the capacity to choose your response rather than react.\n\n💾 <i>Explains the neuroscience mechanism behind why martial arts training builds self-control — concrete framing for understanding how deliberate practice reshapes the brain.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcNgSjahG2_/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#neuroscience #self-control #martial-arts</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Martial Arts Trains Prefrontal Cortex</b><br><i>Consistent martial arts training physically strengthens the prefrontal cortex, improving executive function and self-control over time.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Holding back power and checking yourself mid-technique during drills is repeated impulse-control exercise that trains the brain&#x27;s control circuitry.</li><li>Practitioners&#x27; calmer stress responses are neurological, not personality — the prefrontal cortex has grown stronger through training.</li><li>The shift isn&#x27;t &#x27;nothing gets to you&#x27; — it&#x27;s that you have built the capacity to choose your response rather than react.</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Explains the neuroscience mechanism behind why martial arts training builds self-control — concrete framing for understanding how deliberate practice reshapes the brain.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcNgSjahG2_/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#neuroscience #self-control #martial-arts</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Martial arts drills (holding back power, checking technique mid-move) repeatedly exercise impulse control, which physically strengthens the prefrontal cortex over time. The improved stress response practitioners notice outside the gym is a neurological change, not a personality trait.\n\nYour hobby tells me something about your personality. If you train in martial arts long-term, you&#x27;ve probably experienced something called executive function. This is different from what happens in the moment during a fight or a spa. Science shows that consistent martial arts training actually strengthens your prefrontal cortex over time. That&#x27;s the part of your brain that&#x27;s responsible for self-control, impulse control, and thinking before you react. Every drill where you hold back power, every rep where you check yourself mid-technique, is your brain training that control like a muscle. You&#x27;re not necessarily calmer by nature. It&#x27;s that your prefrontal cortex has been getting stronger every single class. This is why practitioners react to stress outside of the gym differently than people who have never trained. It&#x27;s not that nothing gets to you. It&#x27;s that you have built the control to dictate your response. If you found this interesting, and you like learning about how your brain operates with stress, go to poke.com, use the Stress Tracker recipe, and poke will text you every single day with ways in which you can manage, track, and reduce your stress.</details>",
    "blocks": {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "size": 1,
          "text": "Martial Arts Trains Prefrontal Cortex"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "italic",
              "text": "Consistent martial arts training physically strengthens the prefrontal cortex, improving executive function and self-control over time."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "size": 3,
          "text": "🔑 Key points"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            {
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Holding back power and checking yourself mid-technique during drills is repeated impulse-control exercise that trains the brain's control circuitry."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Practitioners' calmer stress responses are neurological, not personality — the prefrontal cortex has grown stronger through training."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The shift isn't 'nothing gets to you' — it's that you have built the capacity to choose your response rather than react."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "blockquote",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "💾 Explains the neuroscience mechanism behind why martial arts training builds self-control — concrete framing for understanding how deliberate practice reshapes the brain."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "divider"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": [
            {
              "type": "url",
              "text": "🔗 Original reel",
              "url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcNgSjahG2_/"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "details",
          "summary": "📄 Full transcript",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Martial arts drills (holding back power, checking technique mid-move) repeatedly exercise impulse control, which physically strengthens the prefrontal cortex over time. The improved stress response practitioners notice outside the gym is a neurological change, not a personality trait."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Your hobby tells me something about your personality. If you train in martial arts long-term, you've probably experienced something called executive function. This is different from what happens in the moment during a fight or a spa. Science shows that consistent martial arts training actually strengthens your prefrontal cortex over time. That's the part of your brain that's responsible for self-control, impulse control, and thinking before you react. Every drill where you hold back power, every rep where you check yourself mid-technique, is your brain training that control like a muscle. You're not necessarily calmer by nature. It's that your prefrontal cortex has been getting stronger every single class. This is why practitioners react to stress outside of the gym differently than people who have never trained. It's not that nothing gets to you. It's that you have built the control to dictate your response. If you found this interesting, and you like learning about how your brain operates with stress, go to poke.com, use the Stress Tracker recipe, and poke will text you every single day with ways in which you can manage, track, and reduce your stress."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "text": "#neuroscience #self-control #martial-arts"
        }
      ]
