  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcR2L-FOXL3/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "“<b>Failing doesn&#x27;t just mean you got something wrong. Failing means that you got separated and everyone is finding out.</b>”\n— beefcakemaxxing\n\n<i>Your fear of failure isn&#x27;t about the odds — it&#x27;s a childhood-conditioned fear of public separation, and the only rational response is volume: you can&#x27;t predict which attempt wins, so the only lever you control is how many exist.</i>\n💡 <b>Post about things you&#x27;re already doing anyway, at volume — you have no crystal ball, so make more.</b>\n\n💾 <i>Reframes failure-aversion as a childhood social-conditioning response rather than a rational signal — directly explains why you already know volume works but still don&#x27;t act.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcR2L-FOXL3/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#failure #volume #creator-mindset</i>",
    "markdown": "“<b>Failing doesn&#x27;t just mean you got something wrong. Failing means that you got separated and everyone is finding out.</b>”<br>— beefcakemaxxing<br><i>Your fear of failure isn&#x27;t about the odds — it&#x27;s a childhood-conditioned fear of public separation, and the only rational response is volume: you can&#x27;t predict which attempt wins, so the only lever you control is how many exist.</i><br>💡 <b>Post about things you&#x27;re already doing anyway, at volume — you have no crystal ball, so make more.</b><br>💾 <i>Reframes failure-aversion as a childhood social-conditioning response rather than a rational signal — directly explains why you already know volume works but still don&#x27;t act.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcR2L-FOXL3/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#failure #volume #creator-mindset</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Your definition of failure was set in 1st grade: a bad grade didn&#x27;t just mean getting something wrong, it meant being publicly moved to a different group while everyone watched. That fear of public separation — not the actual odds — is what stops you from posting. The fix is volume. Angry Birds was Rovio&#x27;s 52nd game. Dyson built 5,127 failed prototypes. The creator posted 186 times before a grocery run hit 4M views. Not one of them knew which attempt would win. The only lever you control is how many attempts exist.\n\nYou keep saying you want the truth about this, so here you go. The famous game Angry Birds was Robio&#x27;s 52nd game. The first 51 before that completely flopped. They were on the verge of bankruptcy. The very next game they make is one of the most popular mobile games ever played on Earth. That&#x27;s a 98% fail rate. The guy who invented the Dyson vacuum, the one that&#x27;s in your parents&#x27; house right now, 5,127 models that didn&#x27;t do shit. The very next one is a prototype that is still existing today. Hear this out, 99.98% fail rate. That&#x27;s almost like not even comprehensible. I posted 186 times in 6 months starting social media. All I had to show for it, 426 followers, barely scraping hundreds of views. I go to Costco, make a reel about, dates, 4 million views. You know what all three had in common? Not a single one of us knew that was going to be the one. That is why volume works because you don&#x27;t have a crystal fucking ball, bro. You don&#x27;t know which one it&#x27;s going to be. The only lever that you control is how many exist. But the thing is, you already knew this anyway. You&#x27;ve seen the stories. You&#x27;ve gotten motivated. You&#x27;ve been inspired and you still don&#x27;t take action. Why is that? Because it has been ingrained in you since you were 6 years old. You got a bad grade and it wasn&#x27;t just getting a bad grade. You got moved. You got put in a different reading group, a different table. And everyone watched it happen. That&#x27;s the ingraining process. So failing doesn&#x27;t just mean you got something wrong. Failing means that you got separated and everyone is finding out. That is the repercussion. You fast forward 20 years later, you&#x27;re not just scared to fail. You&#x27;re scared of being put back in that room, being judged in front of everyone. That matters to you. I showed up every single day in a room that honestly was just to myself. Six months for a crowd that didn&#x27;t even exist. To Costco, to do a normal thing, get groceries, film a 30-second video. And it completely changed my perspective on what failing is. Film the shit that interests you that you&#x27;re already doing anyway. Because let me ask you this. You read these three lines that are up here again. And tell me that the odds is what&#x27;s really stopping you from doing what you want to do.</details>",
