  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbeHJEBjkfu/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Martial Arts Tier List Real Life</b>\n<i>A practical tier ranking of martial arts by real-world effectiveness and speed to usable results.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• S tier — BJJ: effective across self-defense and competition, results show up quickly in real situations\n• S tier — Wrestling: limited move set but highly effective; you get good fast\n• A tier — Boxing: fundamental skill, essential to have, but you get rusty without consistent training\n• A tier — Judo: similar to wrestling but larger technique library; needs more consistent training to pull together\n• A tier — Muay Thai: same format as boxing — necessary, but requires consistent practice\n• B tier — Karate: longer commitment to get good, but proven effective (Lyoto Machida&#x27;s UFC knockouts as evidence)\n• C tier — Krav Maga: some good techniques but too conditional (groin strikes, eye gouges) for most situations\n• C tier — Kung Fu: good body movement patterns but not broadly applicable\n• D tier — Competition-only styles (YouTube martial arts): no real-life value\n• D tier — Pressure-point techniques: dismissed entirely as ineffective\n\n💾 <i>Practical tier ranking to decide which martial art to invest training time in first.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbeHJEBjkfu/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#martial-arts #self-defense #fitness</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Martial Arts Tier List Real Life</b><br><i>A practical tier ranking of martial arts by real-world effectiveness and speed to usable results.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>S tier — BJJ: effective across self-defense and competition, results show up quickly in real situations</li><li>S tier — Wrestling: limited move set but highly effective; you get good fast</li><li>A tier — Boxing: fundamental skill, essential to have, but you get rusty without consistent training</li><li>A tier — Judo: similar to wrestling but larger technique library; needs more consistent training to pull together</li><li>A tier — Muay Thai: same format as boxing — necessary, but requires consistent practice</li><li>B tier — Karate: longer commitment to get good, but proven effective (Lyoto Machida&#x27;s UFC knockouts as evidence)</li><li>C tier — Krav Maga: some good techniques but too conditional (groin strikes, eye gouges) for most situations</li><li>C tier — Kung Fu: good body movement patterns but not broadly applicable</li><li>D tier — Competition-only styles (YouTube martial arts): no real-life value</li><li>D tier — Pressure-point techniques: dismissed entirely as ineffective</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Practical tier ranking to decide which martial art to invest training time in first.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbeHJEBjkfu/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#martial-arts #self-defense #fitness</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Jasonyerrington ranks martial arts for practical real-life use: BJJ and Wrestling are S tier for fast, applicable results; Boxing, Judo, and Muay Thai are A tier but need consistent training; Karate is B tier with a longer learning curve but proven UFC results; Krav Maga and Kung Fu are C tier for being too situational; competition-only styles and pressure-point techniques are D tier and dismissed as ineffective.\n\nBJJ. S tier. Super effective in really all kinds of situations for real people. From self-defense to competition, little time required to get to where you actually see the results of your training showing up in real life experiences. Most of what you see on YouTube? Competition. No. D tier. 100% D tier. I don&#x27;t even want to talk about it. Boxing. Boxing A tier. Boxing is a necessary skill. Take some time. You got to have consistent training. You do get a little rusty with it. However, like the dynamics and fundamentals of boxing have to be there. Krav Maga. Krav Maga, I would consider that C tier. They have some good stuff, but it&#x27;s super conditional. Groin strikes, eye gouges, really conditional for the situations that you find yourself in. Wrestling. Wrestling, I would call that S tier. There&#x27;s not a lot of moves in wrestling. However, they&#x27;re super effective and you can get good at them pretty fast. Anything that involves pressure points as the primary technique. Let me just stop you right there. D tier. And if there was a lower tier, I would put it there. Judo. Judo, I would call that A tier. I kind of put judo in the same category as wrestling. However, there are a lot more takedowns and a lot more of a library of techniques to go. So you need to have some consistent training to kind of put it all together. Karate B tier. A longer commitment to get good at it. However, super effective. Loyola Machida showed us all in the UFC how legit it could be with some of the most prolific knockouts in the history. Kung Fu, I would call that C tier. Super conditional. Good movements for the body. Good movement patterns. But again, it&#x27;s not really going to be applicable everywhere. Muay Thai. Muay Thai, A tier. I kind of put it in the same like format as boxing. Super necessary. Got to have it. But need to do some consistent training to really get good at it.</details>",
