              "text": "BJJ. S tier. Super effective in really all kinds of situations for real people. From self-defense to competition, little time required to get to where you actually see the results of your training showing up in real life experiences. Most of what you see on YouTube? Competition. No. D tier. 100% D tier. I don't even want to talk about it. Boxing. Boxing A tier. Boxing is a necessary skill. Take some time. You got to have consistent training. You do get a little rusty with it. However, like the dynamics and fundamentals of boxing have to be there. Krav Maga. Krav Maga, I would consider that C tier. They have some good stuff, but it's super conditional. Groin strikes, eye gouges, really conditional for the situations that you find yourself in. Wrestling. Wrestling, I would call that S tier. There's not a lot of moves in wrestling. However, they're super effective and you can get good at them pretty fast. Anything that involves pressure points as the primary technique. Let me just stop you right there. D tier. And if there was a lower tier, I would put it there. Judo. Judo, I would call that A tier. I kind of put judo in the same category as wrestling. However, there are a lot more takedowns and a lot more of a library of techniques to go. So you need to have some consistent training to kind of put it all together. Karate B tier. A longer commitment to get good at it. However, super effective. Loyola Machida showed us all in the UFC how legit it could be with some of the most prolific knockouts in the history. Kung Fu, I would call that C tier. Super conditional. Good movements for the body. Good movement patterns. But again, it's not really going to be applicable everywhere. Muay Thai. Muay Thai, A tier. I kind of put it in the same like format as boxing. Super necessary. Got to have it. But need to do some consistent training to really get good at it."
