  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcLhEeyidmW/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "“<b>The mileage he ran will have you questioning absolutely everything.</b>”\n— cam_duty\n\n<i>Mark Dowdle (USA) won the Last Soul Ultra by completing 95 loops — 395 miles (637 km) — over roughly four days, outlasting 159 other starters in a last-person-standing backyard ultra format.</i>\n💡 <b>Watching someone run 395 miles non-stop reframes what a human limit actually is.</b>\n\n💾 <i>Extreme benchmark for human endurance — 395 miles across 95 hourly loops over four-plus days, with a race format that produces only one true finisher.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcLhEeyidmW/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#ultrarunning #endurance #human-performance</i>",
    "markdown": "“<b>The mileage he ran will have you questioning absolutely everything.</b>”<br>— cam_duty<br><i>Mark Dowdle (USA) won the Last Soul Ultra by completing 95 loops — 395 miles (637 km) — over roughly four days, outlasting 159 other starters in a last-person-standing backyard ultra format.</i><br>💡 <b>Watching someone run 395 miles non-stop reframes what a human limit actually is.</b><br>💾 <i>Extreme benchmark for human endurance — 395 miles across 95 hourly loops over four-plus days, with a race format that produces only one true finisher.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcLhEeyidmW/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#ultrarunning #endurance #human-performance</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>At the Last Soul Ultra, runners completed a 4.2-mile loop every hour until only one remained. After nearly four full days, Mark Dowdle won with 395 miles across 95 loops — employing sprint starts and psychological intimidation tactics against his last opponent. A women&#x27;s world record was also broken during the race. The video is a short recap by @cam_duty celebrating the Backyard Ultra format.\n\nWhat did we just witness? Friday at 2 p.m. 160 runners set out on a 4.2 mile loop every hour for the last Soul Ultra. And about one hour ago on Tuesday morning in Germany, only one person remained and will be crowned champion. The mileage he ran will have you questioning absolutely everything. Throughout this race, we saw the women&#x27;s world record be broken. We got to see some people run the furthest they&#x27;ve ever ran, and many incredible other souls take the course. But for the last 12 hours, we saw two men battle it out. Mark from the USA and Elo from Germany. There were many reports that Mark was sprinting off the start line at every loop, and making howling noises to intimidate his opponent in the middle of the night. Dog. In the end, Mark Dowdle pulled out the win, covering 395 miles or 637 kilometers over 95 loops. But Elo, who took second place, deserves all the credit for running an absolutely crazy race. Absolutely insane stuff, congrats to all of the racers, and long live the Backyard Ultra. If you love endurance sports and want to watch more stuff like this, then hit that follow button.</details>",
