  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcUOo83h6j6/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "“<b>It doesn&#x27;t matter who shows up. It won&#x27;t change what I do.</b>”\n— therunningeffect\n\n<i>Competing only against yourself is the only strategy that can never be used against you.</i>\n💡 <b>You can&#x27;t break a man who never handed you the leverage.</b>\n\n💾 <i>It names the exact mechanism by which internal commitment beats external competition — and gives it a form you can apply anywhere, not just in ultrarunning.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcUOo83h6j6/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#ultrarunning #mindset #self-commitment</i>",
    "markdown": "“<b>It doesn&#x27;t matter who shows up. It won&#x27;t change what I do.</b>”<br>— therunningeffect<br><i>Competing only against yourself is the only strategy that can never be used against you.</i><br>💡 <b>You can&#x27;t break a man who never handed you the leverage.</b><br>💾 <i>It names the exact mechanism by which internal commitment beats external competition — and gives it a form you can apply anywhere, not just in ultrarunning.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcUOo83h6j6/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#ultrarunning #mindset #self-commitment</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>169 runners started a Backyard Ultra in Germany. 94 hours later, Mark Dowdle was the only one still moving: 391 miles, 4 days, under 13 hours of rest. His edge was mental — he never needed anyone else to quit, because he was keeping a promise to himself, not racing the field. His fastest lap came on Loop 87. Harvey Lewis didn&#x27;t survive Loop 50. The reel&#x27;s argument: a Backyard Ultra isn&#x27;t ended by miles, it&#x27;s ended by the moment you need the other person to stop — and Mark never reached that moment.\n\nThe last sole standing in Germany was the only guy out there not racing anybody. 169 runners started last Friday. 94 hours later, Mark Dowdle was the only one still moving. 391 miles, 4 days, under 13 hours of rest total. Harvey Lewis didn&#x27;t make Loop 50, and Mark&#x27;s fastest lap of the entire race came on Loop 87. Months ago, he sat across from me and said, It doesn&#x27;t matter who shows up. It won&#x27;t change what I do. Everybody else out there was doing math on the other guy. Mark was keeping a promise to himself. That&#x27;s what actually ends a Backyard Ultra. Not the miles, but the moment you need the other guy to quit. He never needed anybody to quit, because you can&#x27;t break a man who never handed you the leverage. And that&#x27;s exactly why.</details>",
