  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcnAYnNBOPr/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>Three Phases of Multi-Passionate Mastery</b>\n<i>Multi-passionate people reach mastery not by picking one thing forever, but by moving through three phases — exploring widely, committing to a few survivors, then combining those domains into a unique edge.</i>\n\n🔑 <b>Key points</b>\n• Phase 1 — Curiosity: follow every itch and develop taste; valid as a starting point, but the danger is never leaving it\n• Phase 2 — Commitment: accept you can&#x27;t do everything; narrow to 2–3 interests that survived exploration and build depth through reps without visible results; choosing feels like losing options, but depth is the price of entry to phase 3\n• Phase 3 — Combination: mastery for multi-passionates comes from crossing domains (psychology + filmmaking, business + storytelling, design + technology), not one-track specialization; the combination is the edge\n\n💾 <i>Reframes having too many interests as a three-phase process with a concrete payoff in phase 3, rather than a character flaw.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcnAYnNBOPr/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#multi-passionate #mastery #learning</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>Three Phases of Multi-Passionate Mastery</b><br><i>Multi-passionate people reach mastery not by picking one thing forever, but by moving through three phases — exploring widely, committing to a few survivors, then combining those domains into a unique edge.</i><br>🔑 <b>Key points</b><br><ul><li>Phase 1 — Curiosity: follow every itch and develop taste; valid as a starting point, but the danger is never leaving it</li><li>Phase 2 — Commitment: accept you can&#x27;t do everything; narrow to 2–3 interests that survived exploration and build depth through reps without visible results; choosing feels like losing options, but depth is the price of entry to phase 3</li><li>Phase 3 — Combination: mastery for multi-passionates comes from crossing domains (psychology + filmmaking, business + storytelling, design + technology), not one-track specialization; the combination is the edge</li></ul><br>💾 <i>Reframes having too many interests as a three-phase process with a concrete payoff in phase 3, rather than a character flaw.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcnAYnNBOPr/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#multi-passionate #mastery #learning</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Multi-passionate people tend to get stuck in the curiosity phase, cycling through hobbies. Phase two requires narrowing to 2–3 interests and building depth through reps without visible rewards. Phase three — where mastery actually lives — comes from combining those domains into a unique edge. Donald Glover is cited: ~5 years in a writer&#x27;s room, ~7 years doing comedy, ~3 years making music, in deliberate sequence rather than simultaneously.\n\nIf you think being multi-passionate means you&#x27;ll never really be great at anything, this might change your mind. I came across a Substack article about mastery that reframed how I think about getting good at things. There are three phases to it, and most people never get past the first one. Let&#x27;s break it down. If you have a new hobby every month, you&#x27;re probably here. This is the curiosity phase, so you follow the itch. Filmmaking, knitting, running, whatever sparks your interest. It&#x27;s not really a bad thing because you&#x27;re developing taste, but the danger is most people never leave. Phase two is where curiosity becomes commitment. At some point, you have to accept that you can&#x27;t do everything. You take the two or three things that survived exploration and build depth. The exciting part is over, so you focus on reps without visible results, which makes it hard not to quit. Choosing feels like losing options, but depth is the price you pay to enter the last phase. This is where we&#x27;ve been taught mastery wrong. We think becoming great means picking one thing and doing it forever. But for multi-passionates, mastery comes from combining domains. Psychology and filmmaking, business and storytelling, design and technology. The combination becomes your edge. The author uses Donald Glover as the perfect example. Five years in a writer&#x27;s room, seven years doing comedy, and three years making music. Rather than doing everything, he did everything worth doing. So if you&#x27;re multi-passionate, don&#x27;t kill your curiosity. Explore widely, go deep on what sticks, then connect the dots. Comment mastery and I&#x27;ll send you the full substack.</details>",
    "blocks": {
