              "text": "Multi-passionate people tend to get stuck in the curiosity phase, cycling through hobbies. Phase two requires narrowing to 2–3 interests and building depth through reps without visible rewards. Phase three — where mastery actually lives — comes from combining those domains into a unique edge. Donald Glover is cited: ~5 years in a writer's room, ~7 years doing comedy, ~3 years making music, in deliberate sequence rather than simultaneously."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If you think being multi-passionate means you'll never really be great at anything, this might change your mind. I came across a Substack article about mastery that reframed how I think about getting good at things. There are three phases to it, and most people never get past the first one. Let's break it down. If you have a new hobby every month, you're probably here. This is the curiosity phase, so you follow the itch. Filmmaking, knitting, running, whatever sparks your interest. It's not really a bad thing because you're developing taste, but the danger is most people never leave. Phase two is where curiosity becomes commitment. At some point, you have to accept that you can't do everything. You take the two or three things that survived exploration and build depth. The exciting part is over, so you focus on reps without visible results, which makes it hard not to quit. Choosing feels like losing options, but depth is the price you pay to enter the last phase. This is where we've been taught mastery wrong. We think becoming great means picking one thing and doing it forever. But for multi-passionates, mastery comes from combining domains. Psychology and filmmaking, business and storytelling, design and technology. The combination becomes your edge. The author uses Donald Glover as the perfect example. Five years in a writer's room, seven years doing comedy, and three years making music. Rather than doing everything, he did everything worth doing. So if you're multi-passionate, don't kill your curiosity. Explore widely, go deep on what sticks, then connect the dots. Comment mastery and I'll send you the full substack."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "text": "#multi-passionate #mastery #learning"
        }
      ]
    },
    "platform": "instagram",
    "ts": "2026-08-29T10:30:35"
  },
  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dcj8jJZAs7g/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>7 Sources That Explain the World</b>\n<i>“sources of information that explain how the world actually works and the forces that shape it, not just the headlines that don&#x27;t have a lot of context”</i>\n\n📌 <b>Recommended</b>\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.wsj.com/podcasts/the-journal\">The Journal</a> — Ryan Knutson / WSJ\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/frontline/\">PBS Frontline</a> — PBS\n⚠️ <a href=\"https://www.bloomberg.com/opinion/authors/ARbTQlRLRjE/matthew-s-levine\">Money Stuff</a> — Matt Levine — <i>Bloomberg newsletter by Matt Levine; exact canonical URL not confirmed — live search unavailable</i>\n✅ <a href=\"https://stratechery.com/\">Stratechery</a> — Ben Thompson\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.acquired.fm/\">Acquired</a> — Ben Gilbert and David Rosenthal\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.economist.com/\">The Economist</a> — The Economist Group\n⚠️ <a href=\"https://www.searchengine.show/\">Search Engine</a> — PJ Vogt — <i>PJ Vogt&#x27;s podcast; URL not confirmed — live search unavailable</i>\n✅ <a href=\"https://www.readtangle.com/\">Tangle</a> — Isaac Saul\n\n💾 <i>A curated shortlist from a veteran WSJ journalist of the newsletters and podcasts that build genuine understanding of how the world works, not just what happened.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dcj8jJZAs7g/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#media-diet #journalism #podcasts</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>7 Sources That Explain the World</b><br><i>“sources of information that explain how the world actually works and the forces that shape it, not just the headlines that don&#x27;t have a lot of context”</i><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.wsj.com/podcasts/the-journal\">The Journal</a> — Ryan Knutson / WSJ</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/frontline/\">PBS Frontline</a> — PBS</li><li>⚠️ <a href=\"https://www.bloomberg.com/opinion/authors/ARbTQlRLRjE/matthew-s-levine\">Money Stuff</a> — Matt Levine — <i>Bloomberg newsletter by Matt Levine; exact canonical URL not confirmed — live search unavailable</i></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://stratechery.com/\">Stratechery</a> — Ben Thompson</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.acquired.fm/\">Acquired</a> — Ben Gilbert and David Rosenthal</li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.economist.com/\">The Economist</a> — The Economist Group</li><li>⚠️ <a href=\"https://www.searchengine.show/\">Search Engine</a> — PJ Vogt — <i>PJ Vogt&#x27;s podcast; URL not confirmed — live search unavailable</i></li><li>✅ <a href=\"https://www.readtangle.com/\">Tangle</a> — Isaac Saul</li></ol><br>💾 <i>A curated shortlist from a veteran WSJ journalist of the newsletters and podcasts that build genuine understanding of how the world works, not just what happened.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dcj8jJZAs7g/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#media-diet #journalism #podcasts</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>A ranked list of 7 information sources: PBS Frontline (#7), Matt Levine&#x27;s Money Stuff (#6), Stratechery (#5), Acquired podcast (#4), The Economist (#3), Search Engine with PJ Vogt (#2), and Tangle by Isaac Saul (#1). The common thread: each provides historical context and structural explanation rather than surface-level coverage.\n\nSo this video I made recently went viral about how people should look for sources of information that explain how the world actually works and the forces that shape it, not just the headlines that don&#x27;t have a lot of context about the political horse race. So I decided to put together my top seven favorite sources of information that I think do this extremely well, aside from the journal podcast obviously, which is the best. Number seven is PBS Frontline. This is the gold standard for investigative documentaries. I started watching the show in high school and it completely opened my mind to how the world actually works. I worked there for a few years after college and it is the best. Number six is Matt Levine and Money Stuff. It&#x27;s a newsletter from Bloomberg. He is so good at breaking down complex topics in the most layman&#x27;s terms possible. Number five is Ben Thompson of Stratechery. This is another newsletter and Ben Thompson is so good at explaining the forces that drive a company. How does it fundamentally make money? What is it going to do to try to protect itself in a given situation? And he really does an amazing job of projecting forward about how businesses are going to respond to different forces and how industries sort of collide and how they work together. And he puts that all in the context of the news. It&#x27;s excellent. Number five is Acquired. This is a very long podcast. It&#x27;s like three to four hours per episode, but it gives the full history. Companies don&#x27;t just exist in a vacuum. They don&#x27;t just appear out of nowhere. They were built by people who had ambitions, who faced challenges, who solved those challenges, who saw threats and built their businesses in certain ways to become powerful and how they want to hold on to power. Number three, The Economist. This is a weekly publication. And I feel like every article in The Economist is just written a little bit slower and a little bit more zoomed out. And it gives you just better context generally about how a news story fits into the bigger picture of what&#x27;s going on in an industry. When I started working at The Wall Street Journal, I felt like I needed to understand the business world better. And I realized that The Economist did a really good job of putting news in context. And I found it was so helpful in making me smarter in the world of business that I made sure that I read it every Saturday morning and I still read it to this day. Number two is Search Engine with PJ Vogt. This is just a super entertaining podcast that always has history in mind. One of my favorite episodes recently was about the SpaceX IPO. And PJ, to put the SpaceX IPO into context, he went back and told the whole story of the history of the stock market. Why was it invented? What was the problem that it was invented to solve? How did it grow over time? And how does this wild SpaceX IPO fit into the context of what&#x27;s ultimate purpose? This kind of journalism, I think, is just so great at helping you understand how the world works. Number one, and this is a source that I almost guaranteed that nobody has heard of. It&#x27;s called Tangle. It&#x27;s a newsletter written by Isaac Saul. And I think that if everybody in America read this, the world would be a better place. A lot of people get famous because they&#x27;re super left-wing or super right-wing. And Isaac Saul&#x27;s point is to be fiercely neutral. His newsletter says, this is what the left is saying about the situation. This is what the right is saying about the situation. And here&#x27;s my take. Here is what I think, I think what arguments hold water based on the actual evidence. All right, those are my favorite sources. If you&#x27;ve got others you think I missed, please drop those in the comments.</details>",
