 context generally about how a news story fits into the bigger picture of what's going on in an industry. When I started working at The Wall Street Journal, I felt like I needed to understand the business world better. And I realized that The Economist did a really good job of putting news in context. And I found it was so helpful in making me smarter in the world of business that I made sure that I read it every Saturday morning and I still read it to this day. Number two is Search Engine with PJ Vogt. This is just a super entertaining podcast that always has history in mind. One of my favorite episodes recently was about the SpaceX IPO. And PJ, to put the SpaceX IPO into context, he went back and told the whole story of the history of the stock market. Why was it invented? What was the problem that it was invented to solve? How did it grow over time? And how does this wild SpaceX IPO fit into the context of what's ultimate purpose? This kind of journalism, I think, is just so great at helping you understand how the world works. Number one, and this is a source that I almost guaranteed that nobody has heard of. It's called Tangle. It's a newsletter written by Isaac Saul. And I think that if everybody in America read this, the world would be a better place. A lot of people get famous because they're super left-wing or super right-wing. And Isaac Saul's point is to be fiercely neutral. His newsletter says, this is what the left is saying about the situation. This is what the right is saying about the situation. And here's my take. Here is what I think, I think what arguments hold water based on the actual evidence. All right, those are my favorite sources. If you've got others you think I missed, please drop those in the comments."
