  "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbHFR2nJ0FI/": {
    "status": "done",
    "digest": "<b>3 Men to Study in Your 20s</b>\n<i>“You have no control over your circumstances and only how you respond to them.”</i>\n\n📌 <b>Recommended</b>\n⚠️ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lee_Kuan_Yew\">Lee Kuan Yew</a> — <i>Search tools unavailable; URL based on training knowledge — highly likely correct but unverified.</i>\n⚠️ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nobu_Matsuhisa\">Nobuyuki Matsuhisa (Nobu)</a> — <i>Search tools unavailable; URL based on training knowledge — highly likely correct but unverified.</i>\n⚠️ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viktor_Frankl\">Viktor Frankl</a> — <i>Search tools unavailable; URL based on training knowledge — highly likely correct but unverified.</i>\n⚠️ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/4069.Man_s_Search_for_Meaning\">Man&#x27;s Search for Meaning</a> — Viktor Frankl — <i>Search tools unavailable; URL based on training knowledge — highly likely correct but unverified.</i>\n\n💾 <i>A concise, high-signal list of three non-obvious figures whose life stories model long-term thinking, resilience through prolonged failure, and mastery of attitude toward suffering.</i>\n🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbHFR2nJ0FI/\">Original reel</a>\n<i>#resilience #role-models #long-term-thinking</i>",
    "markdown": "<b>3 Men to Study in Your 20s</b><br><i>“You have no control over your circumstances and only how you respond to them.”</i><br>📌 <b>Recommended</b><br><ol><li>⚠️ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lee_Kuan_Yew\">Lee Kuan Yew</a> — <i>Search tools unavailable; URL based on training knowledge — highly likely correct but unverified.</i></li><li>⚠️ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nobu_Matsuhisa\">Nobuyuki Matsuhisa (Nobu)</a> — <i>Search tools unavailable; URL based on training knowledge — highly likely correct but unverified.</i></li><li>⚠️ <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viktor_Frankl\">Viktor Frankl</a> — <i>Search tools unavailable; URL based on training knowledge — highly likely correct but unverified.</i></li><li>⚠️ <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/4069.Man_s_Search_for_Meaning\">Man&#x27;s Search for Meaning</a> — Viktor Frankl — <i>Search tools unavailable; URL based on training knowledge — highly likely correct but unverified.</i></li></ol><br>💾 <i>A concise, high-signal list of three non-obvious figures whose life stories model long-term thinking, resilience through prolonged failure, and mastery of attitude toward suffering.</i><br>🔗 <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbHFR2nJ0FI/\">Original reel</a><br><i>#resilience #role-models #long-term-thinking</i><details><summary>📄 Full transcript</summary>Short list-style reel recommending three historical figures — a statesman, a chef-entrepreneur, and a psychiatrist-philosopher — and the specific lesson each offers on leadership, resilience through failure, and response to uncontrollable circumstances.\n\nThere are three men that every guy needs to study in his 20s. Number one, Lee Kuan Yew. He&#x27;s the guy who turned Singapore into one of the richest countries in the world, and what I find really interesting about him is that he basically ran the country like a business. He focused on hiring really great people, keeping corruption low, and making decisions that were long-term instead of focusing on election cycles. I think whether you&#x27;re in business, politics, or entrepreneurship, he is a great person to learn from. Number two, Nobuyuki Matsuhisa. He&#x27;s the founder of Nobu, but he genuinely has one of the craziest life stories. He spent decades chasing the dream of becoming a sushi chef, and he moved to several different countries, watched some of his restaurants burn down, and spent decades with nothing to show for it. But this is what helped create the empire that Nobu is today. His story is just, I think there&#x27;s so much to learn from him. Number three, Viktor Frankl. He&#x27;s a Holocaust survivor and also wrote the book Man&#x27;s Search for Meaning, but what I admire most about him is his attitude towards suffering. He&#x27;s a big proponent of the fact that you have no control over your circumstances and only how you respond to them. And I think that&#x27;s something that everyone needs to internalize within their lifetime. Let me know what you guys think and follow for part two. Peace.</details>",
